 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224842 1084 #uuid "35ce56e3-74a9-5b72-a09d-7495bae6b1e9" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224843 1081 "III only" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224843 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224843 1084 #uuid "68d74c82-3706-5ba8-ba5b-2e4b259a4c36" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224844 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224844 1077 "field-1" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224844 1080 #uuid "7c812810-47ef-51e6-aa3b-d19f93af5121" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224845 1069 "e-19349" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224845 1071 "What are the solutions to the equation $2x^2 + 5x - 1 = 0$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224845 1072 #uuid "ca374954-0769-565e-92c0-0c79fbc6849b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224845 1074 "The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0,\\qquad a \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFor the given equation, we have $a=2$, $b=5$, and $c=-1$. Substituting the values for $a$, $b$, and $c$ into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (-5 ± \\sqrt{5^{2} - 4(2)(-1)})/(2(2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-5 ± \\sqrt{25 + 8}}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-5 ± \\sqrt{33}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the two solutions. Therefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-5 \\pm \\sqrt{33}}{4}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224846 1069 "e-19352" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224846 1071 "Which of the following is a solution to the equation\n$v^2 + 1 = 2v + 3$?\n\n1. $v = 1 + \\sqrt{3}$\n2. $v = 1 - \\sqrt{5}$\n3. $v = \\dfrac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}$\n4. $v = \\dfrac{1 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224846 1072 #uuid "7f4ee07e-5fef-5a43-88ac-804d359b8e60" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224846 1074 "The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$\nav^2 + bv + c = 0,\\qquad a \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we move all the terms to the left-hand side and simplify to rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\nv^2 - 2v - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nFor the last equation, we have $a=1$, $b=-2$, and $c=-2$. Substituting the values ​​for $a$, $b$, and $c$ into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-2) ± \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} - 4(1)(-2)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{4 + 8}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{12}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{12} &= \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can further simplify the solution to our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{12}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± 2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 ± \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation are\n\n$$\nv = 1 - \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nv = 1 + \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given answer options, the correct one is\n\n$$\nv = 1 + \\sqrt{3}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224847 1069 "e-19354" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224847 1071 "Given the equation\n$(v - 10)(v + 10) - 12 = -12$,\nwhat is its negative solution?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224847 1072 #uuid "c71b6b47-0d92-5000-84c4-4d1294cde49b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224847 1074 "We start by adding $12$ to both sides and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(v - 10)(v + 10) - 12 &= -12 \\\\\n(v - 10)(v + 10) - 12 + 12 &= -12 + 12 \\\\\n(v - 10)(v + 10) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the solutions to the last equation, we apply the zero-product property. This property states that if a product of factors equals zero, then at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero, we get\n\n- $v - 10 = 0$ gives $v = 10$, and\n- $v + 10 = 0$ gives $v = -10$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the negative solution to the given equation is $v = -10$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224848 1069 "e-19356" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224848 1071 "Find the solution to the equation\n$(4z - 5)(z + 6) = 0$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224848 1072 #uuid "2d9660d6-5506-54dd-a17a-f3a41408c798" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224848 1074 "To find the solutions to the given equation, we use the zero-product property. This property states that if a product of factors equals zero, then at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n4z - 5 = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad z + 6 = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed to solve each equation separately.\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4z - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n4z &= 5 \\\\\nz &= \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nz &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the given equation are\n\n$$\nz = -6, \\, \\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224849 1069 "e-19361" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224849 1071 "In the equation\n$26x^{2} + (26m + n)x + m n = 0$,\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are positive constants, the product of the solutions is equal to $k m n$, where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224849 1072 #uuid "45095b23-6b06-5a6d-8a81-1981b3378d48" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224849 1074 "The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0,\\qquad a \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFor the given equation, we have $a=26$,\n\n$$\nb=26m + n\n$$\n\nand $c=m n$.\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a}\n$$\n\ngives the value of the product of the solutions to the quadratic equation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{mn}{26} = \\dfrac{1}{26} m n\n$$\n\nComparing the last value with the given expression $k m n$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{26}} mn = {\\color{blue}k} mn\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}k} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{26}}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224850 1069 "e-19362" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224850 1071 "What is the sum of the solutions of the equation $x(x - 3) - 14 = 3x(x - 6)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224850 1072 #uuid "25f1febd-0dc8-5408-9488-df0cab38c77f" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224850 1074 "The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0,\\qquad a \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFirst, by expanding the products on both the left-hand side and the right-hand side and then simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3) - 14 &= 3x(x - 6) \\\\\nx^{2} - 3x - 14 &= 3x^{2} - 18x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the above equation as a quadratic equation in standard form, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 3x - 14 &= 3x^{2} - 18x \\\\\n- 2x^{2} + 15x - 14 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the last equation, we have $a=-2$, $b=15$, and $c=-14$.\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{b}{a}\n$$\n\ngives the value of the sum of the solutions to the quadratic equation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{b}{a} = -\\dfrac{15}{(-2)} = \\dfrac{15}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions to our original equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{2}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224851 1069 "e-19364" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224851 1071 "What is the sum of the solutions to the equation $x^2 - 12x - 4 = 0$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224851 1072 #uuid "3bf825b2-8f10-5ca1-9053-32cfd1b15538" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224851 1074 "The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0,\\qquad a \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFor the given equation, we have $a=1$, $b=-12$, and $c=-4$.\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{b}{a}\n$$\n\ngives the value of the sum of the solutions to the quadratic equation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{b}{a} &= - ((-12))/(1) = 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224852 1069 "e-19365" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224852 1071 "The solutions to $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$ are $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, while the solutions to $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$ are $t$ and $u$ with $t < u$. If the solutions to $m^2 + 14m + k = 0$, where $k$ is a constant, are $r + t$ and $s + u$, what is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224852 1072 #uuid "fbcc2525-2899-57e1-9a3a-0da892b7afb1" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224852 1074 "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 9m + 14|= 0; m^{2}+ 7m + 2m_(⏟)_(9m) + 14|= 0; m(m + 7) + 2(m + 7)|= 0; (m + 7)(m + 2)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 7 = 0 & ⇒m =-7 \\\\ m + 2 = 0 & ⇒m =-2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$r =-7ands =-2$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 5m + 4|= 0; m^{2}+ m + 4m_(⏟)_(5m) + 4|= 0; m(m + 1) + 4(m + 1)|= 0; (m + 1)(m + 4)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 1 = 0 & ⇒m =-1 \\\\ m + 4 = 0 & ⇒m =-4\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$t =-4andu =-1$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224853 1069 "e-19455" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224853 1071 "In the triangle shown, what is the value of $\\sin \\theta$?\n\n![](images/07/074a74423885312845f8b53a792970d8c22dc255039cc56c00babecb7a143c47.png)\n\n(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224853 1072 #uuid "f5dd4b44-289e-58c0-8273-600b57e17730" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224853 1074 "In a right triangle, the sine of an angle is defined as the ratio of the *opposite side* to the *hypotenuse*.\n\nIn the given triangle, the hypotenuse has length $5$, and the adjacent side to $\\theta$ has length $4$.\n\nThe side opposite $\\theta$ is unknown, so we apply the Pythagorean Theorem to solve for it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{2} &= 4^{2} + x^{2} \\\\\n25 &= 16 + x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nx &= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224854 1069 "e-19456" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224854 1071 "In the triangle shown, what is the value of $\\cos \\theta$?\n\n![](images/d6/d619df0a1a03b334f5dc69916f318a97a8d67020bf5d1e0d3fdf766a44067bc0.png)\n\n(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224854 1072 #uuid "2d804c0a-bd70-5ec3-9fd6-5460864202e6" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224854 1074 "In a right triangle, the cosine of an angle is defined as the ratio of the *adjacent side* to the *hypotenuse*.\n\nIn the given triangle, the adjacent side to $\\theta$ has length $4$.\n\nTo find the hypotenuse, $c$, we apply the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc^{2} &= 3^{2} + 4^{2} \\\\\nc^{2} &= 9 + 16 \\\\\nc^{2} &= 25 \\\\\nc &= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\nc &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224855 1069 "e-19474" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224855 1071 "The perimeter of an equilateral triangle is $432$ inches. The height of this triangle is $k\\sqrt{3}$ inches, where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224855 1072 #uuid "6c48a349-6d96-5d34-a5c1-573deae650e8" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224855 1074 "In an equilateral triangle, all three sides are equal. So if the perimeter is $432$, then each side has length\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{432}{3} = 144\n$$\n\nTo find the height of an equilateral triangle, we draw an altitude from one vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side. This altitude splits the equilateral triangle into two $30^\\circ$–$60^\\circ$–$90^\\circ$ right triangles.\n\nIn a $30^\\circ$–$60^\\circ$–$90^\\circ$ triangle:\n\n- The hypotenuse is the side of the equilateral triangle, which is $144$.\n- The shorter leg is half the base: $\\dfrac{144}{2} = 72$.\n- The height is the longer leg, which is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the shorter leg.\n\nSo, the height is\n$72\\sqrt{3}$.\n\nThus, the value of $k$ is $72$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224856 1069 "e-19475" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224856 1071 "A triangle with angle measures $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$ has a perimeter of $15 + 5\\sqrt{3}$. What is the length of the longest side of the triangle?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224856 1072 #uuid "d4ebf27d-c4af-597c-b6c7-fda0c5938056" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224856 1074 "For a $30^\\circ$–$60^\\circ$–$90^\\circ$ triangle, we have the following:\n\n- The shorter leg is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle.\n- The longer leg is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle.\n- The hypotenuse is twice the length of the shorter leg.\n- The longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the length of the shorter leg.\n\nLet the shortest side have length $x$. Then the perimeter is\n\n$$\nx + x\\sqrt{3} + 2x = 3x + x\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nWe are told that the perimeter is $15 + 5\\sqrt{3}$, so we write\n\n$$\n3x + x\\sqrt{3} = 15 + 5\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we group terms on the left and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(3 + \\sqrt{3}) &= 15 + 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{15 + 5\\sqrt{3}}{3 + \\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= (5(3 + \\sqrt{3}))/(3 + \\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the shortest side is $5$, and the longest side, the hypotenuse, is\n\n$$\n2x = 2(5) = 10\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224857 1069 "e-19478" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224857 1071 "In the figure shown, $\\tan V = \\dfrac{3}{4}$. If $VY = 45$ and $WX = 8$, what is the length of $\\overline{WZ}$?\n\n![](images/40/400abc2cc6366f588ed1d48fedb2fa3d2b77991f5fecc7cf0b6764dba77eba75.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224857 1072 #uuid "fe172a30-5f6b-5604-b7a2-fc4140f49548" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224857 1074 "To find the length of $\\overline{WZ}$, we use triangle similarity and the given trigonometric ratio to determine missing side lengths.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\tan V = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nand triangle $VXY$ is similar to triangle $VWZ$ by construction.\n\nIn triangle $VXY$, since $\\angle X$ is a right angle, then\n\n$$\n\\tan V = \\dfrac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}} = \\dfrac{XY}{VX}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\tan V = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nthe side $XY$ and the side $VX$ are in a $3:4$ ratio. So we let $XY = 3k$ and $VX = 4k$ for some positive constant $k$.\n\nWe are told that $VY = 45$. Applying the Pythagorean Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVY &= \\sqrt{(VX)^{2} + (XY)^{2}} \\\\\n45 &= \\sqrt{(4k)^{2} + (3k)^{2}} \\\\\n45 &= \\sqrt{16k^{2} + 9k^{2}} \\\\\n45 &= \\sqrt{25k^{2}} \\\\\n45 &= 5k \\\\\nk &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $XY$ and $VX{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nXY &= 3k = 3 \\cdot 9 = 27 \\\\\nVX &= 4k = 4 \\cdot 9 = 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are also told that $WX=8$, which implies that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVW &= VX - WX \\\\\n&= 36 - 8 \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since triangles $VXY$ and $VWZ$ are similar, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{WZ}{XY} &= \\frac{VW}{VX} \\\\\n\\frac{WZ}{27} &= \\frac{28}{36} \\\\\nWZ &= \\frac{27 \\cdot 28}{36} \\\\\nWZ &= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224858 1069 "e-19481" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224858 1071 "Triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $DEF$, where $A$ corresponds to $D$ and $C$ corresponds to $F$. Angles $C$ and $F$ are right angles. If $\\tan(A) = \\dfrac{5}{12}$ and $DF = 36$, what is the length of $\\overline{DE}$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224858 1072 #uuid "54ea4af0-34b5-5703-b566-1a29fc4f0af2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224858 1074 "First, let's sketch a figure (not drawn to scale).\n\n![](images/ab/ab467fd7cea207e7f7db7336649e5e0ad639e1cedb1c24fc792365fc9bfce136.png)\n\nTo find the length of $\\overline{DE}$, we use triangle similarity and the definition of tangent.\n\nSince triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $DEF$, and $A$ corresponds to $D$, we know that $\\angle A \\cong \\angle D$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan(D) = \\tan(A) = \\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$\n\nIn triangle $DEF$, $\\angle F$ is the right angle. The tangent of an acute angle is defined as\n\n$$\n\\tan(\\text{angle}) = \\dfrac{\\text{opposite side}}{\\text{adjacent side}}\n$$\n\nHere, the side opposite $\\angle D$ is $\\overline{EF}$, and the side adjacent to $\\angle D$ is $\\overline{DF}$.\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\tan(D) = \\dfrac{EF}{DF}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\tan(D) = \\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$\n\nand $DF=36$ into the last expression and solve for $EF{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (D) &= \\frac{EF}{DF} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{12} &= \\frac{EF}{36} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{12} \\cdot 36 &= EF \\\\\nEF &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the hypotenuse $\\overline{DE}$ using the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nDE &= \\sqrt{DF^{2} + EF^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36^{2} + 15^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1296 + 225} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1521} \\\\\n&= 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224859 1069 "e-19853" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224859 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $\\big(x^2-2\\sqrt{3}x+3\\big)\\big(x^2+2\\sqrt{3}x+3\\big)$?\n\n1. $(x^2-3)^2$\n2. $(x^2+3)^2$\n3. $(x-1)^2(x+3)^2$\n4. $\\big(x-\\sqrt{3}\\big)^4$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224859 1072 #uuid "287d3108-77fb-5794-96bd-9f4d8af07dc4" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224859 1074 "Notice that we have no like terms to collect, and so we begin by expanding the product and then collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} - 2\\sqrt{3}x + 3)(x^{2} + 2\\sqrt{3}x + 3) &= x^{2}(x^{2} + 2\\sqrt{3}x + 3) - 2\\sqrt{3}x(x^{2} + 2\\sqrt{3}x + 3) + 3(x^{2} + 2\\sqrt{3}x + 3) \\\\\n&= x^{4} + 2\\sqrt{3}x^{3} + 3x^{2} - 2\\sqrt{3}x^{3} - 12x^{2} - 6\\sqrt{3}x + 3x^{2} + 6\\sqrt{3}x + 9 \\\\\n&= x^{4} - 6x^{2} + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by making the substitution $y=x^2$, we have a factorable quadratic\n$y^2-6y+9$.\n\nThe \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\ny^2-{\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}y+a^2=(y-{\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor our trinomial\n$y^2-6y+9$.\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\ny^{2} - 6y + 3^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\ny^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3y + 3^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n(y - 3)^{2}\n$$\n\nSince $y=x^2$, we have\n\n$$\n(x^2-3)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is expression \"I.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224860 1069 "e-19854" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224860 1071 "Factor the expression $8t^4 - 72$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224860 1072 #uuid "87e5c54d-f8c0-5e87-ac93-cd4e2e97c2b9" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224860 1074 "When faced with simplifying expanded polynomial expressions, our strategy should consist of first checking for like terms and factorability.\n\nHere, since we have no like terms, we must consider factoring. First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{GCF}(8t^4, 72)=8\n$$\n\nand so we factor out an $8$ from both terms to obtain\n\n$$\n8t^4 - 72 = 8(t^4 - 9)\n$$\n\nNow, we observe that $t^4 - 9$ is a difference of squares:\n\n$$\nt^4 - 9 = (t^2)^2 - 3^2 = (t^2 + 3)(t^2 - 3)\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $t^2 - 3$ as another difference of squares:\n\n$$\nt^2 - 3 = (t + \\sqrt{3})(t - \\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\nSo the complete factorization is\n$8(t^2 + 3)(t + \\sqrt{3})(t - \\sqrt{3})$.\n\nSince none of these factors can be reduced further, this is our fully factored equivalent expression." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224861 1069 "e-19855" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224861 1071 "Find an equivalent factored form of the expression $121p^2 - 36$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224861 1072 #uuid "18a281e8-74a4-55c0-a5b6-aaf7cc267368" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224861 1074 "When faced with simplifying expanded polynomial expressions, our strategy should consist of first checking for like terms and factorability.\n\nHere, since we have no like terms, we must consider factoring. We have that $121 = 11^2$ and $36 = 6^2$, and so we may factor as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n121p^{2} - 36 &= 11^{2}p^{2} - 6^{2} \\\\\n&= (11p)^{2} - (6)^{2} \\\\\n&= (11p + 6)(11p - 6)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224862 1069 "e-19860" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224862 1071 "Expand and simplify the expression $(x^2 - y)(y^2 - z^3)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224862 1072 #uuid "56f51d18-7f4c-5bc8-b724-26ae70afcbca" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224862 1074 "We wish to expand the product of two binomials,\n$(x^2 - y)(y^2 - z^3)$.\n\nWe multiply each term of the first binomial by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} - y)(y^{2} - z^{3}) &= x^{2}(y^{2} - z^{3}) - y(y^{2} - z^{3}) \\\\\n&= x^{2}y^{2} - x^{2}z^{3} - y^{3} + yz^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNo further simplification is possible, so this is the final form." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224863 1069 "e-19875" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224863 1071 "The expression $(6x - 1)(5x - 2)$ is equivalent to the expression $a x^{2} + b x + c$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants. What is the value of $b$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224863 1072 #uuid "9d43c249-498b-5c8c-98eb-f0adac056011" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224863 1074 "We need to expand the expression $(6x - 1)(5x - 2)$ to put it in the form $a x^{2} + b x + c$.\n\nTo do that, we need to apply the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6x - 1)(5x - 2) &= 6x \\cdot 5x + 6x \\cdot (-2) - 1 \\cdot 5x - 1 \\cdot (-2) \\\\\n&= 30x^{2} - 12x - 5x + 2 \\\\\n&= 30x^{2} - 17x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by matching this with its given equivalent expression, $a x^{2} + b x + c$, we get\n\n$$\nb = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-17}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224864 1069 "e-19876" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224864 1071 "Consider the expression\n$2\\left(4x^2 - rx + 1\\right) + 3x(r + 2)$\nwhere $r$ is a constant. If this expression is equivalent to $8x^2 + 16x + 2$, what is the value of $r$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224864 1072 #uuid "ba79fa88-49e2-552c-a9e6-cb55dfdde429" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224864 1074 "We begin by expanding both parts of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(4x^{2} - rx + 1) &= 8x^{2} - 2rx + 2 \\\\\n3x(r + 2) &= 3rx + 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x^{2} - 2rx + 2 + 3rx + 6x &= 8x^{2} + (-2r + 3r)x + 2 \\\\\n&= 8x^{2} + (r + 6)x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the above expression equal to the given expression:\n\n$$\n8x^2 + (r + 6)x + 2 = 8x^2 + 16x + 2\n$$\n\nComparing the coefficients of $x$, we get\n\n$$\nr + 6 = 16\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + 6 &= 16 \\\\\nr &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $r$ is $10$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224865 1069 "e-19877" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224865 1071 "The expression\n\n$\\left(2900 + 120 z^{2}\\right) + 2 \\left(50 z^{2} - 1500\\right)$\n\ncan be written in the form $a z^{2} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224865 1072 #uuid "498cf102-d21f-54e5-a2d5-801b432d0ea7" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224865 1074 "We are asked to simplify\n\n$$\n2900 + 120 z^{2} + 2 \\left(50 z^{2} - 1500\\right)\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the parentheses:\n$2900 + 120 z^{2} + 100 z^{2} - 3000$\n\nNext, we combine like terms and simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2900 + 120z^{2} + 100z^{2} - 3000 &= (120z^{2} + 100z^{2}) + (2900 - 3000) \\\\\n&= 220z^{2} - 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare the above expression with $az^2+b{:}$\n\n$$\na = 220, \\qquad b = -100\n$$\n\nTherefore, their sum is\n\n$$\na + b = 220 + (-100) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{120}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224866 1069 "e-19879" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224866 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the expression $x^4 + 13x^2 + 36$?\n\n1. $(x^2 + 1)(x^2 + 12)$\n2. $(x^2 + 6)(x^2 + 3)$\n3. $(x^2 + 3)(x^2 + 12)$\n4. $(x^2 + 4)(x^2 + 9)$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224866 1072 #uuid "4dbfc663-afc3-502c-ab88-4d228f2146ca" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224866 1074 "We want to factor the expression $x^4 + 13x^2 + 36$. First, we write this as\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2\\right)^2 + 13x^2 + 36\n$$\n\nLet $u = x^2$. Then, the expression becomes\n\n$$\nu^2 + 13u + 36\n$$\n\nNow, we factor this quadratic. We look for two numbers that multiply to $36$ and add up to $13$. Since\n\n$$\n4 \\cdot 9 = 36\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n4 + 9 = 13\n$$\n\n, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} + 13u + 36 &= u^{2} + 9u + 4u + 36 \\\\\n&= (u^{2} + 9u) + (4u + 36) \\\\\n&= u(u + 9) + 4(u + 9) \\\\\n&= (u + 4)(u + 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x^2$ back in for $u$:\n\n$$\n(x^2 + 4)(x^2 + 9)\n$$\n\nSo, the factored form of $x^4 + 13x^2 + 36$ is $(x^2 + 4)(x^2 + 9)$ which is expression \"IV.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224867 1069 "e-19880" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224867 1071 "Which of the following expressions is (are) a factor of $6k^2 + 11k - 10$?\n\n$\\textrm{I.}2k + 5, \\qquad \\textrm{II.}3k - 2$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224867 1072 #uuid "9708ffea-0a98-51ae-baec-5279aa30d945" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224867 1074 "We are given a quadratic trinomial and asked to determine which of the two listed binomials are factors.\n\nTo do this, we start by factoring the quadratic expression $6k^2 + 11k - 10$.\n\nWe look for two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n(6)(-10) = -60\n$$\n\nand sum to $11$.\n\nThe numbers $15$ and $-4$ satisfy this:\n\n$$\n15 \\cdot (-4) = -60 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 15 + (-4) = 11\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the middle term $+11k$ using $15k$ and $-4k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6k^{2} + 11k - 10 &= 6k^{2} + 15k - 4k - 10 \\\\\n&= 3k(2k + 5) - 2(2k + 5) \\\\\n&= (3k - 2)(2k + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the factored form is $(3k - 2)(2k + 5)$.\n\nFrom this, we note the following:\n\n- $3k - 2$ *is* a factor\n- $2k + 5$ *is* a factor\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224868 1069 "e-19881" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224868 1071 "Which of the following is a factor of the polynomial $49x^2 - 112xy + 64y^2$?\n\n1. $5x - 8y$\n2. $7x - 8y$\n3. $5x + 8y$\n4. $7x + 8y$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224868 1072 #uuid "7acfaf84-9ebe-5446-a779-0940b9a87dd5" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224868 1074 "To find a factor of the polynomial $49x^2 - 112xy + 64y^2$, we attempt to factor it as a perfect square trinomial.\n\nA perfect square trinomial takes the form\n\n$$\n(a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- $49x^2 = (7x)^2$\n- $64y^2 = (8y)^2$\n- $-112xy = -2(7x)(8y)$\n\nSo, our expression is\n$(7x)^2 - 2(7x)(8y) + (8y)^2$.\n\nSince all three conditions match the pattern of a perfect square trinomial, we can factor the expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n49x^{2} - 112xy + 64y^{2} &= (7x)^{2} - 2(7x)(8y) + (8y)^{2} \\\\\n&= (7x - 8y)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $7x - 8y$ which is expression \"II.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224869 1069 "e-19882" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224869 1071 "Find an equivalent factored form of the expression $x^2 - 64$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224869 1072 #uuid "e34dab6d-0b7e-5d3b-a0a3-98170dc63894" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224869 1074 "Find an equivalent factored form of the expression $x^2 - 64$.\n\nThe expression $x^2 - 64$ is a difference of squares. We recognize this pattern as\n\n$$\nx^2 - a^2 = (x + a)(x - a)\n$$\n\nHere, $a^2 = 64$, so $a = 8$.\n\nUsing this identity, we factor our expression as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 64 = (x + 8)(x - 8)\n$$\n\nSo, the expression $x^2 - 64$ is equivalent to $(x + 8)(x - 8)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224870 1069 "e-19883" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224870 1071 "The expression $60x^2 + 180x$ is equivalent to $rx(3x + 9)$, where $r$ is a constant. What is the value of $r$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224870 1072 #uuid "039259c6-f7c8-5017-847d-6d1d27f87d2b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224870 1074 "We begin with the expression $60x^2 + 180x$ and compare it to the form $rx(3x + 9)$.\n\nFirst, we factor $x$ from both terms in the original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60x^{2} + 180x &= x(60x + 180)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out the greatest common factor from the binomial $60x + 180$. The GCF of $60$ and $180$ is $60$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(60x + 180) &= x(60)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= 60x(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told the expression is written as $rx(3x + 9)$. Notice that\n\n$$\n3x + 9 = 3(x + 3)\n$$\n\nso we rewrite the expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60x(x + 3) &= (3 \\cdot \\frac{60}{3})x(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{60}{3}) \\cdot x \\cdot 3(x + 3) \\\\\n&= 20x(3x + 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the value of $r$ is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{20}$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224871 1069 "e-19884" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224871 1071 "Factor out the greatest common factor and simplify the expression $48 s^{4} r^{2}+60 s^{2} r$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224871 1072 #uuid "0c97c33d-8e28-5096-b798-0e4971fd7785" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224871 1074 "First, we find the greatest common factor $(\\textrm{GCF})$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(48s^4r^2,\\ 60s^2r) = 12s^2r\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite each term as a product involving the $\\textrm{GCF}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48s^{4}r^{2} + 60s^{2}r &=  \\\\\n&= (12s^{2}r \\cdot 4s^{2}r) + (12s^{2}r \\cdot 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor out the $\\textrm{GCF}$ using the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12s^{2}r \\cdot 4s^{2}r) + (12s^{2}r \\cdot 5) &=  \\\\\n&= 12s^{2}r(4s^{2}r + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression $48 s^{4} r^{2}+60 s^{2} r$ is equivalent to $12rs^2(4rs^2 + 5)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224872 1069 "e-19885" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224872 1071 "Factor out the greatest common factor and simplify the expression $18x^5 + 27x^3 - 9x^2$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224872 1072 #uuid "a30f3d57-5f0f-5193-829e-f6c2bb2557f8" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224872 1074 "We want to factor the expression $18x^5 + 27x^3 - 9x^2$. To do this, we first identify the greatest common factor (GCF) of all the terms.\n\nEach term shares a numerical factor of $9$, and each term also contains at least two factors of $x$. So the GCF of all three terms is $9x^2$.\n\nNow, we factor out $9x^2$ from each term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18x^{5} + 27x^{3} - 9x^{2} &= 9x^{2}(2x^{3}) + 9x^{2}(3x) - 9x^{2}(1) \\\\\n&= 9x^{2}(2x^{3} + 3x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the expression $18x^5 + 27x^3 - 9x^2$ is equivalent to $9x^2(2x^3 + 3x - 1)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224873 1069 "e-19894" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224873 1071 "Consider the functions\n$f(x) = x^{2} + b x, \\qquad g(x) = 2 x^{2} - 4 x$\nwhere $b$ is a constant. If\n$f(x) \\cdot g(x) = 2 x^{4} + 8 x^{3} - 24 x^{2}$,\nwhat is the value of $b$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224873 1072 #uuid "b253d7b3-33a7-5f2d-91e3-4a3a31338cdf" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224873 1074 "We are asked to find the value of $b$ so that the product of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ matches the given quartic expression.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} + bx)(2x^{2} - 4x) &= x^{2} \\cdot 2x^{2} + x^{2} \\cdot (-4x) + bx \\cdot 2x^{2} + bx \\cdot (-4x) \\\\\n&= 2x^{4} - 4x^{3} + 2bx^{3} - 4bx^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group like terms:\n\n$$\n2 x^{4} + \\left(-4 + 2 b \\right) x^{3} + \\left(-4 b \\right) x^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the above expression with\n\n$$\n2 x^{4} + 8 x^{3} - 24 x^{2}\n$$\n\nComparing coefficients of $x^{3}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4 + 2b &= 8 \\\\\n2b &= 12 \\\\\nb &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing coefficients of $x^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4b &= -24 \\\\\nb &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn both of the cases, the values of $b$ match. Therefore, the value of $b$ is $6$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224874 1069 "e-19895" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224874 1071 "Expand and simplify the expression $(r^2 - 2r)(r^3 + 3r)$.\nWrite your final answer in standard form, with terms ordered by descending degree." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224874 1072 #uuid "554c421b-0c05-5816-8c66-63d69f64eaaf" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224874 1074 "We apply the distributive property to multiply each term in the first binomial by each term in the second.\n\nFirst, we multiply $r^2$ by both terms in the second binomial:\n\n$$\nr^2(r^3 + 3r) = r^5 + 3r^3\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply $2r$ by both terms in the second binomial:\n\n$$\n-2r(r^3 + 3r) = -2r^4 - 6r^2\n$$\n\nNow, we combine all the terms:\n\n$$\nr^5 + 3r^3 - 2r^4 - 6r^2\n$$\n\nWe arrange the terms in descending order of degree to write the expression in standard form.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $r^5 - 2r^4 + 3r^3 - 6r^2$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224875 1069 "e-19906" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224875 1071 "The expression $2y^2 + ay$ is equivalent to $y(2y - 9)$ for some constant $a$. What is the value of $a$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224875 1072 #uuid "b25d71e8-71df-5b21-b34f-50f160652b30" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224875 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n2y^2 + ay = y(2y - 9)\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the right-hand side:\n\n$$\ny(2y - 9) = 2y^2 - 9y\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the two expressions to determine the value of $a$, as follows:\n\n$$\n2{\\color{blue}y^2}-9{\\color{red}y} = 2{\\color{blue}y^2}+a{\\color{red}y} \\[5pt]\n$$\n\nThis gives us\n\n$$\na = -9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $-9$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224876 1069 "e-19907" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224876 1071 "Find a factor of the expression $15x^2 - 30x + 45$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224876 1072 #uuid "96184c55-a037-5132-9ad0-61bd12b29250" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224876 1074 "To determine a factor of $15x^2 - 30x + 45$, we look for a common factor in all three terms.\n\nThe coefficients are $15$, $-30$, and $45$, which all share a common factor of $15$. So, we factor out $15$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n15x^2 - 30x + 45 = 15(x^2 - 2x + 3)\n$$\n\nThis means that $15$ is a factor of the polynomial.\n\nSo, the answer is $15$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224877 1069 "e-19909" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224877 1071 "Factor and simplify the expression $-3(q^5 + q^3) - 4(q^5 + q^3)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224877 1072 #uuid "9fccad23-ede8-57b5-8601-1833bb933db0" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224877 1074 "Notice that we are adding two multiples of the same binomial, $q^5 + q^3$:\n\n$$\n-3(q^5 + q^3) - 4(q^5 + q^3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor out the common binomial:\n\n$$\n(-3 - 4)(q^5 + q^3) = -7(q^5 + q^3)\n$$\n\nNow, we distribute to simplify the expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n-7q^5 - 7q^3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $-7q^5 - 7q^3$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224878 1069 "e-19960" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224878 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{b - 2ub}{2u^2 - 2ub} - \\dfrac{1 + b}{u - b}$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{-2ub - 1}{2u^3 - 4u^2b - 2ub^2}$\n2. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub - 2u^2}{2u^3 - 4u^2b - 2ub^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - ub - 2u^2}{2u^2 - 2ub}$\n4. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub}{2u^2 - 2ub}$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224878 1072 #uuid "02b3f173-512d-5304-81e4-cfd0a1fde0e2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224878 1074 "First, notice that we have factorable terms in our expression.\n\n- For $b - 2ub$ we have $\\operatorname{GCF}(b, 2ub) = b$, and so $b - 2ub = b(1 - 2u)$.\n- For $2u^2 - 2ub$ we have $\\operatorname{GCF}(2u^2, 2ub) = 2u$, and so $2u^2 - 2ub = 2u(u - b)$.\n\nNext, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b(1 - 2u)}{2u(u - b)} - \\dfrac{1 + b}{u - b}\n$$\n\nNow, our common denominator is $2u(u - b)$. Multiply the second term by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2u}{2u}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(b(1 - 2u))/(2u(u - b)) - (2u(1 + b))/(2u(u - b)) &= (b(1 - 2u) - 2u(1 + b))/(2u(u - b)) \\\\\n&= (b - 2ub - 2u - 2ub)/(2u(u - b)) \\\\\n&= (b - 2u - 4ub)/(2u(u - b))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, noticing that all our answer choices have an expanded denominator, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub}{2u(u - b)} = \\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub}{2u^2 - 2ub}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is expression \"IV.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224879 1069 "e-19961" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224879 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{w}{w - 8} + \\dfrac{w}{6(8 - w)}$, where $w\\ne8$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{4w}{6(w - 8)}$\n2. $-\\dfrac{5w}{6(w - 8)}$\n3. $\\dfrac{5w}{6(w - 8)}$\n4. $\\dfrac{2w}{3(w - 8)}$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224879 1072 #uuid "cb9bd620-577b-5cc2-8504-cd78fe5a8c5e" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224879 1074 "Notice that we may factor out a $-1$ from $8 - w$ to obtain\n\n$$\n8 - w = -(w - 8)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we rewrite the expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{w}{w - 8} + \\dfrac{w}{6(8 - w)} = \\dfrac{w}{w - 8} - \\dfrac{w}{6(w - 8)}\n$$\n\nThe least common denominator is $6(w - 8)$, so we multiply the numerator and denominator of the first fraction by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{w}{w - 8} - (w)/(6(w - 8)) &= \\frac{6}{6} \\cdot \\frac{w}{w - 8} - (w)/(6(w - 8)) \\\\\n&= (6w)/(6(w - 8)) - (w)/(6(w - 8)) \\\\\n&= (6w - w)/(6(w - 8)) \\\\\n&= (5w)/(6(w - 8))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5w}{6(w - 8)}\n$$\n\nwhich is expression \"III.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224880 1069 "e-19965" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224880 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{r^2(2r - 3) - 36(2r - 3)}{r + 6}$, where $r\\neq -6$?\n\n1. $(2r - 3)(r + 6)$\n2. $(2r - 3)(r - 6)$\n3. $(2r - 3)(r^2 - 36)$\n4. $2r^3 - 3r^2$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224880 1072 #uuid "0d3a4595-b401-5e13-b9c0-0fa8f6ede251" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224880 1074 "To simplify this quotient, we first factor the numerator. We have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{GCF}\\left(r^2(2r - 3),36(2r - 3)\\right)=2r - 3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r^2(2r - 3) - 36(2r - 3)}{r + 6} = \\dfrac{(2r - 3)(r^2 - 36)}{r + 6}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that we have a difference of squares in our numerator. Factoring this difference of squares, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((2r - 3)(r^{2} - 36))/(r + 6) &= ((2r - 3)(r^{2} - 6^{2}))/(r + 6) \\\\\n&= ((2r - 3)(r - 6)(r + 6))/(r + 6)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nr\\neq -6\n$$\n\nwe may simplify this to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((2r - 3)(r - 6)(r + 6))/(r + 6) &= ((2r - 3)(r + 6)(r - 6))/(r + 6) \\\\\n&= (2r - 3)(r - 6)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is expression \"II.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224881 1069 "e-19966" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224881 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{u^2-11}{u+\\sqrt{11}}$, where $u\\neq-\\sqrt{11}$?\n\n1. $u+\\sqrt{11}$\n2. $u+11$\n3. $u-\\sqrt{11}$\n4. $\\dfrac{u}{\\sqrt{11}}$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224881 1072 #uuid "130fe840-1f5c-5285-829c-eb2fe25ca5ea" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224881 1074 "Observe that our numerator $u^2-11$ may be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\nu^2-11=u^2-\\big(\\sqrt{11}\\big)^2\n$$\n\nHence, applying the formula for factoring the difference of squares, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} - (\\sqrt{11})^{2} &= (u - \\sqrt{11})(u + \\sqrt{11})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we may simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{u^{2} - 11}{u + \\sqrt{11}} &= ((u - \\sqrt{11})(u + \\sqrt{11}))/(u + \\sqrt{11}) \\\\\n&= ((u - \\sqrt{11})(u + \\sqrt{11}))/(u + \\sqrt{11}) \\\\\n&= u - \\sqrt{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is expression \"III.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224882 1069 "e-19970" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224882 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{7}{8x}-\\dfrac{3}{4x}$, where $x \\neq 0$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{1}{8x}$\n2. $\\dfrac{10}{8x}$\n3. $\\dfrac{4}{8x}$\n4. $-\\dfrac{1}{4x}$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224882 1072 #uuid "7673624b-e93d-5d4a-bcd2-b3c8889f4b10" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224882 1074 "Here, we wish to subtract\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8x}-\\dfrac{3}{4x}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we must first obtain a common denominator. As\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{LCM}(8x,4x)=8x\n$$\n\nour common denominator is $8x$. So, we multiply the top and bottom of our second fraction by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8x} - \\frac{3}{4x} &= \\frac{7}{8x} - \\frac{2}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{8x} - \\frac{6}{8x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7 - 6}{8x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is expression \"I.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224883 1069 "e-19978" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224883 1071 "The equation\n$\\dfrac{w^2 + 2w - 15}{w - 3} = bw + c$\nis true for all $w \\neq 3$, where $b$ and $c$ are integers. What is the value of $b + c$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224883 1072 #uuid "f630ba9c-96c4-5f1d-b761-cb452408a702" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224883 1074 "We are given a rational expression that is equivalent to a linear expression for all\n\n$$\nw \\neq 3\n$$\n\nTo determine $b$ and $c$, we divide the numerator by the denominator\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{w^2 + 2w - 15}{w - 3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we look to factor the numerator\n$w^2 + 2w - 15$.\nWe search for two numbers whose product is $-15$ and whose sum is $2$\n\n$$\n5 \\times (-3) = -15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 5 + (-3) = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can factor the expression in the numerator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw^{2} + 2w - 15 &= w^{2} + (5 - 3)w - 15 \\\\\n&= w^{2} + 5w - 3w - 15 \\\\\n&= w(w + 5) - 3(w + 5) \\\\\n&= (w + 5)(w - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the factored form back into the original expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{w^2 + 2w - 15}{w - 3} = \\dfrac{(w + 5)(w - 3)}{w - 3}\n$$\n\nFor all\n\n$$\nw \\neq 3\n$$\n\n, we can cancel the common factor $(w - 3)$ to get\n\n$$\nw + 5 = bw + c\n$$\n\nComparing coefficients, we have $b = 1$ and $c = 5$. Therefore, the value of $b + c$ is $6$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224884 1069 "e-19979" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224884 1071 "Consider the expression\n$\\dfrac{k^{2} - 81}{k - 9}$,\nIf the expression is equivalent to $k + a$, where $k \\ne 9$ and $a$ is a constant, what is the value of $a$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224884 1072 #uuid "3a7e5013-994b-5018-93d0-d05daea17f69" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224884 1074 "We are told that the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k^{2} - 81}{k - 9}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $k + a$ for some constant $a$ and\n\n$$\nk \\ne 9\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{k^{2} - 81}{k - 9} &= ((k - 9)(k + 9))/(k - 9) \\\\\n&= ((k - 9)(k + 9))/(k - 9) \\\\\n&= k + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nk \\ne 9\n$$\n\nwe are allowed to cancel the common factor $(k - 9)$.\n\nTherefore, by matching the final expression $k + 9$ with its given equivalent expression $k + a$, we conclude that $a = 9$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224885 1069 "e-19984" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224885 1071 "$\\sqrt{m - t} = 9 - 2t$\n\nIn the equation given above, $m$ is a constant. The equation has exactly one real solution. What is the minimum possible value of $16m$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224885 1072 #uuid "8bd2cdbd-89ba-5db2-b6bc-d7029ba6fdbb" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224885 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{m - t} = 9 - 2t\n$$\n\nTo solve for $t$, we first square both sides to remove the radical:\n\n$$\nm - t = (9 - 2t)^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, expanding the square, bringing all terms to one side, and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm - t &= 81 - 36t + 4t^{2} \\\\\n0 &= 4t^{2} - 35t + 81 - m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for this equation to have one real solution, the quadratic discriminant\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}=b^{2} - 4ac\n$$\n\nmust equal zero.\n\nHere, $a = 4$, $b = -35$, and $c = 81 - m$. So, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= 0 \\\\\n(-35)^{2} - 4(4)(81 - m) &= 0 \\\\\n1225 - 1296 + 16m &= 0 \\\\\n- 71 + 16m &= 0 \\\\\n16m &= 71 \\\\\nm &= \\frac{71}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's find the potential solution corresponding to\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{71}{16}\n$$\n\nSince it corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}=0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= -\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\\n&= - ((-35))/(2(4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{35}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check that it is a valid solution by substituting it back into our original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{m - t} &= ^(?)9 - 2t \\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{71}{16} - t} &= ^(?)9 - 2t \\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{71}{16} - \\frac{35}{8}} &= ^(?)9 - 2 \\cdot \\frac{35}{8} \\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{71}{16} - \\frac{35}{8}} &= ^(?)9 - \\frac{35}{4} \\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{71}{16} - \\frac{70}{16}} &= ^(?)\\frac{36}{4} - \\frac{35}{4} \\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}} &= ^(?)\\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} &= \\frac{1}{4}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{71}{16}\n$$\n\nis indeed a minimum possible value when the equation has exactly one real solution.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n16m = 16 \\cdot \\frac{71}{16} = 71\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224886 1069 "e-19985" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224886 1071 "Consider the equation\n$\\dfrac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\dfrac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} + 4$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. What are the solutions to this equation?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224886 1072 #uuid "6787b93e-22ad-5ded-ae96-82475b624c6a" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224886 1074 "All terms have the same square root in the denominator. So, multiplying through by $\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}} &= (\\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} + 4) \\cdot \\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}} \\\\\n\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}} &= \\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}} + 4 \\cdot \\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}} \\\\\nr^{2} &= k^{2} + 4\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $k^2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\nr^2 - k^2 = 4 \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nu = \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nu^2= r^2 - k^2\n$$\n\ninto the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= 4u \\\\\nu^{2} - 4u &= 0 \\\\\nu(u - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible values of $u$ are\n\n$$\nu = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad u = 4\n$$\n\nNow, we find the corresponding potential solutions for $r$ and verify if they are valid solutions.\n\n- If $u=0$, then we obtain\n$\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}|= 0; r^{2}|= k^{2}; r|= ± k$.\nHowever, plugging $r = k$ or $r = -k$ into the original equation makes the denominator zero. Hence, $u=0$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm k$, is *not* a valid solution.\n- If $u=4$, then we obtain\n$\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}|= 4; r^{2} - k^{2}|= 16; r^{2}|= k^{2} + 16; r|= ± \\sqrt{k^{2} + 16}$.\nPlugging this into the original equation, we get\n$\\frac{k^{2} + 16}{\\sqrt{k^{2} + 16 - k^{2}}}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{k^{2} + 16 - k^{2}}} + 4; \\frac{k^{2} + 16}{\\sqrt{16}}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{16}} + 4; \\frac{k^{2} + 16}{4}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4; \\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4.✓$\nHence, $u=4$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm \\sqrt{k^2 + 16}$, *is* a valid solution." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224887 1069 "e-19986" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224887 1071 "What are the solutions to $\\sqrt{3x}+1-\\sqrt{x-2}=\\sqrt{x-2}$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224887 1072 #uuid "c4c6c7c4-0ca4-5042-8b66-0652801f8d04" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224887 1074 "To solve for $x$, we first combine like terms and square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 - \\sqrt{x - 2} &= \\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 2\\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n(\\sqrt{3x} + 1)^{2} &= (2\\sqrt{x - 2})^{2} \\\\\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting equation still contains a radical term. To solve, we isolate this term and square both sides again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4x - 8 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{3x} &= x - 9 \\\\\n(2\\sqrt{3x})^{2} &= (x - 9)^{2} \\\\\n12x &= x^{2} - 18x + 81 \\\\\n0 &= x^{2} - 30x + 81 \\\\\nx^{2} - 30x + 81 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 27)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=27$ and $x=3$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=27$, we have\n$\\sqrt{3 \\cdot 27} + 1 - \\sqrt{27 - 2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{27 - 2}; \\sqrt{81} + 1 - \\sqrt{25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{25}; 9 + 1 - 5|= ^(?)5; 5|= 5.✓$\nHence, $x=27$ is a valid solution.\n- For $x=3$, we have\n$\\sqrt{3 \\cdot 3} + 1 - \\sqrt{3 - 2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{3 - 2}; \\sqrt{9} + 1 - \\sqrt{1}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{1}; 3 + 1 - 1|= ^(?)1; 3 \\mid \\ne 1.\\cdot$\nHence, $x=3$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3x}+1-\\sqrt{x-2}=\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nis $x=27$ only." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224888 1069 "e-19989" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224888 1071 "$\\sqrt{4q + 28} = \\sqrt{(q - 1)^{2}}$\n\nFind the smallest real solution of the equation given above." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224888 1072 #uuid "ab625ad3-c77d-53c6-84d2-72ab25bd39f2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224888 1074 "Squaring both sides of the given equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{4q + 28})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{(q - 1)^{2}})^{2} \\\\\n4q + 28 &= (q - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, expanding the square, moving all terms to one side, and factoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4q + 28 &= q^{2} - 2q + 1 \\\\\n0 &= q^{2} - 6q - 27 \\\\\n0 &= (q + 3)(q - 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two possible solutions: $q=-3$ or $q=9$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions, by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $q=-3$, we have\n$\\sqrt{4(-3) + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{((-3) - 1)^{2}}; \\sqrt{-12 + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}}; \\sqrt{16}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{16}.✓$\nHence, $q=-3$ *is* a valid solution.\n- For $q=9$, we have\n$\\sqrt{4(9) + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(9 - 1)^{2}}; \\sqrt{36 + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{8^{2}}; \\sqrt{64}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{64}.✓$\nHence, $q=9$ *is* a valid solution.\n\nSo, the valid solutions are $q=-3$ and $q=9$.\nTherefore, the smallest real solution is $q=-3$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224889 1069 "e-19991" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224889 1071 "What value of $n$ satisfies the equation given below?\n$\\dfrac{n^{2}}{n^{2} + 5n + 6} - \\dfrac{2n}{n + 2} = -\\,\\dfrac{9}{n + 3}$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224889 1072 #uuid "f633f2bd-de31-5489-a817-faadf6f0e61e" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224889 1074 "First, we factor the denominators where possible. Note that $n^{2} + 5n + 6$ factors as $(n + 2)(n + 3)$.\n\nSo, we rewrite the expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n^{2}}{(n + 2)(n + 3)} - \\dfrac{2n}{n + 2} = -\\,\\dfrac{9}{n + 3}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(n+2,\\: n+3,\\: (n+2)(n+3) \\big) = (n+2)(n+3)$.\n- The values $n=-2$ and $n=-3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((n^{2})/((n + 2)(n + 3)) - \\frac{2n}{n + 2}) \\cdot (n + 2)(n + 3) &= -\\frac{9}{n + 3} \\cdot (n + 2)(n + 3) \\\\\n(n^{2})/((n + 2)(n + 3)) \\cdot (n + 2)(n + 3) - \\frac{2n}{n + 2} \\cdot (n + 2)(n + 3) &= -\\frac{9}{n + 3} \\cdot (n + 2)(n + 3) \\\\\n(n^{2})/((n + 2)(n + 3)) \\cdot (n + 2)(n + 3) - \\frac{2n}{n + 2} \\cdot (n + 2)(n + 3) &= -\\frac{9}{n + 3} \\cdot (n + 2)(n + 3) \\\\\nn^{2} - 2n(n + 3) &= -9(n + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand both sides, move all terms to the left-hand side, and factor the result to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^{2} - 2n^{2} - 6n &= -9n - 18 \\\\\n- n^{2} + 3n + 18 &= 0 \\\\\nn^{2} - 3n - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n(n - 6)(n + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible solutions are $n = 6$ or $n = -3$.\n\nHowever, $n = -3$ is excluded because it makes the denominator zero. Therefore, the only valid solution is $n = 6$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224890 1069 "e-19992" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224890 1071 "$\\dfrac{1}{y^{2} + 12y + 36} = 9$.\nIf $y$ is a solution to the given equation, which of the following is a possible value of $y + 6$?\n\n1. $-\\dfrac 2 3$\n2. $\\dfrac 2 3$\n3. $\\dfrac 1 3$\n4. $\\dfrac 5 3$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224890 1072 #uuid "99cce2d1-ed81-52e7-ba93-5e4c78486ff5" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224890 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y^{2} + 12y + 36} = 9\n$$\n\nFirst, we observe that the denominator is a perfect square trinomial:\n\n$$\ny^{2} + 12y + 36 = (y + 6)^2\n$$\n\nSo, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(y + 6)^2} = 9\n$$\n\nNow, we take the reciprocal of both sides:\n\n$$\n(y + 6)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the square root of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(y + 6)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\\ny + 6 &= ± \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the possible values of $y + 6$ are\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nAmong the listed options, only\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis valid." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224891 1069 "e-19996" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224891 1071 "Given that $-8\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{2}v} = -12$, determine the value of $10v$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224891 1072 #uuid "25faeee2-5f48-5a44-a894-6ee45f420105" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224891 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n-8\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{2}v} = -12\n$$\n\nand asked to find the value of $10v$.\n\nFirst, we isolate the square root term by dividing both sides by $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{2}v} = \\frac{-12}{-8} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we square both sides to remove the radical:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{2}v}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}v = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nand multiplying both sides by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10v &= 10 \\cdot \\frac{9}{10} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $9$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224892 1069 "e-19997" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224892 1071 "Given the equation\n$\\sqrt{4w + 25} + 2 = w + 3$,\ndetermine its solution set." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224892 1072 #uuid "2b7c2d3e-4ee4-53ff-82bc-cc8a855aa05b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224892 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{4w + 25} + 2 &= w + 3 \\\\\n\\sqrt{4w + 25} &= w + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the square root must be non-negative, so\n\n$$\nw + 1 \\ge 0\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nw \\ge -1\n$$\n\nFirst, we square both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{4w + 25})^{2} &= (w + 1)^{2} \\\\\n4w + 25 &= (w + 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand the square:\n\n$$\n4w + 25 = w^{2} + 2w + 1\n$$\n\nNext, we bring all terms to one side and simplify:\n\n$$\n0 = w^{2} - 2w - 24\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the resulting quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(w - 6)(w + 4) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the possible solutions are $w = 6$ or $w = -4$.\n\nHowever, $w = -4$ does not satisfy\n\n$$\nw \\ge -1\n$$\n\nso it must be rejected.\n\nTherefore, only $w = 6$ remains. We check this value in the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{4(6) + 25} + 2 &= ^(?)6 + 3 \\\\\n\\sqrt{49} + 2 &= ^(?)9 \\\\\n9 &= 9✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $w = 6$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution set is $\\{6\\}$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224893 1069 "e-19999" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224893 1071 "What is the solution to the equation given below?\n$\\dfrac{(a - 4)(a + 2)}{a + 2} = 5$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224893 1072 #uuid "41325c10-7633-5271-8f7b-616358f48bf6" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224893 1074 "First, note that the denominator $a + 2$ must not be zero:\n\n$$\na + 2 \\neq 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\neq -2\n$$\n\nAssuming that\n\n$$\na \\neq -2\n$$\n\nwe simplify the rational expression by cancelling the common factor and solve for $a$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((a - 4)(a + 2))/(a + 2) &= 5 \\\\\n((a - 4)(a + 2))/(a + 2) &= 5 \\\\\na - 4 &= 5 \\\\\na &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224894 1069 "e-20001" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224894 1071 "Given that $\\sqrt{x - 7} = 6$, determine the value of $x$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224894 1072 #uuid "73aa86e6-3f47-54cf-9ea0-4c30deb3c99d" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224894 1074 "To solve the given equation, we first square both sides to remove the radical. Then, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x - 7} &= 6 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{x - 7})^{2} &= 6^{2} \\\\\nx - 7 &= 36 \\\\\nx &= 43\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging $x = 43$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{43 - 7} &= ^(?)6 \\\\\n\\sqrt{36} &= ^(?)6 \\\\\n6 &= 6✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $x = 43$ is a valid solution." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224895 1069 "e-20026" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224895 1071 "The following equation relates the positive variables $x$, $y$, $z$, and $w{:}$\n$\\dfrac{24}{x} = \\dfrac{24}{y} + \\dfrac{24}{z} - \\dfrac{24}{w}$\n\nExpress $y$ in terms of $x$, $z$, and $w$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224895 1072 #uuid "5c0cf98e-5af5-588f-8254-8584f18bf4a4" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224895 1074 "Question (MC, H)\n\nStem:\n\nExplanation:\n\nWe are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{24}{x} = \\dfrac{24}{y} + \\dfrac{24}{z} - \\dfrac{24}{w}\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor out the common factor of $24$ on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{24}{x} = 24\\left(\\dfrac{1}{y} + \\dfrac{1}{z} - \\dfrac{1}{w}\\right)\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $24$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x} = \\dfrac{1}{y} + \\dfrac{1}{z} - \\dfrac{1}{w}\n$$\n\nTo isolate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y}\n$$\n\nwe subtract\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{z}\n$$\n\nand add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{w}\n$$\n\nfrom both sides:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y} = \\dfrac{1}{x} - \\dfrac{1}{z} + \\dfrac{1}{w}\n$$\n\nWe now combine the terms on the right-hand side using a common denominator of $xzw$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{y} &= \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{z} + \\frac{1}{w} \\\\\n&= \\frac{zw}{zw} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{xw}{xw} \\cdot \\frac{1}{z} + \\frac{xz}{xz} \\cdot \\frac{1}{w} \\\\\n&= \\frac{zw - xw + xz}{xzw}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the reciprocal of both sides gives the desired relation:\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{xzw}{zw - xw + xz}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224896 1069 "e-20027" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224896 1071 "The variables $p$ and $q$ are related by the equation\n$p = \\dfrac{7q - 4}{q}$.\nExpress $q$ in terms of $p$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224896 1072 #uuid "b75229b2-0e48-5641-9558-58020c257f7b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224896 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equation by $q$ gives\n\n$$\npq = 7q - 4\n$$\n\nSubtracting $7q$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\npq - 7q = -4\n$$\n\nFactoring out $q$ on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\nq(p - 7) = -4\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $p - 7$ yields\n\n$$\nq = \\dfrac{-4}{p - 7}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nq = \\dfrac{-4}{p - 7}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224897 1069 "e-20030" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224897 1071 "The formula\n$E = D + \\dfrac{4}{7}(95 - P)$\ncan be used to estimate the energy usage $E$ (in kilowatt-hours) of a cooling tower, where $D$ is the baseline draw (in kilowatt-hours) and $P$ is the pressure differential (in psi), provided $P < 95$. Express $P$ in terms of $E$ and $D$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224897 1072 #uuid "b6ca1db1-ac22-5e4f-b6ea-b6eb2d632097" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224897 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nE = D + \\dfrac{4}{7}(95 - P)\n$$\n\nTo isolate $P$, we first subtract $D$ from both sides:\n\n$$\nE - D = \\dfrac{4}{7}(95 - P)\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nto eliminate the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4}(E - D) = 95 - P\n$$\n\nNow, subtract $95$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4}(E - D)-95 =-P\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying both sides by $-1$ gives\n\n$$\n95 - \\dfrac{7}{4}(E - D) = P\n$$\n\nRewriting with $P$ on the left-hand side, we get the correct answer:\n\n$$\nP = 95 - \\dfrac{7}{4}(E - D)\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224898 1069 "e-20031" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224898 1071 "A spaceship moves through deep space, and its average speed $u$ (in kilometers per minute) during a time interval of $6$ minutes is given by the equation\n$u = \\dfrac{z - 30}{6}$,\nwhere $z$ is the ship's displacement in kilometers. If the equation is rewritten in the form\n$z = p u + q$,\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $p$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224898 1072 #uuid "c72a4e86-2cb0-53a1-9cce-8ca316d83094" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224898 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{z - 30}{6}\n$$\n\nand asked to rewrite it in the form\n\n$$\nz = p u + q\n$$\n\nin order to determine the value of $p$.\n\nFirst, we multiply *both sides* of the initial equation by $6$ to eliminate the fraction:\n$6u = z - 30$\n\nThen, we add *$30$ to both sides* to solve for $z{:}$\n\n$$\nz = 6u + 30\n$$\n\nFinally, we identify the coefficient of $u$. The equation is now in the form\n\n$$\nz = \\underbrace{6}_{p} \\cdot u + \\underbrace{30}_{q}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $p$ is $6$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224899 1069 "e-20033" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224899 1071 "The following equation relates the positive numbers $n$, $f$, and $t{:}$\n$\\dfrac{1}{9n} = \\dfrac{2f}{t}$.\nExpress $f$ in terms of $t$ and $n$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224899 1072 #uuid "91a48695-d826-54fe-8537-8bda604a5476" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224899 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9n} = \\dfrac{2f}{t}\n$$\n\nand asked to solve for $f$ in terms of $t$ and $n$.\n\nTo eliminate the denominators, we use *cross multiplication*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 \\cdot t &= 2f \\cdot 9n \\\\\nt &= 18fn\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow divide *both sides* by $18n$ to isolate $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{t}{18n} &= \\frac{18fn}{18n} \\\\\n\\frac{t}{18n} &= f\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting with $f$ on the left-hand side, we get the correct answer:\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{t}{18n}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224900 1069 "e-20035" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224900 1071 "The following equation relates the numbers $g$ and $w$, where $w\\neq 0$ and $g>-12{:}$\n$g = -12 + \\dfrac{48}{w}$.\nExpress $w$ in terms of $g$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224900 1072 #uuid "dc5032f2-480f-52d1-a39c-c7f85ff829c6" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224900 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\ng = -12 + \\dfrac{48}{w}\n$$\n\nand asked to solve for $w$ in terms of $g$.\n\nFirst, we add $12$ to *both sides*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng + 12 &= -12 + \\frac{48}{w} + 12 \\\\\ng + 12 &= \\frac{48}{w}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we multiply *both sides* of the equation by $w$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(g + 12) \\cdot w &= \\frac{48}{w} \\cdot w \\\\\nw(g + 12) &= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide *both sides* by $g + 12$ to isolate $w{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(w(g + 12))/(g + 12) &= \\frac{48}{g + 12} \\\\\nw &= \\frac{48}{g + 12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{48}{g + 12}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224901 1069 "e-20037" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224901 1071 "The following equation relates the numbers $q, r$, and $s{:}$\n$12q = 3(r + s)$\nExpress $r$ in terms of $q$ and $s$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224901 1072 #uuid "9ee9f2d4-b64a-5629-905f-7195e058cb4c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224901 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n12q = 3(r + s)\n$$\n\nand asked to solve for $r$ in terms of $q$ and $s$.\n\nFirst, we divide *both sides* of the equation by $3$ to isolate the expression in parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{12q}{3} &= (3(r + s))/(3) \\\\\n4q &= r + s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we subtract $s$ from *both sides* to isolate $r{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4q - s &= r + s - s \\\\\n4q - s &= r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting with $r$ on the left-hand side, we get the correct answer:\n$r = 4q - s$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224902 1069 "e-20045" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224902 1071 "The cost $C$ of a certain taxi ride, in dollars, can be modeled by the formula $C = 3.6 + 2.4m$, where $m$ is the distance in miles. Express $m$ in terms of $C$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224902 1072 #uuid "7b870e9f-e726-5138-b00f-d1f7503b7c6b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224902 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nC = 3.6 + 2.4m\n$$\n\nand asked to solve for $m$ in terms of $C$.\n\nFirst, we subtract *$3.6$ from both sides*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC - 3.6 &= 3.6 + 2.4m - 3.6 \\\\\nC - 3.6 &= 2.4m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we divide *both sides* of the equation by $2.4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{C - 3.6}{2.4} &= \\frac{2.4m}{2.4} \\\\\n\\frac{C - 3.6}{2.4} &= m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting with $m$ on the left-hand side, we get the correct answer:\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{C - 3.6}{2.4}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224903 1069 "e-20054" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224903 1071 "${y - m = 4x - 2; y + m = x^{2} + 2x$\n\nIn the given system of equations, $m$ is a constant. The system has exactly one distinct real solution. What is the value of $m$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224903 1072 #uuid "2033bf51-c2a0-5f40-8a78-b509d7bbe027" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224903 1074 "We first rewrite the system by isolating both equations for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n{y = 4x - 2 + m; y = x^{2} + 2x - m\n$$\n\nSince both equations are now solved for $y$, we set them equal and put in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2 + m &= x^{2} + 2x - m \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + (2 - 2m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe may now compute the discriminant with the parameters $a=1$, $b=-2$, and $c=2 - 2m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-2)^{2} - 4(1)(2 - 2m) \\\\\n&= 4 - 8 + 8m \\\\\n&= 8m - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we need a single solution, we require the discriminant to be $0$. We obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= 8m - 4 \\\\\n8m &= 4 \\\\\nm &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of $m$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224904 1069 "e-20060" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224904 1071 "${y = x^{2} - 5x + 3; x - y = 2$\n\nIf $(x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $(x_{2}, y_{2})$ are the two solutions to the system of equations above, what is the value of $y_{1} + y_{2}$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224904 1072 #uuid "bb323e3d-2398-5de3-9c2d-ea9073e5de82" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224904 1074 "We are given a system of equations and asked to find $y_1 + y_2$, the sum of the $y$-coordinates of the two points where the graphs intersect.\n\nFirst, we substitute the expression for $y$ into the second equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 2 \\\\\nx - (x^{2} - 5x + 3) &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 3 &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $5$ and add to $-6$. These are $-1$ and $-5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x - x_(⏟)_(-6x) + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 5)(x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ using\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 5x + 3{:}\n$$\n\n- If $x_1 = 5$, then\n$y_1 = 5^2 - 5(5) + 3 = 25 - 25 + 3 = 3$\n- If $x_2 = 1$, then\n$y_2 = 1^2 - 5(1)+3=1 - 5 + 3 = -1$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\ny_1 + y_2 = 3 + (-1) = 2\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224905 1069 "e-20064" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224905 1071 "${x - y = 10; x + y = x^{2} - 10$\n\nWhich ordered pair is a solution to the system of equations above?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224905 1072 #uuid "554a6d3e-ba42-5335-8b1d-6ed86aa249fc" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224905 1074 "To solve the system, we first use the linear equation to express one variable in terms of the other. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\ny = x - 10\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this into the second equation and solve:\n\nHence, applying the zero product rule yields\n\n$$\nx = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ using\n\n$$\ny = x - 10{:}\n$$\n\n- If $x = 0$, then\n$y = 0 - 10 = -10$.\n- If $x = 2$, then\n$y = 2 - 10 = -8$.\n\nSo the two solutions to the system are $(0, -10)$ and $(2, -8)$.\n\nAmong the given answer choices, we have solution $(2, -8)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224906 1069 "e-20065" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224906 1071 "${u^{2} =-11u + v; v = 11u + 121$\n\nA solution to the given system of equations is $(u, v)$. Which of the following is a possible value of $uv$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224906 1072 #uuid "5ea623fa-d951-50bc-9621-939435c454bd" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224906 1074 "We are given a system of equations involving a quadratic and a linear equation. To solve, we substitute the second equation into the first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= -11u + v \\\\\nu^{2} &= -11u + (11u + 121) \\\\\nu^{2} &= 121\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides to solve for $u{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{u^{2}} &= \\sqrt{121} \\\\\n\\mid u \\mid &= 11 \\\\\nu &= ± 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute each $u$-value into the second equation to find the corresponding $v$-values:\n\n- If $u = 11$, then\n$v|= 11u + 121; = 11(11) + 121; = 242$.\n- If $u = -11$, then\n$v|= 11(-11) + 121; =-121 + 121; = 0$.\n\nFinally, we compute $uv$ in each case:\n\n- If $u = 11,\\ v = 242$, then\n$uv = 11 \\cdot 242 = 2662$.\n- If $u = -11,\\ v = 0$, then\n$uv = -11 \\cdot 0 = 0$.\n\nAmong the given answer choices, the only possible value of $uv$ is $0$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224907 1069 "e-20066" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224907 1071 "${y = (x + 3)(x - 6); y = 9x - 54$\n\nWhat is the ordered pair $(x, y)$ that satisfies the given system of equations?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224907 1072 #uuid "3068b8c8-6af7-57ba-aee8-6a6da0abbe9b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224907 1074 "First, we write both equations of the given system in factored form:\n\n$$\n{y = (x + 3)(x - 6); y = 9(x - 6)\n$$\n\nNotice that $x-6$ is a common factor in both equations. To find the solution, we set the two expressions for $y$ equal to each other and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)(x - 6) &= 9(x - 6) \\\\\n(x + 3)(x - 6) - 9(x - 6) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 6)[(x + 3) - 9] &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 6)(x - 6) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 6)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule then yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 6 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x = 6$ into either equation to find the corresponding $y$-value. Using\n\n$$\ny = 9(x - 6)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 9(x - 6) \\\\\n&= 9(6 - 6) \\\\\n&= 9(0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $(6, 0)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224908 1069 "e-20067" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224908 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, what is the $y$-coordinate of the point of intersection of the graphs of $y = (x - 12)^2$ and $y = 24x - 432$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224908 1072 #uuid "f0a2d6bc-9a27-5e6f-94ea-b32edd597ad9" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224908 1074 "To find the coordinates of the point of intersection, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = (x - 12)^{2}; y = 24x - 432\n$$\n\nSince both equations are solved for $y$, we set them equal and put in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 12)^{2} &= 24x - 432 \\\\\nx^{2} - 24x + 144 &= 24x - 432 \\\\\nx^{2} - 48x + 576 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 48x + 576 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot 24 \\cdot x + 24^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 24)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule yields $x=24$, and so we substitute $x = 24$ into either equation to find the corresponding $y$-value. We use\n\n$$\ny = 24x - 432{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 24x - 432 \\\\\n&= 24(24) - 432 \\\\\n&= 576 - 432 \\\\\n&= 144\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $144$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224909 1069 "e-20070" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224909 1071 "${y = 3x - 5; y =-2x^{2} + 3x + 3$\n\nIf $(x,y)$ is a solution to the system of equations given above, what is the smallest possible value of $x$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224909 1072 #uuid "f502be84-fbd5-5e7d-b500-2f1cb977184c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224909 1074 "First, notice that we may factor the system of equations as follows:\n\n$$\n{y = 3x - 5; y =-2x^{2} + 3x + 3\n$$\n\nSince both equations are solved for $y$, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 5 &= -2x^{2} + 3x + 3 \\\\\n0 &= -2x^{2} + 8 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx=-2\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=2\n$$\n\nSince $-2<2$, the smallest possible value of $x$ is $-2$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224910 1069 "e-20085" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224910 1071 "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = \\mid x - 4x \\mid$. What values of $a$ satisfy $f(5) - f(a) = -15$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224910 1072 #uuid "3a4cc4d0-a85c-5608-a33d-a435b04295a8" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224910 1074 "First, we evaluate $f(5){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(5) &= \\mid 5 - 4(5) \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 5 - 20 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 15 \\mid \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $f(5)$ into the equation\n\n$$\nf(5) - f(a) = -15\n$$\n\nand solve it for $f(a)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(5) - f(a) &= -15 \\\\\n15 - f(a) &= -15 \\\\\n- f(a) &= -30 \\\\\nf(a) &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substitute\n\n$$\nf(a)= \\mid a - 4a \\mid\n$$\n\ninto the above and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(a) &= 30 \\\\\n\\mid a - 4a \\mid &= 30 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-3a & &= 30 \\\\ 3 & a\\end{vmatrix}|= 30 \\\\\n\\mid a \\mid &= 10 \\\\\na &= ± 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a$ could be $10$ or $-10$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224911 1069 "e-20086" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224911 1071 "Given the function $f(x) = 4x^3$, find the value of $x$ for which $f(x) = 108$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224911 1072 #uuid "85d3e367-ee46-58e9-b39e-659c8585df83" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224911 1074 "We set $f(x)$ equal to $108$ and solve the obtained equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 108 \\\\\n4x^{3} &= 108 \\\\\nx^{3} &= 27 \\\\\nx^{3} &= 3^{3} \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required value of $x$ is $3$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224912 1069 "e-20089" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224912 1071 "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 5(3)^x - 7$. What is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = g(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224912 1072 #uuid "387d6d90-dfbf-5a0f-9957-0ab991df489f" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224912 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept of the graph of a function, we evaluate the function at $x = 0$.\n\nSubstituting $x = 0$ into the equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= 5(3)^{x} - 7 \\\\\ng(0) &= 5(3)^{0} - 7 \\\\\n&= 5(1) - 7 \\\\\n&= 5 - 7 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is the point $(0, -2)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224913 1069 "e-20098" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224913 1071 "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 5(3^{x})$. What is the value of $f(2)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224913 1072 #uuid "8466e5fc-8ff2-5ec8-9207-c22b81c9947a" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224913 1074 "To find the value of $f(2)$, we substitute $x=2$ into the function's definition and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 5(3^{x}) \\\\\nf(2) &= 5(3^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(2)$ is $45$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224914 1069 "e-20101" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224914 1071 "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = \\dfrac{x^{2} - 4x + 2}{x - 2}$. What is the value of $g(-2)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224914 1072 #uuid "6d40af24-9096-590e-a1dd-fca1c7344820" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224914 1074 "Explanation:\n\nTo find the value of $g(-2)$, we substitute $x=-2$ into the function's definition and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} - 4x + 2}{x - 2} \\\\\ng(-2) &= ((-2)^{2} - 4(-2) + 2)/((-2) - 2) \\\\\n&= (4 - (-8) + 2)/((-4)) \\\\\n&= (14)/((-4)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $g(-2)$ is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224915 1069 "e-20102" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224915 1071 "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = \\mid x - 4 \\mid$. What is the value of $f(-2)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224915 1072 #uuid "e98427ee-d090-5907-88e0-bd943421e754" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224915 1074 "To find the value of $f(-2)$, we substitute $x=-2$ into the function's definition and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\mid x - 4 \\mid \\\\\nf(-2) &= \\mid - 2 - 4 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 6 \\mid \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(-2)$ is $6$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224916 1069 "e-20103" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224916 1071 "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 2x^{3} - 4$. What is the value of $g(-2)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224916 1072 #uuid "75da0226-dd85-5d4b-9e41-bd4ef50e9ae4" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224916 1074 "To find $g(-2)$, we substitute $x = -2$ into the function's definition and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= 2x^{3} - 4 \\\\\ng(-2) &= 2(-2)^{3} - 4 \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-8) - 4 \\\\\n&=-16 - 4 \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224917 1069 "e-20107" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224917 1071 "| Minute | Online players |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $7.5 \\cdot 10^{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $1.5 \\cdot 10^{4}$ |\n| $3$ | $3.0 \\cdot 10^{4}$ |\n\nAn online game’s player count is increasing at an exponential rate, as shown in the table above. At this rate, during which minute will the number of players reach $1.536 \\cdot 10^{7}$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224917 1072 #uuid "5bb0eb64-be67-5ce0-aa11-3a7879a68623" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224917 1074 "We are told that the number of players is growing exponentially. The formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot b^t\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can note the following:\n\n- At Minute $1$, there are $7.5 \\cdot 10^3$ players.\n- At Minute $2$, there are $1.5 \\cdot 10^4$ players.\n- At Minute $3$, there are $3.0 \\cdot 10^4$ players.\n\nSince the count doubles each minute (\n\n$$\n15000 \\div 7500 = 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n30000 \\div 15000 = 2\n$$\n\n), the growth factor is $b = 2$.\n\nBecause the count doubles per minute and the first data point is at Minute $1$, the initial count, $a$, at Minute $0$ was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= (7.5 \\cdot 10^{3}) \\div 2 \\\\\n&= 3.75 \\cdot 10^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = (3.75 \\cdot 10^3) \\cdot 2^t\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the value of $t$ such that\n\n$$\ny(t) = 1.536 \\cdot 10^{7}\n$$\n\nthat is,\n\n$$\n(3.75 \\cdot 10^3) \\cdot 2^t = 1.536 \\cdot 10^{7}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3.75 \\cdot 10^{3}) \\cdot 2^{t} &= 1.536 \\cdot 10^{7} \\\\\n2^{t} &= \\frac{1.536 \\cdot 10^{7}}{3.75 \\cdot 10^{3}} \\\\\n2^{t} &= 4.096 \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n2^{t} &= 4096 \\\\\n2^{t} &= 2^{12} \\\\\nt &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of players will reach $1.536 \\times 10^{7}$ during the $12$-th minute." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224918 1069 "e-20109" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224918 1071 "A university’s endowment currently has a value of $\\[math]200,000$, and the endowment value is expected to increase by $6\\%$ each year. Which of the following equations can be used to estimate the number of years, $t$, it will take for the endowment value to reach $\\[/math]300,000$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224918 1072 #uuid "710f4f8a-f325-5aea-8041-847c22b2f5ae" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224918 1074 "We're told that a university’s endowment currently has a value of $\\[math]200,000$, and the endowment value is expected to increase by $6\\%$ each year.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nE(t) = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n- $E$ is the endowment value.\n- $a = 200,000$ dollars is the current value.\n- $r = 0.06$ is the growth rate ($6\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t$ is the time, in years.\n\nThen, the expression for $E(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE(t) &= 200, 000 \\cdot (1 + 0.06)^{t} \\\\\n&= 200, 000 \\cdot (1.06)^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we want to determine how many years it will take for the endowment value to reach $\\[/math]300,000$. To represent this, we set up the equation\n\n$$\n300,000 = 200,000 \\cdot (1.06)^t\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224919 1069 "e-20110" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224919 1071 "For the function $f$, we have $f(0) = 400$, and for each increase in $x$ by $3$, the value of $f(x)$ decreases by $75\\%$. What is the value of $f(6)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224919 1072 #uuid "e42d104d-53f7-51f1-a0f1-298140c24b89" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224919 1074 "We are told that\n\n$$\nf(0) = 400\n$$\n\nand that $f(x)$ decreases by $75\\%$ for each increase of $3$ in $x$. This means that each time $x$ increases by $3$, the value of $f(x)$ becomes $25\\%$ of the previous value.\n\nTherefore, the function follows exponential decay with a factor of $0.25$ ($25\\%$ expressed as a decimal) per $3$ units. The general form is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 400 \\cdot (0.25)^{x/3}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $f(6){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(6) &= 400 \\cdot (0.25)^{6/3} \\\\\n&= 400 \\cdot (0.25)^{2} \\\\\n&= 400 \\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224920 1069 "e-20114" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224920 1071 "A model estimates that at the end of each year from $2012$ to $2017$, the number of trees in a forest was $50\\%$ more than the number of trees in the forest at the end of the previous year. The model estimates that at the end of $2013$, there were $300$ trees in the forest.\n\nWrite the equation that represents this model, where $f(t)$ is the estimated number of trees in the forest $t$ years after the end of $2012$ and $t \\leq 5$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224920 1072 #uuid "c751ff97-65b4-5d8e-a6e2-eeae91bd64e4" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224920 1074 "We're told that from $2012$ to $2017$, the number of trees in a forest increased by $50\\%$ each year compared to the previous year. We also know that at the end of $2013$, there were $300$ trees in the forest.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nf(t) = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n- $r = 0.5$ is the growth rate ($50\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after the end of $2012$.\n\nThen, the expression for $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t) &= a \\cdot (1 + 0.5)^{t} \\\\\n&= a \\cdot (1.5)^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt only remains for us to determine the value of $a$.\n\nWe know that at the end of $2013$ there were $300$ trees. The end of $2013$ is $1$ year after the end of $2012$. So, when $t=1$, we have $f = 300$. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 300 \\\\\na \\cdot (1.5)^{1} &= 300 \\\\\na \\cdot 1.5 &= 300 \\\\\na &= \\frac{300}{1.5} \\\\\na &= 200\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(t) = 200 \\cdot (1.5)^t\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224921 1069 "e-20117" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224921 1071 "Two variables, $h$ and $j$, are related such that for each increase of $1$ in the value of $h$, the value of $j$ is multiplied by $\\dfrac{3}{2}$. When $h = 0$, $j = 64$. Which equation represents this relationship?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224921 1072 #uuid "4e1ac044-ddf7-5fac-bc49-fe3a6ac10928" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224921 1074 "We are told that the value of $j$ is multiplied by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nfor each increase of $1$ in $h$. That means the variable $j$ grows exponentially with base\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h$ and $j$ are related by the expression\n\n$$\nj = a \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^h\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $j = 64$ when $h = 0$. Hence, we can determine the value of $a$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nj &= a \\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})^{h} \\\\\n64 &= a \\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})^{0} \\\\\n64 &= a \\cdot 1 \\\\\na &= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nj = 64 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^h\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224922 1069 "e-20127" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224922 1071 "The function $p$ is defined by the equation $p(x) = 150 \\cdot 10^x$. If $q(x) = p(x - 2)$,find the equation that defines the function $q$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224922 1072 #uuid "fc11bbd9-0017-561e-a6c4-af731a322f89" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224922 1074 "We are told that\n\n$$\np(x) = 150 \\cdot 10^x\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nq(x) = p(x - 2)\n$$\n\nTo find the equation for $q(x)$, we substitute $x-2$ into the definition of $p(x)$ wherever $x$ appears. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nq(x) = p(x-2) = 150 \\cdot 10^{x-2}\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(x) &= 150 \\cdot 10^{x - 2} \\\\\n&= 150 \\cdot 10^{x} \\cdot 10^{-2} \\\\\n&= 150 \\cdot 10^{-2} \\cdot 10^{x} \\\\\n&= 150 \\cdot \\frac{1}{100} \\cdot 10^{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{150}{100} \\cdot 10^{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 10^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that defines $q$ is\n\n$$\nq(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2} \\cdot 10^x\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224923 1069 "e-20128" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224923 1071 "The function $h$ is defined by the equation $h(x) = 5 \\cdot 2^x$. If $k(x) = h(x + 3)$, find the equation that defines the function $k$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224923 1072 #uuid "41fec1f1-d336-5e64-9634-7005487649bd" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224923 1074 "We are told that\n\n$$\nh(x) = 5 \\cdot 2^x\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nk(x) = h(x + 3)\n$$\n\nTo find the equation for $k(x)$, we substitute $x+3$ into the definition of $h(x)$ wherever $x$ appears. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nk(x) = h(x + 3) = 5 \\cdot 2^{x + 3}\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk(x) &= 5 \\cdot 2^{x + 3} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 2^{x} \\cdot 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 2^{3} \\cdot 2^{x} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 2^{x} \\\\\n&= 40 \\cdot 2^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that defines $k$ is\n\n$$\nk(x) = 40 \\cdot 2^x\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224924 1069 "e-20129" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224924 1071 "A machine was purchased in $2015$ for $\\[math]32{,}000$. Its value halves every $6$ years. In which year will the value reach $\\[/math]2{,}000$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224924 1072 #uuid "ec39c33f-7c10-526a-a7ef-6a09826afb2e" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224924 1074 "We're told that a machine was purchased in $2015$ for $\\[math]32{,}000$, and its value halves every $6$ years. We want to find the year when the value will reach $\\[/math]2{,}000$.\n\nThe formula for exponential decay, in terms of a halving period $d$, is\n\n$$\ny = a \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/d}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=32{,}000$ is the initial value in $2015$.\n- The value halves every $6$ years. So, the halving period is $d=6$.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after $2015$.\n\nTherefore, the expression for the value $y$ after $t$ years is\n\n$$\ny = 32{,}000 \\cdot \\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $t$ when\n\n$$\ny = 2{,}000\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 2{,}000$ in the last equation and simplifying, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2,000 &= 32,000 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{t/6} \\\\\n\\frac{2,000}{32,000} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{t/6} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{16} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{t/6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{16} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= \\frac{t}{6} \\\\\nt &= 4 \\cdot 6 \\\\\nt &= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $24$ years for the value to reach $\\[math]2{,}000$.\n\nFinally, to find the year, we add this to the initial year $2015{:}$\n\n$$\n2015 + 24 = 2039\n$$\n\nSo, the value will reach $\\[/math]2{,}000$ in the year ${2039}$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224925 1069 "e-20131" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224925 1071 "The function $f(t) = 120,000 \\cdot 2^{t/6}$ gives the number of followers on a social media page $t$ months after it was initially measured. How much time, in months, does it take for the number of followers to double?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224925 1072 #uuid "18c76f57-d4a3-52be-99c1-d25048620763" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224925 1074 "We are told that the number of followers is modeled by the function\n\n$$\nf(t) = 120,000 \\cdot 2^{t/6}\n$$\n\nThe exponent $t/6$ means that the number of followers is multiplied by $2$ when $t = 6$.\n\nTherefore, the number of followers doubles every $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{6}$ months." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224926 1069 "e-20132" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224926 1071 "A lab technician observes a population of $3,000$ mold spores in a sample. After $6$ hours, the number of mold spores has grown to $24,000$. Assuming exponential growth, the formula $N = A \\cdot 2^{rt}$ models the number of mold spores, where $A$ and $r$ are constants and $N$ is the number of spores $t$ hours after the initial observation. What is the value of $r$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224926 1072 #uuid "6673f565-7919-56e0-a87d-7ff75cb110c3" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224926 1074 "We are told that the number of mold spores follows the equation\n\n$$\nN = A \\cdot 2^{rt}\n$$\n\nand that $3,000$ mold spores grow to $24,000$ after $6$ hours.\n\nIf we substitute these values into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n24,000 = 3,000 \\cdot 2^{r \\cdot 6}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $3,000$, we obtain\n\n$$\n8 = 2^{6 \\cdot r}\n$$\n\nNotice that $8=2^3$, so we can rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n2^3 = 2^{6 \\cdot r}\n$$\n\nSince the bases are equal, the exponents must be equal. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 &= 6r \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224927 1069 "e-20134" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224927 1071 "An exponential function $D$ is defined by $D(x) = 400 \\cdot b^x$, where $b$ is a positive constant. If $D(2) = 100$, what is the value of $D(5)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224927 1072 #uuid "6ac4a2b8-d1f6-5e95-890e-6af40e659151" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224927 1074 "We are told that\n\n$$\nD(x) = 400 \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nand that $D(2) = 100$.\n\nSubstituting $x = 2$ into the function $D$ and solve for $b$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(2) &= 400 \\cdot b^{2} \\\\\n100 &= 400 \\cdot b^{2} \\\\\nb^{2} &= \\frac{100}{400} \\\\\nb^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nb &= ± \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $b$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nb = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the function to calculate $D(5){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(5) &= 400 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{5} \\\\\n&= 400 \\cdot \\frac{1}{32} \\\\\n&= 12.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224928 1069 "e-20137" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224928 1071 "An employee’s annual salary is $\\[math]52,000$ when they start a new job. The salary is modeled by an exponential function $F$, where $F(t)$ represents the salary, in dollars, $t$ years after starting. After $7$ years, the salary grows to $\\[/math]68,428.45$.\n\nRounding the base of the exponential function to two decimal places, write the equation that could define $F$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224928 1072 #uuid "8c0d3c75-338c-5d00-8d02-94ddd40b8dc3" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224928 1074 "We are told that the salary starts at $\\[math]52,000$ and grows to $\\[/math]68,428.45$ after $7$ years.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\nF(t) = a b^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=52,000$ is the initial salary.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after the job is started.\n- After $t=7$ years, the salary is $F(7) = 68,428.45$.\n\nNow, we proceed to substitute the previous values in the formula for $F(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(7) &= 52, 000 \\cdot b^{7} \\\\\n68, 428.45 &= 52, 000 \\cdot b^{7} \\\\\n52, 000 \\cdot b^{7} &= 68, 428.45 \\\\\nb^{7} &= \\frac{68, 428.45}{52, 000} \\\\\nb^{7} &= 1.31593…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $b$, we take the $7$-th root of both sides. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= \\sqrt[7]{1.31593…} \\\\\n&= 1.04000… \\\\\n&\\approx 1.04\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that could define $F$ is\n\n$$\nF(t) = 52,000 \\cdot (1.04)^t\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224929 1069 "e-20139" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224929 1071 "A wildlife preserve had $1,200$ deer in $2020$. Starting in $2021$, the population is projected to decline by $5\\%$ each year. Write the function that models this relationship, where $P(t)$ is the number of deer in the preserve $t$ years after $2020$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224929 1072 #uuid "c565e194-3f85-543c-9806-b6e142edee7d" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224929 1074 "We're told that the wildlife preserve had $1,200$ deer in $2020$, and starting in $2021$, the population is projected to decline by $5\\%$ each year.\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nP(t) = a(1-r)^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=1,200$ is the initial population of deer.\n- $r = 0.05$ is the decay rate ($5\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after $2020$.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $P(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(t) &= 1, 200 \\cdot (1 - 0.05)^{t} \\\\\n&= 1, 200 \\cdot (0.95)^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function models the relationship where $P(t)$ is the number of deer in the preserve $t$ years after $2020$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224930 1069 "e-20143" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224930 1071 "A small streaming platform was launched in March $2020$ with $850$ users. Each month for the next $18$ months, the number of users increased by $12\\%$ compared to the previous month.\n\nWrite the equation that represents the total number of users $u(t)$ on the platform $t$ months after March $2020$, where $t \\leq 18$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224930 1072 #uuid "8ebf6584-cd66-51f6-8724-c5df79832027" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224930 1074 "We are told that the number of users starts at $850$ and increases by $12\\%$ each month for the next $18$ months. This means we have exponential growth, where the quantity increases by the same percentage every month.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nu(t) = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=850$ is the initial number of users.\n- $r = 0.12$ is the growth rate ($12\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t$ is the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $u(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= 850 \\cdot (1 + 0.12)^{t} \\\\\n&= 850 \\cdot 1.12^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224931 1069 "e-20144" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224931 1071 "A certain bacteria culture starts with $5$ cells. Each hour, the number of cells triples. If $N$ represents the number of cells after $t$ hours, write the expression that gives $N$ in terms of $t$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224931 1072 #uuid "faba10cd-1a4c-53df-a245-db767fa12cd5" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224931 1074 "We're told that the initial number of cells is $5$. Each hour, the number of cells triples.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor) is\n\n$$\nN = a \\cdot b^t\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of cells.\n- $b = 3$ is the growth factor (the population triples each hour).\n- $t$ is the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for the number of cells $N$ after $t$ hours is\n\n$$\nN = 5 \\cdot 3^t\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224932 1069 "e-20147" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224932 1071 "A lighthouse installs a beacon on top of a tower that stands $50$ feet high. Due to salty sea winds, the beacon’s maximum height decreases slightly each day. After each day, the maximum height of the tip of the beacon is $70\\%$ of the height it reached the day before.\n\nLet $h(n)$ represent the estimated maximum height (in feet) of the beacon's tip after $n$ days, where $n$ is a whole number greater than $1$ and less than $10$. Which equation correctly defines $h(n)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224932 1072 #uuid "0c2dc34d-da38-528e-aa8b-cbbdcd6bc158" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224932 1074 "The height of the tip of the beacon starts at $50$ feet and reduces to $70\\%$ of its previous height each day. This means we are dealing with exponential decay.\n\nThe general formula for exponential decay using a decay factor $b$ is\n\n$$\nh(n) = a \\cdot b^n\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a = 50$ is the initial height.\n- $b = 0.70$ is the decay factor (since $70\\% = 0.70$).\n- $n$ is the number of days.\n\nSubstituting these into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nh(n) = 50 \\cdot (0.70)^n\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224933 1069 "e-20148" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224933 1071 "A colony of bacteria is placed in a petri dish. Initially, there are $800$ bacteria. The number of bacteria is expected to double every $6$ hours.\n\nFind the equation that represents the number of bacteria $N(t)$, $t$ hours after the bacteria were first placed in the dish." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224933 1072 #uuid "16be1aca-e235-5e6b-bfd2-563e7a26e13f" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224933 1074 "We're told that a colony of bacteria starts with an initial count of $800$ bacteria, and the number of bacteria is expected to double every $6$ hours.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth, in terms of a doubling period $p$, is\n\n$$\nN(t) = a \\cdot 2^{t/p}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=800$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n- The number of bacteria doubles every $6$ hours. Hence, the doubling period is $p=6$.\n- $t$ is the time, in hours, after the bacteria were first placed in the dish.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for the number of bacteria $N(t)$ is\n\n$$\nN(t) = 800 \\cdot 2^{t/6}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224934 1069 "e-20151" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224934 1071 "A soccer ball is kicked straight upward. It reaches a maximum height of $48$ feet at $2$ seconds and hits the ground at $4$ seconds. Which equation represents the height above ground $H$, in feet, of the soccer ball $t$ seconds after it is kicked?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224934 1072 #uuid "72f44e87-2f16-5dc2-ae00-f5b86b8cb683" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224934 1074 "We can model the motion of the soccer ball with a downward-opening quadratic function whose vertex (maximum point) is $(h, k)$, in vertex form:\n\n$$\nH(t) = a(t - h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nFirst, we know the ball reached its maximum height of $48$ feet after $2$ seconds. This means the vertex of the quadratic function is at $(2, 48)$, so\n\n$$\nh = 2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k = 48\n$$\n\nNext, we know the ball returned to the ground level at $t = 4$, so $H(4) = 0$. So we set up the equation and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH(4) &= a(4 - 2)^{2} + 48 \\\\\n0 &= a(2)^{2} + 48 \\\\\n0 &= 4a + 48 \\\\\n- 4a &= 48 \\\\\na &= -\\frac{48}{4} \\\\\na &= -12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the height equation is\n\n$$\nH(t) = -12(t - 2)^2 + 48\n$$\n\nTherefore, rewriting it in standard form, the equation that represents the height of the soccer ball above the ground $t$ seconds after it is kicked is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH(t) &= -12(t - 2)^{2} + 48 \\\\\n&=-12(t^{2} - 4t + 4) + 48 \\\\\n&=-12t^{2} + 48t - 48 + 48 \\\\\n&=-12t^{2} + 48t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224935 1069 "e-20155" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224935 1071 "The total distance $s$, in yards, glided by a skier can be modeled by a quadratic function defined in terms of $t$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. At a time of $5.0$ seconds, the skier has traveled $125.0$ yards, and at a time of $10.0$ seconds, the skier has traveled $450.0$ yards. If the skier started at a distance of $0$ yards when $t = 0$, then what is the total distance traveled, in yards, by the skier after $15.0$ seconds?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224935 1072 #uuid "ec73f503-88d8-5cdf-a0b4-c67ebe9c9be3" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224935 1074 "We are told that the distance $s$, in yards, traveled by a skier over time $t$ seconds, follows a quadratic form\n\n$$\ns(t) = at^2 + bt + c\n$$\n\nSince the skier starts at $s = 0$ when $t = 0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= a(0)^{2} + b(0) + c \\\\\nc &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the function simplifies to\n\n$$\ns(t) = at^2 + bt\n$$\n\nFirst, given that $s=125.0$ at $t=5.0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n125.0 &= a(5.0)^{2} + b(5.0) \\\\\n125 &= 25a + 5b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, given that $s=450.0$ at $t=10.0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n450.0 &= a(10.0)^{2} + b(10.0) \\\\\n450 &= 100a + 10b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a system:\n\n$$\n{25a + 5b = 125; 100a + 10b = 450\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we first multiply the second equation by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nthen subtract the resulting equation from the first to eliminate $a$ and solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(25a + 5b) - (50a + 5b) &= 125 - 225 \\\\\n25a + 5b - 50a - 5b &= -100 \\\\\n- 25a &= -100 \\\\\na &= \\frac{-100}{-25} \\\\\na &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging $a = 4$ back into the first equation and solving for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25(4) + 5b &= 125 \\\\\n100 + 5b &= 125 \\\\\n5b &= 25 \\\\\nb &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the distance function becomes\n\n$$\ns(t) = 4t^2 + 5t\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting $t = 15.0$, the distance traveled by the skier after $15.0$ seconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(15.0) &= 4(15.0)^{2} + 5(15.0) \\\\\n&= 4(225) + 75 \\\\\n&= 900 + 75 \\\\\n&= 975yards\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224936 1069 "e-20156" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224936 1071 "A skier launches off a ramp into the air. Her height, in meters above the ground, is modeled by a quadratic function of the time, in seconds, since she left the ramp. According to the model, she launched from a height of $8$ meters above the ground and reached her highest point of $17$ meters after $3$ seconds. After how many seconds does the model predict she will land? (Round to the nearest whole number)" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224936 1072 #uuid "a346db28-db5a-5087-943c-30a81bb19dc6" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224936 1074 "We can model the height of the skier above the ground with a downward-opening quadratic function whose vertex (maximum point) is $(h, k)$, in vertex form:\n\n$$\nh(t) = a(t - h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nFirst, we know the skier reached her maximum height of $17$ meters after $3$ seconds. This means the vertex of the quadratic function is at $(3, 17)$, so\n\n$$\nh = 3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k = 17\n$$\n\nNext, we know the skier launched from a height of $8$ meters at $t = 0$, which means $h(0) = 8$. So we set up the equation and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(0) &= a(0 - 3)^{2} + 17 \\\\\n8 &= a(3)^{2} + 17 \\\\\n8 &= 9a + 17 \\\\\n- 9a &= 17 - 8 \\\\\n- 9a &= 9 \\\\\na &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the height function is\n\n$$\nh(t) = -1(t - 3)^2 + 17\n$$\n\nNow, to determine when the skier lands (zero height), we set up the equation $h(t) = 0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(t) &= -1(t - 3)^{2} + 17 \\\\\n0 &= -1(t - 3)^{2} + 17 \\\\\n(t - 3)^{2} &= 17 \\\\\nt - 3 &= ± \\sqrt{17} \\\\\nt &= 3 ± \\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating, we find that the roots are\n\n$$\nt\\approx -1.123, \\qquad t\\approx 7.1234\n$$\n\nSince $t > 0$, we discard the negative option. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nt\\approx 7.1234\n$$\n\nand rounding this to the nearest second gives $t = 7$ seconds." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224937 1069 "e-20157" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224937 1071 "The quadratic function $D$ models the depth, in meters, below the surface of the water of a research vessel’s tethered camera $t$ minutes after it was lowered. The function estimates that the camera reached its maximum depth of $225$ meters $4$ minutes after it was lowered and then reached the surface of the water $10$ minutes after it was lowered. Based on the function, what was the estimated depth, to the nearest meter, of the camera $6$ minutes after it was lowered?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224937 1072 #uuid "7429f7fe-9273-5b6a-bb34-27b8f20cd0fc" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224937 1074 "We model the depth of the camera below the water’s surface with a quadratic function $D(t)$, where $t$ is the number of minutes since it was lowered and $D(t)$ gives the depth in meters. Since the camera reaches a maximum depth, the function takes the vertex form:\n\n$$\nD(t) = a(t - h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h, k)$ is the vertex of the parabola.\n\nFirst, we know the camera reached its maximum depth of $225$ meters after $4$ minutes. This means the vertex of the quadratic function is at $(4, 225)$, so\n\n$$\nh = 4 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k = 225\n$$\n\nNext, we know the camera reached the surface of the water ($0$ depth) at $t = 10$, which means $D(10) = 0$. So we set up the equation and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(10) &= a(10 - 4)^{2} + 225 \\\\\n0 &= a(6)^{2} + 225 \\\\\n0 &= 36a + 225 \\\\\n- 36a &= 225 \\\\\na &= -\\frac{225}{36} \\\\\na &= -\\frac{25}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the depth function is\n\n$$\nD(t) = -\\frac{25}{4}(t - 4)^2 + 225\n$$\n\nTherefore, the depth at $t=6$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(6) &= -\\frac{25}{4}(6 - 4)^{2} + 225 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{25}{4}(2)^{2} + 225 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{25}{4}(4) + 225 \\\\\n&=-25 + 225 \\\\\n&= 200meters\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224938 1069 "e-20166" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224938 1071 "The quadratic function $s$ is defined by\n$s(x) = -4(x - 6)^2 + 100$.\nIn the $xy$-plane, the graph of $y = s(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $(1, 0)$ and $(n, 0)$, where $n$ is a constant. What is the value of $n$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224938 1072 #uuid "19d95ca4-8274-52d5-b591-dcd5031f791a" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224938 1074 "Since the function intersects the $x$-axis at $(n, 0)$, we substitute it into the given function and solve for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(x) &= -4(x - 6)^{2} + 100 \\\\\n0 &= -4(n - 6)^{2} + 100 \\\\\n4(n - 6)^{2} &= 100 \\\\\n(n - 6)^{2} &= \\frac{100}{4} \\\\\n(n - 6)^{2} &= 25 \\\\\nn - 6 &= ± 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, the negative root gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn - 6 &= -5 \\\\\nn &= -5 + 6 \\\\\nn &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the graph intersects the $x$-axis at the point $(1, 0)$, which is already given.\n\nTherefore, the positive root gives the $x$-coordinate of the other point, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn - 6 &= 5 \\\\\nn &= 6 + 5 \\\\\nn &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224939 1069 "e-20168" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224939 1071 "For the function $g(x)=3x^2+bx+3$, if $g(2)$ is a minimum, what is $b$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224939 1072 #uuid "3eabf0df-c276-5de3-b126-a198e2f51f3d" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224939 1074 "The $x$-coordinate of the minimum point is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nFor the function $g$, we have $a = 3$. Also, since $g(2)$ is the minimum, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= -\\frac{b}{2 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n2 &= -\\frac{b}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $b = -12$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224940 1069 "e-20169" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224940 1071 "The function $t$ is defined by the equation\n$t(x) = (-x - 5)(x + 3)$.\nThe graph of $t$ in the $xy$-plane is a parabola. Which of the following intervals contains the $x$-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of $t$?\n\n1. $-5 < x < -3$\n2. $-2 < x < 0$\n3. $2 < x < 3$\n4. $3 < x < 4$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224940 1072 #uuid "2f2cad95-1617-554e-a6aa-6a4becf730af" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224940 1074 "The given function is a quadratic in factored form. Since the coefficients of $x$ multiply to give a negative $x^2$ term, the parabola opens downward.\n\nThe vertex is exactly midway between the roots. To find the roots, we set $t(x) = 0$ and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt(x) &= (-x - 5)(x + 3) \\\\\n0 &= (-x - 5)(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the roots are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x_2 = -3\n$$\n\nSo the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is the midpoint of $x_1$ and $x_2$, giving\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= (-5 + (-3))/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-8}{2} \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct interval that contains this vertex is\n$-5 < x < -3$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224941 1069 "e-20172" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224941 1071 "The function $n$ is defined by\n$n(x) = -(x - 2)(x + 8)$.\nFor what value of $x$ does $n(x)$ reach its maximum?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224941 1072 #uuid "bc9c9dad-63a4-52c1-9291-2766066c9234" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224941 1074 "The given function is a quadratic in factored form. Expanding the expression results in a negative $x^2$ term, the parabola opens downward and reaches a maximum at its vertex.\n\nThe vertex (maximum) is exactly midway between the roots. To find the roots, we set $n(x) = 0$ and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn(x) &= - (x - 2)(x + 8) \\\\\n0 &= - (x - 2)(x + 8)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the roots are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x_2 = -8\n$$\n\nSo the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is the midpoint of $x_1$ and $x_2$, giving\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= (2 + (-8))/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-6}{2} \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n(x)$ reaches its maximum at $x = -3$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224942 1069 "e-20178" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224942 1071 "The equation $y = -x^{2} + 10x - 9$ relates the variables $x$ and $y$. For what value of $x$ does the value of $y$ reach its maximum?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224942 1072 #uuid "18fa5493-ec9e-5a07-8997-3d0d880ef916" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224942 1074 "The given equation is a quadratic function of the form:\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwhere $a = -1$, $b = 10$, and $c = -9$.\n\nTherefore, since $a < 0$, the parabola opens downward and has a maximum at its vertex, whose $x$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\\n&= - (10)/(2(-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-10}{-2} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224943 1069 "e-20179" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224943 1071 "What is the maximum value of the function $j$ defined by\n$j(x) = -2(x + 3)^2 - 5$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224943 1072 #uuid "e9ac817a-ea27-5d9a-89d5-ff18635cb32a" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224943 1074 "The function is a quadratic function with vertex at $(h, k)$, written in vertex form:\n\n$$\nj(x) = a(x - h)^{2} + k\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\na = -2, \\quad h = -3, \\quad k = -5\n$$\n\nSince the coefficient $a = -2$ is negative, the parabola opens downward, and the vertex represents the maximum value.\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of the function $j$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nj(h) &= k \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224944 1069 "e-20180" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224944 1071 "The function $t$ is defined by the equation\n$t(x) = -6(x - 6)^2$.\nFor what value of $x$ does $t(x)$ reach its maximum?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224944 1072 #uuid "71c49caa-2aee-5903-b34f-4edc108ff571" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224944 1074 "We are given a quadratic function that describes $t(x)$, and we want to find its maximum. The function is written in vertex form:\n\n$$\nt(x) = -6(x - 6)^2 + 0\n$$\n\nThis is of the form\n\n$$\nt(x) = a(x - h)^{2} + k\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na = -6, \\quad h = 6, \\quad k = 0\n$$\n\nSince the coefficient $a = -6$ is negative, the parabola opens downward, and the vertex represents the maximum value.\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ at which the function $t$ reaches its maximum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= h \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224945 1069 "e-20182" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224945 1071 "A drone’s elevation above the ground, in meters, is modeled by the function\n$v(t) = -0.4(t - 5)^2 + 20$,\nwhere $t$ is the time in seconds since it took off, for $0 \\leq t \\leq 10$. Which of the following best describes the vertex of the graph of $y = v(t)$ in the $ty$-plane?\n\n1. The drone’s maximum elevation was $20$ meters above the ground.\n2. The drone’s maximum elevation was $5$ meters above the ground.\n3. The drone was $20$ meters above the ground at launch.\n4. The drone was $5$ meters above the ground at launch." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224945 1072 #uuid "c3d1790d-6911-5814-8240-4b8915cfe171" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224945 1074 "The elevation function is a quadratic function with vertex at $(h, k)$, written in vertex form:\n\n$$\nv(t) = a(t - h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nHere, we have the vertex is at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (5, 20)\n$$\n\nSince the coefficient $a = -0.4$ is negative, the parabola opens downward, and the vertex represents the maximum elevation of the drone.\n\nTherefore, the vertex tells us that \"The drone’s maximum elevation was $20$ meters above the ground.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224946 1069 "e-20186" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224946 1071 "The product of a positive number $x$ and the number that is $6$ more than three times $x$ is $297$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224946 1072 #uuid "844e5137-bd2a-5c0a-a8c3-1665975c7297" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224946 1074 "We are told that the product of a positive number $x$ and the number that is $6$ more than three times $x$ is $297$. This gives the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(3x + 6) &= 297 \\\\\n3x^{2} + 6x &= 297 \\\\\n3x^{2} + 6x - 297 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x - 99 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since this equation is a quadratic of the form\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwe apply the quadratic formula to solve it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (-2 ± \\sqrt{(2)^{2} - 4(1)(-99)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 ± \\sqrt{4 + 396}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 ± \\sqrt{400}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 ± 20}{2} \\\\\n&=-11, 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the two solutions are $x = -11$ and $x = 9$.\n\nSince $x$ must be a positive number, we take the positive root. Therefore, the value of $x$ is $9$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224947 1069 "e-20189" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224947 1071 "$D(t) = -1.4(t - 18)^2 + 210$\n\nA wildlife researcher found that the function above models the population density $D(t)$, in deer per square kilometer, as a function of average temperature $t$ in degrees Celsius. According to this model, what temperature gives the maximum population density?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224947 1072 #uuid "09c73234-79b6-59bc-b3ff-c10f17bf33a0" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224947 1074 "We are given a quadratic function that models population density, and we are asked to find the temperature that maximizes it.\n\nThe function is a quadratic in vertex form:\n\n$$\nD(t) = a(t - h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\na = -1.4, \\quad h = 18, \\quad k = 210\n$$\n\nSince the coefficient $a = -1.4$ is negative, the parabola opens downward, and the vertex represents the maximum population density.\n\nTherefore, the temperature that gives the maximum population density is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= h \\\\\n&= 18^{∘}Celsius\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224948 1069 "e-20192" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224948 1071 "A basketball is thrown upward from a platform. The equation $h = -5.3 t^{2} + 16 t + 24$ represents this situation, where $h$ is the height of the basketball above the ground, in meters, $t$ seconds after it is thrown. Which number represents the height, in meters, from which the basketball was thrown?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224948 1072 #uuid "a08a15af-7628-5123-9556-30d724f94817" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224948 1074 "We are told that the equation\n\n$$\nh = -5.3t^{2} + 16t + 24\n$$\n\ngives the height of the basketball $t$ seconds after it is thrown.\n\nThe initial height from which the basketball was thrown is its height at time $t = 0$.\n\nThus, after substituting this value into the given equation, we find that the initial height is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= -5.3t^{2} + 16t + 24 \\\\\n&=-5.3(0)^{2} + 16(0) + 24 \\\\\n&= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the basketball was thrown from a height of $24$ meters." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224949 1069 "e-20197" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224949 1071 "The equation\n$z(x) + 3 = x^{2}$\nrelates the value of $x$ and its corresponding value of $z(x)$ for the function $z$. What is the minimum value of the function $z$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224949 1072 #uuid "6d1bc24f-f19a-59e2-9a3e-dbc4f4e4e0af" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224949 1074 "We start by isolating $z(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz(x) + 3 &= x^{2} \\\\\nz(x) &= x^{2} - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, this equation is a quadratic function of the form:\n\n$$\nz(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwhere $a = 1$, $b = 0$, and $c = -3$.\n\nBecause $a > 0$, the parabola opens upward, so it has a minimum at its vertex, whose $x$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\\n&= - (0)/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting this value into the given function, we find that its minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz(0) &= (0)^{2} - 3 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224950 1069 "e-20198" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224950 1071 "The function $r$ is defined by\n$r(x) = x^2 + 17$.\nFor which value of $x$ is $r(x) = 81$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224950 1072 #uuid "0b996b7d-dffd-59c6-92df-ef220e950c41" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224950 1074 "We start by setting up the equation and then solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(x) &= x^{2} + 17 \\\\\n81 &= x^{2} + 17 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 81 - 17 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 64 \\\\\nx &= ± \\sqrt{64} \\\\\nx &= ± 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the correct answer is $8$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224951 1069 "e-20215" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224951 1071 "![](images/f9/f9ad52f0a6a191ed8626a79c137f07f4f7c2e15a7cbd2077228da67326061108.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown, where the function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = ax^{3} + bx^{2} + cx + d$ and $a, b, c, d$ are constants. For how many distinct values of $x$ does $f(x) = 0$?\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224951 1072 #uuid "33354bf0-42ec-5a6d-b982-9b34ae2f852d" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224951 1074 "We are given the graph of a cubic function and asked how many values of $x$ are such that\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nif and only if the graph of $f$ passes through the point $(x,0)$. Now, by observing the graph, we can see that the curve passes through the points $(-2,0)$, $(1,0)$, and $(4,0)$.\n\nGiven that the sum of the multiplicities of the roots for a third-degree polynomial is $3$, then all roots have multiplicity $1$ and there are no other roots.\n\nTherefore, there are **three** values where\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nThese values are $x = -2$, $x = 1$, and $x = 4$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224952 1069 "e-20219" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224952 1071 "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-8$ | $6$ |\n| $-5$ | $-3$ |\n| $-2$ | $6$ |\n\nThe table shows three values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $y$, where $y = p(x) + 9$ and $p$ is a quadratic function. What is the $y$-coordinate of the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = p(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?\n\n$XXXXX$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224952 1072 #uuid "317063b0-9fdd-5103-ae24-7c2d615093cd" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224952 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\ny = p(x) + 9\n$$\n\nso to find the values of $p(x)$, we subtract $9$ from each $y$-value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(-8) &= 6 - 9 =-3 \\\\\np(-5) &= -3 - 9 =-12 \\\\\np(-2) &= 6 - 9 =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we observe that\n\n$$\np(-8) = p(-2) = -3\n$$\n\nSince these values are equal and symmetric, the axis of symmetry must lie halfway between $x = -8$ and $x = -2$. Hence, the axis of symmetry is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-8 + (-2)}{2} = -5\n$$\n\nSo, the vertex occurs at $x = -5$, and since\n\n$$\np(-5) = -12\n$$\n\nthe vertex is the point $(-5,-12)$.\n\nThus, the equation of the parabola in vertex form is given by the following equation:\n\n$$\np(x) = a(x+5)^2 - 12\n$$\n\nWe can compute $a$ by substituting any of the obtained function values into the equation above. Let's take the value $p(-8)=-3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(x) &= a(x + 5)^{2} - 12 \\\\\n- 3 &= a(-8 + 5)^{2} - 12 \\\\\n- 3 &= a(-3)^{2} - 12 \\\\\n- 3 &= 9a - 12 \\\\\n9a &= 9 \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola $y=p(x)$ is\n\n$$\np(x) = (x+5)^2 - 12\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = p(x)$, we need to compute $p(0){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(0) &= (0 + 5)^{2} - 12 \\\\\n&= 25 - 12 \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is $\\bbox[3pt,border:1px solid lightgray]{13}$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224953 1069 "e-20220" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224953 1071 "| $x$ | $q(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $b^{-7}$ |\n| $2$ | $b^{3}$ |\n| $3$ | $b^{8}$ |\n\nFor the exponential function $q$, the table above shows several values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $q(x)$, where $b$ is a constant greater than $1$. If $m$ is a constant such that $q(m) = b^{23}$, what is the value of $m$?\n\n$XXX$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224953 1072 #uuid "92cbdc8c-ccd5-59ed-a56b-b9b598062435" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224953 1074 "We are told that $q$ is an exponential function, so the relationship between $x$ and $q$ must have the form\n\n$$\nq(x) = a \\cdot k^x\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial value and $k$ is the growth factor.\n\nWe will use the values from the table to write a system of equations for the parameters $a$ and $k$.\n\n- From $x = 0$, we have\n$q(0)|= b^{-7}; a \\cdot k^{0}|= b^{-7}; a|= b^{-7}$.\n- From $x = 2$, we have\n$q(2)|= b^{3}; a \\cdot k^{2}|= b^{3}$.\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= b^{-7} \\\\\na \\cdot k^{2} &= b^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a \\cdot k^{2}}{a} &= \\frac{b^{3}}{b^{-7}} \\\\\nk^{2} &= b^{10} \\\\\nk &= b^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $a=b^{-7}$ and $k=b^{5}$ into the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(x) &= a \\cdot k^{x} \\\\\n&= b^{-7} \\cdot (b^{5})^{x} \\\\\n&= b^{-7} \\cdot b^{5x} \\\\\n&= b^{5x - 7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that\n\n$$\nq(x) = b^{5x - 7}\n$$\n\nworks for $x = 3{:}$\n\n$$\nq(3) = b^{5(3)-7}=b^{8}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the value in the table.\n\nWe are asked to find $m$ such that\n\n$$\nq(m) = b^{23}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nq(m) = b^{5m - 7}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nb^{5m - 7} = b^{23}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate the exponents and solve for $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5m - 7 &= 23 \\\\\n5m &= 30 \\\\\nm &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224954 1069 "e-20224" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224954 1071 "| $x$ | $g(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $2$ |\n| $1$ | $-3$ |\n\nFor the quadratic function $g$, the table shows three values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $g(x)$. Which equation defines $g$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224954 1072 #uuid "1c1fd817-eb87-534b-a50b-0cba27935837" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224954 1074 "We are told that $g$ is a quadratic function, so the relationship between $x$ and $g$ must have the form\n\n$$\ng(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nWe will use the values from the table to write a system of equations for the parameters $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n\n- From $x = -2$, we have\n$g(-2)|= 0; a(-2)^{2} + b(-2) + c|= 0; 4a - 2b + c|= 0$.\n- From $x = 1$, we have\n$g(1)|=-3; a(1)^{2} + b(1) + c|=-3; a + b + c|=-3$.\n- From $x = 0$, we have\n$g(0)|= 2; a(0)^{2} + b(0) + c|= 2; c|= 2$.\n\nNow, we substitute $c = 2$ into the other two equations.\n\n- From $4a - 2b + c = 0$, we have\n$4a - 2b + 2|= 0; 4a - 2b|=-2; 2a - b|=-1$.\n- From $a + b + c = -3$, we have\n$a + b + 2|=-3; a + b|=-5$.\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - b &= -1 \\\\\na + b &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a &= -6 \\\\\na &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the value $a=-2$ into\n\n$$\na + b = -5{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n-2 + b = -5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b = -3\n$$\n\nFinally, collecting $a=-2$, $b=-3$, and $c=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= ax^{2} + bx + c \\\\\n&=-2x^{2} - 3x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224955 1069 "e-20227" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224955 1071 "| Time (years) | Value (dollars) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $950.00$ |\n| $1$ | $959.50$ |\n| $2$ | $969.10$ |\n\nMaya invested in a certificate of deposit that earns interest over time. The table shows the exponential relationship between the number of years $t$ since the investment began and the total value $v$, in dollars, of the account.\n\nWhat is the equation that represents the relationship between $t$ and $v$ if no additional money was added or withdrawn?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224955 1072 #uuid "6e6f6aee-0d9a-54d4-b8b9-8f10c7287496" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224955 1074 "We are told that $v$ grows exponentially over time, so the relationship between $t$ and $v$ must have the form\n\n$$\nv(t) = a \\cdot b^t\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial amount and $b$ is the growth factor.\n\nFrom the table, we see that the value at time $t = 0$ is $950$, so\n$a = 950$.\n\nTherefore, the function has the form\n\n$$\nv(t)=950\\cdot b^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the growth factor $b$, we use the value at $t=1{:}$\n\n$$\nv(1)=950\\cdot b = 959.50\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $950$ to isolate $b{:}$\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{959.50}{950}=1.01\n$$\n\nSo, the exponential equation is\n\n$$\nv(t)=950\\cdot (1.01)^t\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that this equation works for $t=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(2) &= 950 \\cdot (1.01)^{2} \\\\\n&= 969.10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the value in the table.\n\nTherefore, the equation representing the relationship is\n\n$$\nv = 950 \\cdot (1.01)^t\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224956 1069 "e-20229" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224956 1071 "| $x$ | $h(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $7$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{7}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{49}$ |\n\nThe table above shows values of an exponential function $h(x) = a \\cdot b^x$. Determine the constants $a$ and $b$, and write the equation that defines $h(x)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224956 1072 #uuid "e0aa006e-f822-5c72-adee-700ceb2484d3" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224956 1074 "We are told that $h$ is an exponential function, so the relationship between $x$ and $h$ must have the form\n\n$$\nh(x) = a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial value and $b$ is the growth factor.\n\nFrom the table, we see that the value at $x = 0$ is $1$, so\n\n$$\nh(0) = a = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the form\n\n$$\nh(x) = b^{x}\n$$\n\nTo find the base $b$, we use the value at $x = 1{:}$\n\n$$\nh(1) = b = \\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the exponential equation is\n\n$$\nh(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that this works for other values in the table.\n\n- When $x = 2$, we have\n$h(2)= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{49}. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- When $x = -1$, we have\n$h(-1) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^{-1} = 7. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nBoth values match the table. So, the equation is\n\n$$\nh(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^x\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224957 1069 "e-20251" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224957 1071 "The height, in meters, of a drone as it ascends during flight can be modeled by the function $h(t) = -4t^{2} + 52t + 1$. Which of the following statements best interprets the equation $h(12) = 49$?\n\n1. The height of the drone $12$ seconds after it begins flying is $49$ meters.\n2. The height of the drone $49$ seconds after it begins flying is $12$ meters.\n3. The drone reaches a maximum height of $49$ meters at $12$ seconds.\n4. The drone’s average speed over the first $12$ seconds is $49$ meters per second." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224957 1072 #uuid "cda2dc24-c60e-5343-8fd8-38bafa09a733" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224957 1074 "The function\n\n$$\nh(t) = -4t^{2} + 52t + 1\n$$\n\nmodels the height of a drone, in meters, $t$ seconds after it begins flying.\n\nSubstituting $t = 12$ into the formula gives $h(12)$, which represents the height of the drone $12$ seconds after it begins flying.\n\nSince $h(12) = 49$, this means that the drone is $49$ meters above the ground $12$ seconds after it begins flying.\n\nTherefore, the best interpretation is as follows:\n\n> The height of the drone $12$ seconds after it begins flying is $49$ meters." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224958 1069 "e-20258" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224958 1071 "![](images/96/96a52d3e1299c3918469dbf7c2570f15b30382a8b325516a59b361a24dbf1a42.png)\n\nThe rational function $r$ is defined by an equation in the form $r(x) = \\dfrac{a}{x + b}$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. The partial graph of $y = r(x)$ is shown above. If $s(x) = r(x + 2)$, which equation could define the function $s$?\n\n1. $s(x) = \\dfrac{6}{x + 5}$\n2. $s(x) = \\dfrac{6}{x + 3}$\n3. $s(x) = \\dfrac{6}{x + 2}$\n4. $s(x) = \\dfrac{6}{x - 5}$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224958 1072 #uuid "a3bf13ea-2554-5db0-8a31-87f7bb2564e2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224958 1074 "From the graph of\n\n$$\nr(x) = \\dfrac{a}{x + b}\n$$\n\nwe observe that the vertical asymptote occurs at $x = -3$, which means the denominator is zero when $x = -3$. Then, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= -3 + b \\\\\nb &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of $r(x)$ becomes\n\n$$\nr(x) = \\dfrac{a}{x + 3}\n$$\n\nNow, to identify the value of $a$, we choose a convenient point on the graph. For example, when $x = -2$, the graph passes through $y = 6$. Plugging $(-2, 6)$ into $r(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(x) &= \\frac{a}{x + 3} \\\\\n6 &= \\frac{a}{-2 + 3} \\\\\n6 &= \\frac{a}{1} \\\\\na &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of $r(x)$ is\n\n$$\nr(x) = \\dfrac{6}{x + 3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find an expression for $s(x)$, we replace each $x$ in $r(x)$ by $(x+2)$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(x) &= r(x + 2) \\\\\n&= (6)/((x + 2) + 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{x + 5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224959 1069 "e-20263" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224959 1071 "$r(x) = \\dfrac{a - 5}{x} + 7$\nIn the given function above, $a$ is constant. The graph of the function $r$ in the $xy$-plane, where $y = r(x)$, is shifted $2$ units up and **$3$ units to the left** to produce the graph of $y = s(x)$. Which equation defines function $s$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224959 1072 #uuid "ccfd3827-b429-5163-8a29-d0d4c11fed9e" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224959 1074 "To apply a transformation to the graph of a function, we modify its equation according to the direction of the shift:\n\n- A horizontal shift left by $3$ units means we replace every $x$ in the original function with $(x + 3)$.\n- A vertical shift up by $2$ units means we add $2$ to the entire function.\n\nWe start with the function:\n\n$$\nr(x) = \\dfrac{a - 5}{x} + 7\n$$\n\nTo shift left $3$ units, we replace $x$ with $x + 3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 7\n$$\n\nThen, to shift up $2$ units, we add $2$ to the whole expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 7\\right) + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by simplifying, we find the equation that defines $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(x) &= (\\frac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 7) + 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224960 1069 "e-20265" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224960 1071 "![](images/a8/a806b34487c4893ce9f2460cc2da276e0a03eb1e8b3d87917ad6e5908d28e5eb.png)\n\nThe graph shown gives the estimated number of bacteria in a refrigerated lab culture as a function of the number of hours since the culture was removed from the fridge.\nWhat is the best interpretation of the $y$-intercept of the graph in this context?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224960 1072 #uuid "c332ff57-6fdf-53a3-8a98-aade0846623d" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224960 1074 "The $y$-intercept of a graph of $y=f(x)$ represents the value of $y$ when $x=0$.\n\nIn this context, the variable $x$ represents the number of hours since the culture was removed from the fridge, and $y$ is the number of bacteria present in the culture.\n\nSo the $y$-intercept represents the number of bacteria *at the moment the culture was removed from the fridge*, i.e., when $x = 0$.\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the point $(0, 340)$ lies on the curve. This means there were $340$ bacteria at the start.\n\nTherefore, the correct interpretation is as follows:\n\n> The estimated number of bacteria in the culture was $340$ when it was removed from the fridge." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224961 1069 "e-20266" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224961 1071 "![](images/0d/0dfa2ff43445b2ce32e89025e1718b5575e9f45708036ba561a1ad3f8324b964.png)\n\nA soccer ball is kicked into the air. The graph shows the ball’s height above the ground, $y$, in meters, $t$ seconds after it was kicked. What is the best interpretation of the $x$-intercept of the graph?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224961 1072 #uuid "9b411d7e-60b2-56ee-b4f9-864bdbcc8bee" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224961 1074 "The $x$-intercept of the graph is the point where the graph intersects the $x$-axis. This happens when the height is $0$, which corresponds to the ball reaching the ground.\n\nFrom the graph, we observe that the $x$-intercept occurs at approximately $t = 3$, which represents the amount of time that passed before the ball hit the ground.\n\nTherefore, the best interpretation of the $x$-intercept of the graph is the following:\n\n> The ball hits the ground at $3$ seconds." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224962 1069 "e-20267" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224962 1071 "![](images/87/872823c02d01ac9a25c8ba4e436d5cf975350bfc040aabc5542077ef3a6feb71.png)\n\nAn archer shoots an arrow straight upward. The graph shown models the arrow’s height above the ground, $y$, in meters, $x$ seconds after release. What is the intervals of time was the arrow falling throughout the entire interval?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224962 1072 #uuid "7c1cd52f-3401-575f-9193-a13c04cf039a" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224962 1074 "We want to find when the height was decreasing continuously. The graph is a downward-opening parabola with a vertex at $x=2.8$ seconds.\n\nThe arrow rises until the peak at $x=2.8$, then descends. So, it was falling continuously for\n\n$$\n2.8\\le x\\le 5.2\n$$\n\nAmong the given choices, the only interval lying entirely within this falling section is\n\n$$\n\\text{From} x = 3 \\text{to} x = 5\n$$\n\n#" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224963 1069 "e-20268" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224963 1071 "![](images/4e/4e3a039022c6652be177e5d5cb2f633766b72e21136518b3df832f8557d2a1a6.png)\n\nA streaming platform tracks its subscriber count. The graph shows the number of subscribers, $y$, in thousands, $x$ months after a new series premiered, where $0 \\le x \\le 5$. What is the best describes the increase in subscribers from $x = 0$ to $x = 3$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224963 1072 #uuid "23f7c41d-986c-5b3e-8d15-9821c038703f" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224963 1074 "From the graph, we have:\n\n- At $x = 0{:}$ the number of subscribers is $3$ thousands.\n- At $x = 3{:}$ the number of subscribers is approximately $9$ thousands.\n\nTo find how many times larger the value at $x = 3$ is compared to $x = 0$, we divide the number of subscribers at $x = 3$ by the number at $x = 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{,}000}{3{,}000} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best interpretation that describes the increase from $x = 0$ to $x = 3$ is the following:\n\n> *The number of subscribers at $x = 3$ is $3$ times the number at* $x = 0$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224964 1069 "e-20270" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224964 1071 "The $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = 7 - 4x^{2}$ in the $xy$-plane is $(0, y)$. What is the value of $y$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224964 1072 #uuid "bdd74a5a-66db-531d-aea7-41c975274ddd" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224964 1074 "To find the value of the $y$-coordinate of the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x = 0$ into the equation of the graph and solve for $y$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 7 - 4x^{2} \\\\\n&= 7 - 4(0)^{2} \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224965 1069 "e-20272" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224965 1071 "![](images/76/76f5e4c14cc7e84ae23ffcf5e4d2ba602a0e5ad71add20b8b133e76bcfb8de66.png)\n\nThe graph models the number of bike rentals recorded by a city program $x$ months after the start of January $2022$, where $0 \\leq x \\leq 8$. According to the model, what is the predicted number of bike rentals recorded at the start of January $2022$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224965 1072 #uuid "9b978a2f-8acb-51d5-bcad-1337bf5744cf" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224965 1074 "We want to predict the number of bike rentals recorded at the start of January $2022$.\n\nSince $x$ represents the number of months after the start of January $2022$ and $y$ is the number of rentals, the $y$-intercept ($x=0$) corresponds to the number at the beginning of that month.\n\nFrom the graph, we note that the curve passes through $(0,10)$. Substituting $x=0$ gives $y=10$, so the model predicts $10$ rentals at the start of January $2022$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224966 1069 "e-20283" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224966 1071 "![](images/5d/5dec8cf1e118a1b7744e0c383f8234bb6210f9df55232a6560d2e520947f2814.png)\n\nThe graph shows the predicted value $y$, in dollars, of a particular refrigerator $x$ years after it is first purchased. Which of the following is closest to the predicted value of the refrigerator $2$ years after it is first purchased?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224966 1072 #uuid "e2a36e8f-fb26-5da6-8fff-f11f2cf00dcb" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224966 1074 "We want the predicted value at $x=2$. Since $x$ counts years after purchase and $y$ gives the value, we read the point on the curve above $x=2$ and project it to the $y$-axis.\n\nFrom the graph, the point above $x=2$ lies between the $5{,}000$ and $10{,}000$ marks on the $y$-axis. From the given options, the closest estimate is $\\[math]7{,}300$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224967 1069 "e-20284" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224967 1071 "![](images/fc/fc8776a44a8578122c838f0e8a708133610ecbf562225fd777a4abf717875244.png)\n\nA football is punted into the air. The graph shows the football’s height above the ground, in meters, at time $x$ seconds after it is kicked. What is the best interpretation of the marked point $(2,\\ 19.2)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224967 1072 #uuid "f996190e-ff8d-583b-82c8-fb5cffdcaaad" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224967 1074 "We begin by interpreting each coordinate of the point $(2,\\ 19.2){:}$\n\n- $x = 2$ represents the time in seconds after the football was kicked.\n- $y = 19.2$ represents the football’s height above the ground in meters at that moment.\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,\\ 19.2)$ indicates the following:\n\n> $2$ seconds after it was kicked, the football’s height above the ground is $19.2$ meters." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224968 1069 "e-20892" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224968 1071 "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 7x^{-1}$. What is the value of $f(4)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224968 1072 #uuid "79a5539d-ed7f-540f-8fec-52755c34e884" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224968 1074 "Explanation:\n\nTo find $f(4)$, we substitute $x = 4$ into the function's definition and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 7x^{-1} \\\\\nf(4) &= 7 \\cdot 4^{-1} \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224969 1069 "e-21094" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224969 1071 "For $\\triangle{JKL}$, we are given $JK = 0.84\\, \\mathrm{in}$ and $KL = 0.84\\, \\mathrm{in}$. Which of the following statements is sufficient to prove that $\\triangle{JKL}$ is equilateral?\n\n1. $JL = 0.42 \\, \\mathrm{in}$\n2. $JL = 0.84 \\, \\mathrm{in}$\n3. $JL = 0.42\\sqrt{3} \\, \\mathrm{in}$\n4. No additional information is needed" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224969 1072 #uuid "03e10ded-885f-522c-9f0d-9a76d635f717" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224969 1074 "A triangle is equilateral if all three sides have the same length.\n\nIn our case, $\\triangle{JKL}$ is an equilateral triangle if\n\n$$\nJL = JK = KL\n$$\n\nSince we are given that\n\n$$\nJK = KL = 0.84\\, \\mathrm{in}\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that $\\triangle{JKL}$ is an equilateral triangle if\n\n$$\nJL = 0.84 \\, \\mathrm{in}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224970 1069 "e-21143" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224970 1071 "![](images/6a/6a0b05f246168e186567b6f4b65c1de926e68bf32d21a47f74c9e8b8943df275.png)\n\nThe figure above (not drawn to scale) represents a circular patio surrounded by a ring-shaped border of uniform width $x$ meters. The patio with the border has a radius of $11$ meters. If the area of the patio without the border is $49\\pi$ square meters, write an equation that defines the value of $x$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224970 1072 #uuid "bb93c88a-7749-509f-905b-c4ac55a16087" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224970 1074 "First, we notice that the radius of the patio without the border is $11 - x$ meters.\n\nThus, the area of the patio without the border is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= π(11 - x)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate this expression to $49\\pi$ and obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nπ(11 - x)^{2} &= 49π \\\\\n(11 - x)^{2} &= 49 \\\\\n121 - 22x + x^{2} &= 49 \\\\\nx^{2} - 22x + 72 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224971 1069 "e-21144" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224971 1071 "![](images/95/95b2fc631a81b2bfbbc1ba3d0d2eaa5242aecc5755cf178881f14e3f952e7d95.png)\n\nThe figure above (drawn not to scale) represents a rectangular courtyard with a length of $12$ meters and a width of $10$ meters that is surrounded by a border with a uniform width of $x$ meters. If the combined area of the courtyard and the border is $168$ square meters, what is the value of $x$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224971 1072 #uuid "9db6a078-2d64-5a99-92a7-155e606f04f6" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224971 1074 "First, we write down the dimensions of the courtyard, including the border:\n\n- the length is given by $2x+12$ meters, and\n- the width is given by $2x+10$ meters.\n\nThus, the area of the courtyard with the border is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= length \\cdot width \\\\\n&= (2x + 12)(2x + 10)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate this expression to (168) and obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 12)(2x + 10) &= 168 \\\\\n4x^{2} + 24x + 20x + 120 &= 168 \\\\\n4x^{2} + 44x + 120 &= 168 \\\\\n4x^{2} + 44x - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 11x - 12 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the obtained equation using the quadratic formula\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\na=1,\\qquad b=11,\\qquad c=-12\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (-11 ± \\sqrt{11^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-12)})/(2 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-11 ± \\sqrt{121 + 48}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-11 ± \\sqrt{169}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-11 ± 13}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=-12$ or $x=1$. We disregard the negative solution since the width of the border must be positive.\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ meter." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224972 1069 "e-21145" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224972 1071 "![](images/c6/c69a45dd9453514efc4fed45211e10d415f1fe224525d1ce0545eb159deb0db4.png)\n\nThe figure above (not drawn to scale) represents a circular fountain surrounded by a border with a uniform width of $x$ meters. The fountain with the border has a radius of $12$ meters. If the area of the fountain without the border is $25\\pi$ square meters, what is the value of $x$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224972 1072 #uuid "cc325a67-cde1-5050-9968-0826dc6c6890" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224972 1074 "First, we notice that the radius of the fountain, without the border, is $12-x$ meters.\n\nThus, the area of the fountain without the border is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi(12-x)^2\n$$\n\nNow, we equate this expression to $25\\pi$ and obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nπ(12 - x)^{2} &= 25π \\\\\n(12 - x)^{2} &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a perfect square on the left-hand side, we can solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12 - x)^{2} &= 25 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(12 - x)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n\\mid 12 - x \\mid &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n12-x = 5\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 12-x = -5\n$$\n\nSo, $x=7$ or $x=17$. We disregard $x=17$ since then the inner radius is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nradius &= 12 - x \\\\\n&= 12 - 17 \\\\\n&=-5 < 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is impossible.\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ meters." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224973 1069 "e-21146" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224973 1071 "$P = 2l + 2(l+7)$\nThe perimeter $P$, in yards, of a rectangle can be represented by the expression shown above, where $l$ is the length, in yards, of the rectangle. What expression represents the width, in yards, of the rectangle?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224973 1072 #uuid "af7c0018-739e-5a5f-9001-7fb77b6dcea9" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224973 1074 "The perimeter of a rectangle is given by\n\n$$\nP = 2\\cdot\\text{length} + 2\\cdot \\text{width}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nP = 2\\cdot l + 2\\cdot (l+7)\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length.\n\nTherefore, comparing the two expressions, we conclude that the width must be\n\n$$\nl+7\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224974 1069 "e-21147" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224974 1071 "$V = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi \\cdot (r + 2) \\cdot r^2$\nA right cone has a height that is $2$ inches more than its radius $r$ of the base. The function $V$, given above, models the volume of the cone, in cubic inches. Which expression represents the height, in inches, of the cone?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224974 1072 #uuid "f6c3b3d4-3b3b-5404-bd06-1d54b423e784" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224974 1074 "The volume of a right cone is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi \\cdot \\text{height} \\cdot \\text{radius}^2\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi \\cdot (r+2) \\cdot r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the base.\n\nTherefore, comparing the two expressions, we conclude that the height must be\n$r+2$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224975 1069 "e-21148" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224975 1071 "A right cone has a height of $x$ meters. The radius of the cone's base is $2$ meters more than the height. What is the function $V$ that gives the volume of the cone, in cubic meters, in terms of the height of the cone?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224975 1072 #uuid "fb82df48-b38a-5ec0-91ca-c2a37cbb3cab" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224975 1074 "From the description, our right cone has the following measurements:\n\n- The height is $h = x$ meters.\n- The radius of the base is $r = x+2$ meters ($2$ meters more than the height).\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cone is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot (x + 2)^{2} \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}πx(x + 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224976 1069 "e-21149" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224976 1071 "A triangle has a height of $x$ inches. The length of the triangle's base is $5$ inches more than the height. What function $A$ gives the area of the triangle, in square inches, in terms of the height of the triangle?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224976 1072 #uuid "36f9f4ff-f340-540f-983c-1caa1538cfa8" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224976 1074 "From the description, our triangle has the following measurements:\n\n- The height is $h = x$ inches.\n- The base is $b = x+5$ inches ($5$ inches more than the height).\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= \\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (x + 5) \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x(x + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224977 1069 "e-21150" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224977 1071 "A right rectangular prism has a base width of $x$ meters. The length of the prism's base is $7$ meters, and its height is $2x$ meters less than the length. Which function $S$ gives the surface area of the prism, in square meters, in terms of the width of the prism's base?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224977 1072 #uuid "33045ace-4416-5fb5-a640-2cd588c3ec89" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224977 1074 "From the description, our right rectangular prism has the following measurements:\n\n- The width is $w = x$ meters.\n- The length is $l = 7$ meters.\n- The height is $h = 7 - 2x$ meters ($2x$ meters less than the length).\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the prism is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(x) &= 2(lw + lh + wh) \\\\\n&= 2(7 \\cdot x + 7 \\cdot (7 - 2x) + x \\cdot (7 - 2x)) \\\\\n&= 2(7x + 49 - 14x + 7x - 2x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2(49 - 2x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224978 1069 "e-21152" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224978 1071 "Equilateral triangle $S$ has a side length of $x$ centimeters. Equilateral triangle $T$ has a perimeter that is $15$ centimeters less than the perimeter of triangle $S$. What is the function $A$ that represents the area of triangle $T$, in square centimeters, where $x > 5$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224978 1072 #uuid "e1aa8ca5-5935-5d13-a2fa-751e4101c783" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224978 1074 "Let\n\n- $x$ be the side length of equilateral triangle $S$, and\n- $y$ be the side length of equilateral triangle $T$.\n\nThen,\n\n- the perimeter of triangle $S$ is $3x$, and\n- the perimeter of triangle $T$ is $3y$.\n\nWe are told that the perimeter of triangle $T$ is $15$ centimeters less than the perimeter of triangle $S$. In symbols, this means that\n\n$$\n3y = 3x - 15\n$$\n\nSince we want to find an expression for the area of triangle $T$, we solve this equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3y &= 3x - 15 \\\\\n\\frac{3y}{3} &= \\frac{3x - 15}{3} \\\\\ny &= x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $A$ that represents the area of triangle $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}y^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(x - 5)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224979 1069 "e-21153" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224979 1071 "Cube $M$ has a side length of $m$ inches. Cube $N$ has a surface area that is $18$ square inches greater than one-half of the surface area of cube $M$. What is the function $V$ that represents the volume of a cube $N$, in cubic inches?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224979 1072 #uuid "dc9b5205-0229-51ea-9179-f75145801cc7" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224979 1074 "Let\n\n- $m$ be the side length of cube $M$, and\n- $n$ be the side length of cube $N$.\n\nThen,\n\n- the surface area of cube $M$ is $6m^2$, and\n- the surface area of cube $N$ is $6n^2$.\n\nWe are told that the surface area of cube $N$ is $18$ square inches greater than one half of the surface area of cube $M$. In symbols, this means that\n\n$$\n6n^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 6m^2 + 18\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the obtained equation for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6n^{2} &= 3m^{2} + 18 \\\\\n\\frac{6n^{2}}{6} &= \\frac{3m^{2} + 18}{6} \\\\\nn^{2} &= \\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3 \\\\\nn &= \\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solutions since the side length must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the function $V$ that represents the volume of cube $N$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(m) &= n^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3})^{3} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3)^{1/2})^{3} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3)^{3})^{1/2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3)^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224980 1069 "e-21158" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224980 1071 "Find the area of a circle inscribed in a square with sides of length $18\\,\\textrm{m}$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224980 1072 #uuid "dcf99d1d-f30f-59d3-860b-9b2b529b6ce2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224980 1074 "![](images/c8/c835620833e8814cad234d0ec91c03673ac016b5fa7e6f27d4b72fd452b41235.png)\n\nThe side of the square is $18 \\: \\textrm{m}$, which means that the diameter $d$ of the circle is\n\n$$\nd = 18 \\: \\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nThus, the radius $r$ of the circle is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{d}{2} = \\dfrac{18}{2} = 9 \\: \\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n&= π(9)^{2} \\\\\n&= 81πm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224981 1069 "e-21159" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224981 1071 "Given that the area of a square that's fully inscribed in a circle is $162\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2$, what is the circle's radius?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224981 1072 #uuid "9e98d570-2131-5a3e-98b2-cae07d3d1851" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224981 1074 "![](images/5b/5bb82f56d8ede8d8322bf047a56fc8be6f3872a42bf056afd89880da9887b0e4.png)\n\nIf $d$ is a diagonal of a square, then the area of the square is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{1}{2}d^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known area and solving for $d$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}d^{2} \\\\\n162 &= \\frac{1}{2}d^{2} \\\\\n324 &= d^{2} \\\\\nd &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagonal of the square inscribed in the circle equals the circle's diameter, and the diameter is twice the radius. Therefore, the radius is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{d}{2} = \\dfrac{18}{2} = 9\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224982 1069 "e-21163" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224982 1071 "The circumference of a circle inscribed in a square is $7\\pi$. What is the area of the square?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224982 1072 #uuid "80bb9793-10eb-56b2-9b88-ba35375010c9" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224982 1074 "![](images/c8/c835620833e8814cad234d0ec91c03673ac016b5fa7e6f27d4b72fd452b41235.png)\n\nThe formula for a circle's circumference is $C = \\pi d$, where $d$ is the diameter. So, we can find the diameter as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{C}{π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{π} \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diameter of the circle is $7$, which means that the side $s$ of the square is\n$s = 7$.\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = s^2 = (7)^2 = 49\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224983 1069 "e-21164" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224983 1071 "Determine the length of the side of a square that's fully inscribed in a circle, given that the circle's area is $4\\pi$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224983 1072 #uuid "f8b62462-c9c8-5c1e-93dc-3d3acd501285" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224983 1074 "![](images/d3/d349cde2d1540f10d43bc8a211cf101ff2eb0ca9cb6d84aaa816cae34af5de71.png)\n\nIf $r$ is the circle's radius, then the circle's area is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known area and solving for $r$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n4π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n4 &= r^{2} \\\\\nr &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagonal of the square inscribed in the circle equals the circle's diameter, and the diameter is twice the radius. Therefore, the diagonal is\n\n$$\nd = 2r = 2(2) = 4\n$$\n\nFinally, if $s$ is the length of the side of the square, then $2s^2 = d^2$. Substituting $d=4$ and solving for $s$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2s^{2} &= d^{2} \\\\\n2s^{2} &= 4^{2} \\\\\n2s^{2} &= 16 \\\\\ns^{2} &= 8 \\\\\ns &= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224984 1069 "e-21165" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224984 1071 "A circle of radius $7 \\: \\text{cm}$ is inscribed into an equilateral triangle, as shown above. What is the area of the triangle?\n\n![](images/5d/5d825777b6613650f7afe511c58b21c0df41d65d842d18252fa5ec165e951dda.png)" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224984 1072 #uuid "98bcb7a7-784a-50d9-8e3f-2f4a5e72598c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224984 1074 "To find the side length of the triangle, we draw line segments from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/be/be544e496d80cb95dc1c3260ea74f58a20b7db531f506871557c73c429871973.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- Let the longer leg, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, be $x$. Notice that it is half of our equilateral triangle's side length.\n- The shorter leg is the radius $r = 7 \\: \\text{cm}$ of the circle.\n\nNext, in the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the shorter leg:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n&= 7\\sqrt{3}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equilateral triangle's side length is\n\n$$\ns = 2 \\cdot 7 \\sqrt{3} = 14 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}s^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(14\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot 588 \\\\\n&= 147\\sqrt{3}cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224985 1069 "e-21166" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224985 1071 "![](images/94/94594802c73e052e9d8f333f13801a04bc6dc0f1e9fdd3d112ca2f0620e4d0ec.png)\n\nAn equilateral triangle is inscribed in a circle, as shown above. What is the area of the triangle if the radius of the circle is $6$ meters?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224985 1072 #uuid "3f6d062e-b5c1-5176-9cf4-5a64317c915b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224985 1074 "To find the area of the triangle, we draw radii from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a42f60fce0184d40831450e286c3670dc0113b98807c4ed0530c07aa508f0ed5.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- Let the longer leg, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, be $x$. Notice that it is half of our equilateral triangle's side length.\n- The hypotenuse is a radius of the circle and has a length $r = 6\\:\\text{m}$.\n\nNext, in the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\ntimes the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equilateral triangle's side length is\n\n$$\ns = 2x = 2 \\cdot 3 \\sqrt{3} = 6 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{m}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}s^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(6\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot 108 \\\\\n&= 27\\sqrt{3}m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224986 1069 "e-21167" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224986 1071 "The area of a circle inscribed in a square is $32\\pi \\,\\textrm{mm}^2$. Find the diagonal of the square." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224986 1072 #uuid "56ea480c-73f0-5f15-b794-0f94f9b26ef8" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224986 1074 "![](images/86/864676a13c102c547c8b4937d9eb2f8b3422340688630452611a7f111e489bb0.png)\n\nThe area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known area and solving for the radius $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n32π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n32π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n32 &= r^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand taking the square root gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{32} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle equals $4\\sqrt 2\\,\\textrm{mm}$.\n\nThe length $s$ of the square's side equals the diameter of the inscribed circle, and the diameter is twice the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= 2r \\\\\n&= 2(4\\sqrt{2}) \\\\\n&= 8\\sqrt{2}mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the length $d$ of the square's diagonal is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= s\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= 8\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 16mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224987 1069 "e-21168" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224987 1071 "The ratio between the longest side of a rectangle and its diagonal is $\\sqrt{3}:2$, and the area of the rectangle is $108\\sqrt{3}$ square inches. What is the length, in inches, of the shortest side of the rectangle?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224987 1072 #uuid "b7249dc6-cff4-5a72-bd4c-a2d1289ee66d" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224987 1074 "Let $s$ be the length of the shortest side. Then, we have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/4b/4b3047df372796183cfc309b75986b58bb0ee8fdb6aa6b80ab6cea16fa25c6a6.png)\n\nHere, in rectangle $ABCD$,\n\n- the length of the diagonal $\\overline{AC}$ is $2$ times the length of the shortest side $\\overline{BC}$, and\n- the length of the longest side $\\overline{AB}$ is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the length of the shortest side $\\overline{BC}$.\n\nThus, we have:\n\n$$\n{AC}:{AB} = 2:\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, since in the right triangle $ABC$, the ratio of the hypotenuse to the longest leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{hypotenuse}}{\\text{longest}} = \\dfrac{AC}{AB} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nwe have that $\\triangle ABC$ is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/f9/f9d1521ad22fd060819b2b19472a081287232a41f45cc7b9fec9562b39de11a3.png)\n\nRecall that the area of a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle whose shortest side has length $s$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2} s^2\n$$\n\nThe area of $\\triangle ADC$ is half the area of the rectangle:\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 108\\sqrt{3} = 54\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following equation that can be solved for $s{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}s^{2} &= 54\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3}s^{2} &= 108\\sqrt{3} \\\\\ns^{2} &= 108 \\\\\ns &= \\sqrt{108} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shortest side of the rectangle is\n\n$$\nBC = s= 6\\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{in}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224988 1069 "e-21170" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224988 1071 "A circle is inscribed into an equilateral triangle with a side length of $72$ feet, as shown above. What is the radius of the circle?\n\n![](images/da/da4eec9cfc4586722576b49ea51415dc626dad2850eceec56816580f4abe86a3.png)" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224988 1072 #uuid "14b624d8-29f0-5d11-b839-304c5403eae2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224988 1074 "To find the radius of the circle, we draw line segments from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/aecff45af3948fe8518a830c2190cca88d58c6b9457c1860f618bb34fa3ae00c.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$x = \\dfrac{ML}{2} = \\dfrac{72}{2} = 36$\n- The shorter leg is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the shorter leg:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n36 &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 12\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $12\\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224989 1069 "e-21171" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224989 1071 "![](images/a2/a261047506119ebea9fad10ac40811f380bc0e58839460ae1419874d26a9e3af.png)\n\nThe side of an equilateral triangle is $27$ feet. The three vertices of the triangle lie on a circle, as depicted above. What is the radius of the circle?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224989 1072 #uuid "e219941c-f193-5746-aed8-2d25d5b297cc" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224989 1074 "To find the radius of the circle, we draw radii from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c053b3b368ffd379091b1b72c4701c3b77c0438394235a0abede8676043086c.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The length of the longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$x = \\dfrac{AC}{2} = \\dfrac{27}{2} = 13.5$.\n- The length of the hypotenuse is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\ntimes the length of the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n13.5 &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot 13.5 \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 9\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $9 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224990 1069 "e-21173" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224990 1071 "A rectangle is inscribed in a circle such that each vertex of the rectangle lies on the circumference.\nThe ratio between the longest side of a rectangle and its diagonal is $\\sqrt{3}:2$, and the area of the rectangle is $200\\sqrt{3}$ square meters.\nWhat is the length, in meters, of the diameter of the circle?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224990 1072 #uuid "afdb3885-abf0-55f1-88b3-abb96611bb95" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224990 1074 "Let $s$ be the length of the shortest side. Then, we have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/f3/f33ba8b94e84b6e06aa70f68fb3fb54b71a3acb98577e8904e5f63478cafc6c0.png)\n\nHere, in rectangle $ABCD$,\n\n- the length of the diagonal $\\overline{AC}$ is $2$ times the length of the shortest side $\\overline{CD}$, and\n- the length of the longest side $\\overline{AD}$ is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the length of the shortest side $\\overline{CD}$.\n\nThus, we have:\n\n$$\n{AC}:{AD} = 2:\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, since in the right triangle $ADC$, the ratio of the hypotenuse to the longest leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{hypotenuse}}{\\text{longest}} = \\dfrac{AC}{AD} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nwe have that $\\triangle ADC$ is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/15/151639a04f35dcfc67bc034ba9985dc4b2dc5f354e78e63fe870a65dddd9d28e.png)\n\nRecall that the area of a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle whose shortest side has length $s$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2} s^2\n$$\n\nThe area of $\\triangle ADC$ is half the area of the rectangle:\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 200\\sqrt{3} = 100\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following equation that can be solved for $s{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}s^{2} &= 100\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3}s^{2} &= 200\\sqrt{3} \\\\\ns^{2} &= 200 \\\\\ns &= \\sqrt{200} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= 10\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the diagonal of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= 2s \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 10\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= 20\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the diameter of the circle is equal to the diagonal of the rectangle, we have that the diameter is\n\n$$\nAC = 20\\sqrt{2} \\: \\text{m}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224991 1069 "e-21174" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224991 1071 "Find the value of $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{127\\pi}{6}\\right)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224991 1072 #uuid "de6c8fce-3e98-5676-95bb-b37da7bc3c99" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224991 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{127\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $127$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 6 = 12\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n127 \\div 12 = 10 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n127 = 10 \\cdot 12 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{127π}{6} &= ((10 \\cdot 12 + 7)π)/(6) \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot \\frac{12π}{6} + \\frac{7π}{6} \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{127\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{127\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224992 1069 "e-21175" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224992 1071 "Find the value of $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{159\\pi}{4}\\right)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224992 1072 #uuid "3bb29211-aaa2-5e11-87df-8adfd4bf0c77" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224992 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{159\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $159$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n159 \\div 8 = 19 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n159 = 19 \\cdot 8 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{159π}{4} &= ((19 \\cdot 8 + 7)π)/(4) \\\\\n&= 19 \\cdot \\frac{8π}{4} + \\frac{7π}{4} \\\\\n&= 19 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{159\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{159\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224993 1069 "e-21176" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224993 1071 "Find the value of $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\\right)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224993 1072 #uuid "a86679dc-fb8b-5292-9add-667989f05693" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224993 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $215$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n215 \\div 8 = 26 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n215 = 26 \\cdot 8 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{215π}{4} &= ((26 \\cdot 8 + 7)π)/(4) \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot \\frac{8π}{4} + \\frac{7π}{4} \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} = -1\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224994 1069 "e-21178" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224994 1071 "![](images/20/20a439a916d40f1fa8a21060c3ab32b8b0844bdbd4d82fc6bd0310b9dc2008ec.png)\n\nA tile path in a park features three congruent equilateral tiles arranged in a straight line. One of the tiles is shaded. If the total perimeter of the tile arrangement is $20$ centimeters, what is the area of the shaded region, in square centimeters?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224994 1072 #uuid "15f02359-0e18-5c69-90b5-356f6574fe9d" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224994 1074 "The perimeter of the row formed by the three congruent equilateral triangles consists of five outer triangle sides. If $s$ is the side length of each small triangle, then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPerimeter &= 20 \\\\\n5s &= 20 \\\\\ns &= \\frac{20}{5} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the area of the shaded equilateral triangle using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}s^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot 4^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot 16 \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the shaded region is $4\\sqrt{3}$ square centimeters." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224995 1069 "e-21196" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224995 1071 "Which of the following **could** be a solution to the equation\n$(x-5)(x-k+4) = 0$\nif $k > 2$ is a constant?\n\n1. $x=5$\n2. $x=3$\n3. $x=-5$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224995 1072 #uuid "818206c4-1b47-5ac6-a727-b9ca700b4b88" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224995 1074 "We are given the factored equation\n\n$$\n(x-5)(x-k+4) = 0\n$$\n\nAccording to the zero product property, a product equals zero only if one of its factors equals zero. So we set each factor equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx - 5 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x = 5\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx - k + 4 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x = k - 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the potential solutions are $x = 5$ or $x = k - 4$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- Option I: $x = 5$ is indeed a solution.$\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Option II: $x = 3$ would require $k - 4 = 3$, which gives $k = 7$. Since $k > 2$, this is possible, so $3$ can be a solution.$\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Option III: $x = -5$ would require $k - 4 = -5$, which gives $k = -1$. But this contradicts the condition $k > 2$. So $-5$ is not a solution.$\\:{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224996 1069 "e-21197" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224996 1071 "Which of the following **must** be a solution to the equation\n$(x - p + 3)(x - 15) - 6(x - 15) = 0$\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\n1. $x = 15$\n2. $x = p+4$\n3. $x = p+3$\n4. $x = p+6$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224996 1072 #uuid "62c024c8-b663-5a01-b1a1-65ea97435616" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224996 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n(x - p + 3)(x - 15) - 6(x - 15) = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first factor out $(x - 15)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x - 15)\\big[(x - p + 3) - 6\\big] = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n(x - 15)(x - p - 3) = 0\n$$\n\nAccording to the zero product property, a product equals zero only if one of its factors equals zero. So we set each factor equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx - 15 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x = 15\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx - p - 3 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x = p + 3\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- Option I: $x = 15$ is indeed a solution.$\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Option II: $x = p+4$ is **not** a solution.$\\:{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- Option III: $x = p+3$ is indeed a solution.$\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Option IV: $x = p+6$ is **not** a solution.$\\:{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224997 1069 "e-21198" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224997 1071 "Which of the following **must** be a solution to the equation\n$(x - y + 9)(x - 16) = 11(x - 16)$\nwhere $y$ is a constant?\n\n1. $x = 16$\n2. $x = y-3$\n3. $x = y+2$\n4. $x = y+7$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224997 1072 #uuid "f132b437-3dbd-5308-a7b6-82ab08341def" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224997 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n(x - y + 9)(x - 16) = 11(x - 16)\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first bring everything over to one side by subtracting $11(x-16)$ from both sides, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x - y + 9)(x - 16) - 11(x - 16) = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we factor out $(x - 16){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 16)[(x - y + 9) - 11] &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 16)(x - y - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the zero product property, a product equals zero only if one of its factors equals zero. So we set each factor equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx - 16 = 0⇒x = 16\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx - y - 2 = 0⇒x = y + 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- Option I: $x = 16$ is indeed a solution.$\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Option II: $x = y-3$ is **not** a solution.$\\:{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- Option III: $x = y+2$ is indeed a solution.$\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Option IV: $x = y+7$ is **not** a solution.$\\:{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224998 1069 "e-21199" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224998 1071 "Which of the following **must** be a solution to the equation\n$(x - y + 8)(x - 11) + 5(11 - x) = 0$\nwhere $y$ is a constant?\n\n1. $x = 11$\n2. $x = y+1$\n3. $x = y-1$\n4. $x = y-3$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224998 1072 #uuid "c7f1e273-64eb-5858-be5e-fc117911413a" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224998 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n(x - y + 8)(x - 11) + 5(11 - x) = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that the last term can be rewritten by factoring out $-1{:}$\n\n$$\n5(11 - x) = -5(x - 11)\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n(x - y + 8)(x - 11) - 5(x - 11) = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we factor out $(x - 11)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 11)[(x - y + 8) - 5] &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 11)(x - y + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the zero product property, a product equals zero only if one of its factors equals zero. So we set each factor equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx - 11 = 0⇒x = 11\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx - y + 3 = 0⇒x = y - 3\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- Option I: $x = 11$ is indeed a solution.$\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Option II: $x = y+1$ is **not** a solution.$\\:{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- Option III: $x = y-1$ is **not** a solution.$\\:{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- Option IV: $x = y-3$ is indeed a solution.$\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224999 1069 "e-21200" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224999 1071 "If $k$ is a solution of the equation $t^2 - t - 30 = 0$ and $k > 0$, what is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224999 1072 #uuid "f42d410f-9ada-5b30-9ecb-cbcdd6c114b7" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186224999 1074 "To factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-30$ and add to $-1$. These two numbers are $-6$ and $5$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - t - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2}- 6t + 5t_(⏟)_(-t) - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt(t - 6) + 5(t - 6) &= 0 \\\\\n(t - 6)(t + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}t - 6 = 0 & ⇒t = 6 \\\\ t + 5 = 0 & ⇒t =-5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that $k$ is a solution to our equation, with $k \\gt 0$. Therefore, we conclude that $k = 6$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225000 1069 "e-21201" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225000 1071 "What is the positive solution to the equation $-p^2 - 3p + 4 = 0$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225000 1072 #uuid "e2d6473e-9c36-5c92-b758-a1dae8cf98dd" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225000 1074 "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-p^{2} - 3p + 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-4$ and add to $3$. These two numbers are $4$ and $-1$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2}+ 4p - p_(⏟)_(3p) - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np(p + 4) - 1(p + 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(p + 4)(p - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}p + 4 = 0 & ⇒p =-4 \\\\ p - 1 = 0 & ⇒p = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **positive** solution to the given equation is $p = 1$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225001 1069 "e-21202" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225001 1071 "What is the positive solution to the equation $4y^2 - 17y - 15 = 0$?\n\nExplanation:\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to $4 \\cdot (-15) = -60$ and add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$4y^{2} - 17y - 15|= 0; 4y^{2}- 20y + 3y_(⏟)_(-17y) - 15|= 0; 4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5)|= 0; (y - 5)(4y + 3)|= 0$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225001 1072 #uuid "b8f02563-5129-59ad-9d71-ad375e3c5413" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225001 1074 "To factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n4 \\cdot (-15) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4y^{2} - 17y - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4y^{2}- 20y + 3y_(⏟)_(-17y) - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 5)(4y + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225002 1069 "e-21204" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225002 1071 "One solution to the equation\n$x^2 - 6x + 2 = 0$\ncan be written in the form $3 - \\sqrt{k}$, where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225002 1072 #uuid "5c37b762-7e57-5e4d-9771-4c861b6eb69e" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225002 1074 "The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0,\\qquad a \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFor the given equation, we have $a=1$, $b=-6$, and $c=2$. Substituting the values ​​for $a$, $b$, and $c$ into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-6) ± \\sqrt{(-6)^{2} - 4(1)(2)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 ± \\sqrt{28}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{28} &= \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 7} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can further simplify the solution to our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{6 ± \\sqrt{28}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 ± 2\\sqrt{7}}{2} \\\\\n&= 3 ± \\sqrt{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the smaller solution with the given expression $3 - \\sqrt{k}$, we have\n\n$$\n3 - \\sqrt{\\color{blue}7} = 3 - \\sqrt{\\color{blue}k}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}k} = {\\color{blue}7}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225003 1069 "e-21205" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225003 1071 "The expression $ax^{2} + bx + c$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants, can be rewritten as $(4x+2)(x+5)$. What are the values of the constants?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225003 1072 #uuid "618f003d-ca1c-5d87-839d-5ad0cf476629" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225003 1074 "First, expand the factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4x + 2)(x + 5) &= 4x(x + 5) + 2(x + 5) \\\\\n&= 4x^{2} + 20x + 2x + 10 \\\\\n&= 4x^{2} + 22x + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing this with the general quadratic $ax^{2} + bx + c$, we identify the following:\n\n- The leading coefficient gives $a = 4$.\n- The middle coefficient gives $b = 22$.\n- The constant term gives $c = 10$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225004 1069 "e-21206" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225004 1071 "The function $h$ is defined by $h(x) = a x^{2} + b x + c$, where $a, b$, and $c$ are constants. The graph of $y = h(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the points $(2, 0)$ and $(-3, 0)$. If $a$ is an integer greater than $1$, which of the following could be the value of $a + b$?\n\n1. $2$\n2. $10$\n3. $15$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225004 1072 #uuid "f6bf5aa9-4347-549e-a7f7-5def7f1a1ea1" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225004 1074 "Since the graph passes through $(2, 0)$ and $(-3, 0)$, which are the $x$-intercepts (roots) where $y = 0$, the quadratic factored form of the function $h$ is\n\n$$\nh(x) = a(x - 2)(x + 3)\n$$\n\nNext, we expand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(x) &= a(x^{2} + x - 6) \\\\\n&= ax^{2} + ax - 6a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, comparing the coefficient of $x$ in the above expression with the given form\n\n$$\nh(x) = a x^2 + b x + c\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nb = a\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\na + b = a + a = 2a\n$$\n\nNow, we're told that $a$ is *an integer greater than $1$.* This means that the possible values of $2a$ are all the even integers greater than $2{:}$\n\n$$\n4, 6, 8, 10,\\ldots\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option:\n\n- $2$ is NOT an even integer greater than $2.\\:\\:{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $10$ is an even integer greater than $2.\\:\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $15$ is NOT an even integer greater than $2.\\:\\:{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225005 1069 "e-21207" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225005 1071 "The expression $6x^{2} + bx - 35$, where $b$ is a constant, can be rewritten as $(px + q)(x + r)$, where $p, q$, and $r$ are integer constants. Which of the following must be an integer?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{35}{q}$\n2. $\\dfrac{35}{p}$\n3. $\\dfrac{b}{p}$\n4. $\\dfrac{b}{q}$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225005 1072 #uuid "184f11d2-f6be-5564-86c8-d93dc2270aac" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225005 1074 "First, we expand the factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(px + q)(x + r) &= px(x + r) + q(x + r) \\\\\n&= px^{2} + prx + qx + qr \\\\\n&= px^{2} + (pr + q)x + qr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing this with the given quadratic, $6x^{2} + bx - 35$, we have the following:\n\n- The leading coefficient gives $p = 6$.\n- The middle coefficient gives $pr + q = b$.\n- The constant term gives $qr = -35$.\n\nNext, since $q$ and $r$ are integers and $qr = -35$, it follows that $q$ and $r$ must be factors of $-35$.\nWith that in mind, let's check the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I is valid. Indeed, $\\dfrac{35}{q}$ is an integer since $q$ must be a factor of $-35$.\n- Option II is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{35}{p} = \\dfrac{35}{6}$,\nwhich is not an integer.\n- Option III is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{p} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{p} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{6} = r + \\dfrac{q}{6}$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $q = 1$.\n- Option IV is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{q} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r}{q} + 1$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $r = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225006 1069 "e-21208" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225006 1071 "Consider the equation\n$m^2 - km + 18 = 0$.\nIn the equation above, $k$ is a constant with $k > 0$. If the equation has two integer solutions, what is the smallest possible value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225006 1072 #uuid "2179cafa-21b1-57b5-9193-4c5561e53f88" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225006 1074 "Let $p$ and $q$ be the integer roots of the equation. Then, the equation can be factored as\n\n$$\n(m-p)(m-q) = 0\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\nm^{2} - qm - pm + pq = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nm^2-(p+q)m + pq = 0\n$$\n\nSince this is equivalent to\n\n$$\nm^2 - km + 18 = 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nm^2-(p+q)m + pq = m^2 - km + 18\n$$\n\nWe now compare the coefficients on both sides.\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n$pq = 18$.\nSince the right-hand side is positive, $p$ and $q$ must have the same sign. And since $p$ and $q$ are both integers, they must be factors of $18$.\n- Comparing the coefficients of $m$, we have\n$p + q = k$\nNow, since $k > 0$ and $p$ and $q$ have the same sign, this means that $p$ and $q$ are both positive.\n\nTherefore, listing the positive factor pairs of $18$, we get\n$(1,18), \\qquad (2,9), \\qquad (3,6)$.\n\nWe now evaluate all possible values of $k{:}$\n\n- For the pair $(1,18)$, we have $k = 1 + 18 = 19$.\n- For the pair $(2,9)$, we have $k = 2 + 9 = 11$.\n- For the pair $(3,6)$, we have $k = 3 + 6 = 9$.\n\nTherefore, the **smallest** positive value of $k$ is $9$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225007 1069 "e-21209" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225007 1071 "In the equation\n$81x^{2} - (27p+9q)x + pq = 0$,\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are positive constants, the sum of the solutions is equal to $k(3p + q)$, where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225007 1072 #uuid "2f94ae24-968d-5ac0-b116-31da1344a523" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225007 1074 "The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0,\\qquad a \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFor the given equation, we have $a=81$,\n\n$$\nb=-(27p+9q)\n$$\n\nand $c=pq$.\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{b}{a}\n$$\n\ngives the value of the sum of the solutions to the quadratic equation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{b}{a} &= - (- (27p + 9q))/(81) \\\\\n&= \\frac{27p + 9q}{81} \\\\\n&= (9(3p + q))/(81) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3p + q}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}(3p + q)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the last value with the given expression $k(3p + q)$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{9}}(3p+q) = {\\color{blue}k} (3p+q)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}k} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{9}}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225008 1069 "e-21210" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225008 1071 "Given that $kx^2 + 14x + k = 0$, where $k$ is a **positive** constant, has exactly one real solution, what is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225008 1072 #uuid "fc71ce09-00d3-5933-9533-46e1b6b92287" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225008 1074 "A quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = k, \\qquad b = 14, \\qquad c = k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (14)^{2} - 4(k)(k) \\\\\n&= 196 - 4k^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n196 - 4k^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n196 &= 4k^{2} \\\\\nk^{2} &= 49 \\\\\n\\mid k \\mid &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be positive, we conclude that $k=7$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225009 1069 "e-21213" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225009 1071 "Given that $-3kx^2 - 2kx - 5 = 0$, where $k$ is a **positive** constant, has exactly one real solution, what is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225009 1072 #uuid "61528920-5229-59cd-aafa-48c0a745668c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225009 1074 "A quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -3k, \\qquad b = -2k, \\qquad c = -5\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-2k)^{2} - 4(-3k)(-5) \\\\\n&= 4k^{2} - 60k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4k^{2} - 60k &= 0 \\\\\n4k(k - 15) &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 0, 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be positive, we conclude that $k=15$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225010 1069 "e-21215" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225010 1071 "Given that $2x^2 + qx + 50 = 0$, where $q$ is an **integer** constant, has no real solution, what is the least possible value of $q$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225010 1072 #uuid "186c7eaf-22ca-56c7-8512-49c85c57cf67" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225010 1074 "A quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 2, \\qquad b = q, \\qquad c = 50\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (q)^{2} - 4(2)(50) \\\\\n&= q^{2} - 400\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\nq^{2} - 400\\begin{vmatrix}< 0 \\\\ q^{2}\\end{vmatrix}< 400; \\sqrt{q^{2}} \\mid < \\sqrt{400}; \\mid q \\mid \\mid < 20; - 20 < q \\mid < 20\n$$\n\nSince $q$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $-19$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225011 1069 "e-21216" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225011 1071 "Given that $x(5x - k) = -10$, where $k$ is a **positive** constant, has real solutions, what is the least possible value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225011 1072 #uuid "84246139-995d-57ba-8c2f-38ae2e4eb6e0" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225011 1074 "A quadratic equation has real solutions if the discriminant is non-negative:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(5x - k) &= -10 \\\\\n5x^{2} - kx &= -10 \\\\\n5x^{2} - kx + 10 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 5, \\qquad b = -k, \\qquad c = 10\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-k)^{2} - 4(5)(10) \\\\\n&= k^{2} - 200\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have real solutions, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k^{2} - 200 & \\ge 0 \\\\ k^{2} & \\ge 200 \\\\ k & \\ge \\sqrt{200}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be positive, we conclude that the least possible value is $10\\sqrt{2}$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225012 1069 "e-21220" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225012 1071 "Given that $x(px - 24) = -9$, where $p$ is an **integer** constant, has no real solutions, what is the least possible value of $p$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225012 1072 #uuid "627ada13-71a5-5c98-81b7-0f8c6edea94e" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225012 1074 "A quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(px - 24) &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x + 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = p, \\qquad b = -24, \\qquad c = 9\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-24)^{2} - 4(p)(9) \\\\\n&= 576 - 36p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}576 - 36p & < 0 \\\\ -36p & < - 576 \\\\ p & > \\frac{-576}{-36} \\\\ p & > 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $p$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $17$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225013 1069 "e-21221" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225013 1071 "$3y^{2} + 6y = k$\nGiven that in the equation above, $k$ is a constant and the equation has no real solutions, which of the following could be the value of $k$?\n\n1. $-2$\n2. $0$\n3. $-4$\n4. $3$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225013 1072 #uuid "637531b2-697d-5b69-a5e6-1c66ac5e7c1c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225013 1074 "For a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nay^2 + by + c = 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, there are two distinct real solutions.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, there is one real solution.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, there are no real solutions (the solutions are complex).\n\nFirst, rewrite the given equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n3y^{2} + 6y - k = 0\n$$\n\nWe have $a = 3$, $b = 6$, and $c = -k$.\n\nComputing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4(3)(-k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we require $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}36 + 12k & < 0 \\\\ 12k & < - 36 \\\\ k & < - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, any $k$ less than $-3$ would work.\nNotice that among the given options, only $-4$ is less than $-3$.\n\nTherefore, $k = -4$ is the correct answer." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225014 1069 "e-21223" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225014 1071 "If $ax^2+3x-1=0$ has exactly one solution, what is the value of $a$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225014 1072 #uuid "88add6fb-9c08-52b6-8463-ed900373f6f8" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225014 1074 "To solve this problem, let us apply the quadratic formula. The known coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\nb = 3,\\qquad c = -1\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (-3 ± \\sqrt{(-3)^{2} - 4(a)(-1)})/((2)(a)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-3 ± \\sqrt{9 + 4a}}{2a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in order for there to be exactly one solution, the discriminant must be zero. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= 9 + 4a \\\\\n4a &= -9 \\\\\na &= -\\frac{9}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225015 1069 "e-21225" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225015 1071 "Given that $x(2x + 12) = -k$, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, has two distinct real solutions, what is the greatest possible value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225015 1072 #uuid "f60db9c3-59ad-506d-9d12-02266ec8ab1c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225015 1074 "A quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(2x + 12) &= -k \\\\\n2x^{2} + 12x &= -k \\\\\n2x^{2} + 12x + k &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 2, \\qquad b = 12, \\qquad c = k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (12)^{2} - 4(2)(k) \\\\\n&= 144 - 8k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}144 - 8k & > 0 \\\\ 144 & > 8k \\\\ 8k & < 144 \\\\ k & < \\frac{144}{8} \\\\ k & < 18\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $17$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225016 1069 "e-21226" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225016 1071 "$4t^{2} + kt + 36 = 0$\n\nGiven that in the above equation, $k$ is a constant and the equation has more than one real solution, which of the following could be the value of $k$?\n\n1. $0$\n2. $-26$\n3. $10$\n4. $-22$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225016 1072 #uuid "0e90e713-28db-56d3-83bb-6aed8d724b2c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225016 1074 "For a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nat^2 + bt + c = 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, there are two distinct real solutions.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, there is exactly one real solution.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, there are no real solutions (the solutions are complex).\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = k, \\qquad c = 36\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= k^{2} - 4(4)(36) \\\\\n&= k^{2} - 576\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have more than one real solution, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nk^{2} - 576\\begin{vmatrix}> 0 \\\\ k^{2}\\end{vmatrix}> 576; \\sqrt{k^{2}} \\mid > \\sqrt{576}; \\mid k \\mid \\mid > 24\n$$\n\nNotice that among the given options, only $-26$ satisfies this condition.\n\nTherefore, $k = -26$ is the correct answer." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225017 1069 "e-21231" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225017 1071 "What are the solutions to $\\sqrt{x-x^2}=\\sqrt{-3x-5}$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225017 1072 #uuid "32863885-5d2e-5adf-bca7-9e62fbd25e37" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225017 1074 "To solve for $x$, we first square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x - x^{2}})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{-3x - 5})^{2} \\\\\nx - x^{2} &= -3x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move all terms to one side and factor the result to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - 5 &= x - x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=-1$ and $x=5$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=-1$, we have\n$\\sqrt{-1 - (-1)^{2}}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-3(-1) - 5}; \\sqrt{-1 - 1}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{3 - 5}; \\sqrt{-2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-2}, \\cdot$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n- For $x=5$, we have\n$\\sqrt{5 - 5^{2}}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-3(5) - 5}; \\sqrt{5 - 25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-15 - 5}; \\sqrt{-20}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-20}, \\cdot$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=5$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-x^2}=\\sqrt{-3x-5}\n$$\n\nhas no solutions." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225018 1069 "e-21232" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225018 1071 "The equation below relates the distinct positive real numbers $a$, $b$, and $z{:}$\n$\\dfrac{36a}{6b} = 12\\sqrt{z + 10}$.\nExpress $z$ in terms of $a$ and $b$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225018 1072 #uuid "20b8be63-65cc-5636-a28a-f713f5289468" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225018 1074 "First, notice that we can simplify the fraction left-hand side since $36$ and $6$ have a common factor of $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36a}{6b} = \\dfrac{6a}{b}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6a}{b} = 12\\sqrt{z + 10}\n$$\n\nTo express our equation in terms of $z$, we first isolate the square root by dividing both sides by $12{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6a}{b \\cdot 12} &= \\frac{12\\sqrt{z + 10}}{12} \\\\\n\\frac{6a}{b \\cdot 12} &= \\frac{12\\sqrt{z + 10}}{12} \\\\\n\\frac{a}{2b} &= \\sqrt{z + 10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we square both sides:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{a}{2b}\\right)^2 = z + 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{a}{2b})^{2} &= z + 10 \\\\\n(\\frac{a}{2b})^{2} - 10 &= z + 10 - 10 \\\\\n(\\frac{a}{2b})^{2} - 10 &= z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nz = \\left(\\dfrac{a}{2b}\\right)^2 - 10\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225019 1069 "e-21233" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225019 1071 "The equation\n$n = 2(b - 5)^2$\nrelates the positive numbers $n$ and $b$. Express $b$ in terms of $n$, where $b > 5$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225019 1072 #uuid "2f82be79-841e-5007-9c94-e4003d0f1af3" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225019 1074 "First, we isolate $(b - 5)^2$ by dividing both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 2(b - 5)^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{n}{2} &= (2(b - 5)^{2})/(2) \\\\\n\\frac{n}{2} &= (b - 5)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $(b-5)$ by finding the square root of both sides. Notice that since $b > 5$, we have that $(b-5)>0$. So, we consider only the positive square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb - 5 &= \\sqrt{\\frac{n}{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= \\sqrt{\\frac{n}{2}} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nb = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{n}{2}} + 5\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225020 1069 "e-21234" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225020 1071 "Consider the equation\n$\\sqrt[6]{x^7}=\\sqrt[5]{y^3}$,\nwhere $x$, and $y$ are all positive numbers. Express $x$ in terms of $y$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225020 1072 #uuid "9da69409-d045-5ab9-85c9-87fff6013fcf" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225020 1074 "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nFirst, let us convert our radicals to fractional exponents. Our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx^{7/6}=y^{3/5}\n$$\n\nNow, since we would like to isolate $x$, we take both sides to the power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nand apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{7/6} &= y^{3/5} \\\\\n(x^{7/6})^{6/7} &= (y^{3/5})^{6/7} \\\\\nx^{(7/6) \\cdot (6/7)} &= y^{(3/5) \\cdot (6/7)} \\\\\nx &= y^{18/35}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225021 1069 "e-21236" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225021 1071 "![](images/6c/6cd48db20633372ab6e563d47716cf7c5702ea5b7af73646cb5278d390b70be4.png)\n\nThe volume of the container shown is given by the formula\n$V = \\frac{13\\pi k^3}{6}$.\n\nIf the container holds $500$ cubic centimeters when completely filled, what is the value of $k$ in centimeters? Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225021 1072 #uuid "daa68e0f-f5ea-538f-9748-3fa74e19047d" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225021 1074 "We are given the volume formula\n\n$$\nV = \\frac{13\\pi k^3}{6}\n$$\n\nand we are told that $V = 500$.\n\nNow, we substitute this value into the formula and solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n500 &= \\frac{13πk^{3}}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{500 \\cdot 6}{13π} &= k^{3} \\\\\n\\frac{3000}{13π} &= k^{3} \\\\\nk &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3000}{13π}} \\\\\n&= k \\mid \\approx 6.83\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, the value of $k$ is closest to $6.83$ centimeters." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225022 1069 "e-21238" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225022 1071 "${y = 13; y = \\mid - 3x + 4 \\mid$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations in the $xy$-plane intersect at the point $(x, y)$. What are the possible values of $x$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225022 1072 #uuid "34e6730b-bd93-53bf-814f-517cdbe5ffa9" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225022 1074 "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 13; y = \\mid - 3x + 4 \\mid\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we know that $y = 13$.\n\nSubstituting $y = 13$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\mid -3x + 4 \\mid = 13\n$$\n\nThis absolute value equation has two cases:\n\n- If $-3x + 4 > 0$, we have\n$-3x + 4|= 13; - 3x|= 9; x|=-3$.\n- If $-3x + 4 < 0$, we have\n$-3x + 4|=-13; - 3x|=-17; x|= \\frac{17}{3}$.\n\nTherefore, $x = -3$ and\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{17}{3}\n$$\n\nare the two solutions of the given system of equations." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225023 1069 "e-21239" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225023 1071 "${x = 9; y = 4 \\cdot 2^{x - 2} + 1$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225023 1072 #uuid "56330c5f-6ee4-550e-a239-96ac0dd7a73a" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225023 1074 "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\n{x = 9; y = 4 \\cdot 2^{x - 2} + 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 9$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4 \\cdot 2^{7} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 128 + 1 \\\\\n&= 512 + 1 \\\\\n&= 513\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $y=513$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225024 1069 "e-21240" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225024 1071 "${x = 144; y = \\sqrt{x} - 5$\n\nThe graphs of the equations above intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225024 1072 #uuid "a2fe4a0a-7e55-5b56-a0ab-92c8b88c2116" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225024 1074 "We are given the system of two equations\n\n$$\n{x = 144; y = \\sqrt{x} - 5\n$$\n\nTo find the point where the graphs intersect, we substitute $x = 144$ into the second equation. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{x} - 5 \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{144} - 5 \\\\\n&= 12 - 5 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $y$ is $7$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225025 1069 "e-21241" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225025 1071 "${y =-2x^{2} + 20x - 60; y = k$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225025 1072 #uuid "4c388659-fb10-57b4-b24d-3065f8bd4996" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225025 1074 "The line $y=k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-2x^2 + 20x - 60\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-2x^2 + 20x - 60 = k\n$$\n\nWe need to determine the value of $k$ such that this equation has one real solution.\n\nA quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x^{2} + 20x - 60 &= k \\\\\n- 2x^{2} + 20x + (-60 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -2, \\qquad b = 20, \\qquad c = -60 - k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (20)^{2} - 4(-2)(-60 - k) \\\\\n&= 400 + 8(-60 - k) \\\\\n&= 400 - 480 - 8k \\\\\n&=-80 - 8k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-80 - 8k &= 0 \\\\\n- 8k &= 80 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{80}{8} \\\\\nk &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is $y=-10$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225026 1069 "e-21242" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225026 1071 "${y =-5x^{2} + 10x - 1; y = 4x - k$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225026 1072 #uuid "479869ce-10a1-5b7f-905f-8c2d8b219a76" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225026 1074 "The line $y=4x - k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-5x^2 + 10x - 1\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-5x^2 + 10x - 1 = 4x - k\n$$\n\nWe need to determine the value of $k$ such that this equation has one real solution.\n\nA quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 10x - 1 &= 4x - k \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 10x - 1 - 4x + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x + (k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -5, \\qquad b = 6, \\qquad c = k-1\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4(-5)(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20k - 20 \\\\\n&= 16 + 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16 + 20k &= 0 \\\\\n20k &= -16 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{16}{20} \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=4x-\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)=4x+\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinate, we solve the original quadratic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 6x + (-\\frac{4}{5} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x - \\frac{9}{5} &= 0 \\\\\n- 25x^{2} + 30x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- (25x^{2} - 30x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n- (5x - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n5x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the $y$-coordinate by substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\ninto one of the two equations and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4(\\frac{3}{5}) + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\dfrac{3}{5},\\dfrac{16}{5}\\Big)\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225027 1069 "e-21244" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225027 1071 "For the exponential function $f$, the value of $f(1)$ is $k$, where $k$ is a constant. Which of the following equivalent forms of the function $f$ shows the value of $k$ as the coefficient or the base?\n\n1. $f(x)=15\\cdot 2^{x+1}$\n2. $f(x)=30\\cdot 2^{x}$\n3. $f(x)=120\\cdot 2^{x-2}$\n4. $f(x)=60\\cdot 2^{x-1}$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225027 1072 #uuid "301fc44b-c8db-5239-ac88-3889f7662ed1" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225027 1074 "The value $f(1)$ is found by evaluating the function at $x=1$. Using the convenient equivalent form\n\n$$\nf(x)=30\\cdot 2^x\n$$\n\ngiven in option II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 30 \\cdot 2^{1} \\\\\n&= 30 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=60$.\n\nOf the given options, only the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=60\\cdot 2^{x-1}\n$$\n\ndisplays this value of $k$ as a coefficient or base.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only\"." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225028 1069 "e-21245" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225028 1071 "The function\n$v(t) = 2,250 \\cdot (1.09)^{(1/6)t}$\nmodels the number of video views $t$ seconds after a clip is uploaded.\nWhat function models the number of video views $m$ minutes after the clip is uploaded?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225028 1072 #uuid "6a028589-31d1-5bf2-83bd-0d08e36d213c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225028 1074 "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nv(t) = 2,250 \\cdot (1.09)^{(1/6)t}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the number of seconds after upload. We want to rewrite this model in terms of minutes rather than seconds.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=60m$ into the original equation.\n- We *multiply* by $60$ (not divide) because we want $m=1$ minute to correspond to $t=60$ seconds.\n\nSubstituting, we get the model\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(m) = v(60m) &= 2, 250 \\cdot (1.09)^{(1/6) \\cdot (60m)} \\\\\n&= 2, 250 \\cdot (1.09)^{10m}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis models the number of video views as a function of time in minutes after the clip is uploaded." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225029 1069 "e-21246" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225029 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = -6\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{x} - 4$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f$ has a minimum value.\n2. $f$ does **NOT** have a maximum value.\n3. The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty,-4)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225029 1072 #uuid "1160a3ec-0339-5efe-ae1e-0c4fbeb3b4bd" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225029 1074 "On the domain $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$, an exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x)=a\\cdot b^x+c\n$$\n\napproaches, but never reaches, a horizontal asymptote on one side, and increases (or decreases) without bound on the other.\n\nLet's start by sketching the graph of $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](images/eb/eba5b4045b6098a51f39cce78d4c03fd408e3cb22a4da3da5011a19921a425d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- Looking at the end-behavior on the left-hand side, we see that $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- Looking at the end-behavior on the right-hand side, we see that $f(x) < -4$.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function decreases without bound as $x\\to -\\infty$, so it does not have a minimum value.\n- Statement II is true. Since $f(x)$ approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=-4$ from below but never reaches $-4$, $f$ does not have a maximum value.\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ can be made arbitrarily negative and approaches $-4$ from below, the range is $(-\\infty,-4)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct statements are “II and III only”." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225030 1069 "e-21247" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225030 1071 "Which of the following functions' equations displays, as a constant term or as a coefficient, the maximum value of the function, each defined on $x\\ge 0$?\n\n1. $f(x)=18\\cdot(1.2)^x$\n2. $g(x)=27\\cdot(0.75)^x$\n3. $h(x)=9\\cdot(0.75)^x+4$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225030 1072 #uuid "0d7e0959-4e48-5ba0-bfe3-9740ddffd8c0" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225030 1074 "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, with its maximum at $x=0$, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, and has no maximum.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=18\\cdot(1.2)^x$,\nthe base is $1.2>1$. So, the function has no maximum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n- For the function $g(x)=27\\cdot(0.75)^x$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$g(0)|= 27 \\cdot (0.75)^{0}; = 27$.\nThus, the maximum value is $27$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $g$ *does* display the maximum as a coefficient.\n- For the function $h(x)=9\\cdot(0.75)^x+4$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$h(0)|= 9 \\cdot (0.75)^{0} + 4; = 9 + 4; = 13$.\nThus, the maximum value is $13$. However, neither the constant term ($4$) nor the coefficient ($9$) equals $13$. Hence, the equation of $h$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225031 1069 "e-21248" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225031 1071 "A function is defined by $f(x) = 6 \\cdot 3^{x} + n$, where $n$ is a constant. The graph of $y = f(x)$ has its $y$-intercept at $(0,15)$. What is the value of $n$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225031 1072 #uuid "c4a8bc8f-ea66-578d-b7b4-74da9302b0dc" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225031 1074 "Since the graph passes through the point $(0,15)$, we have\n\n$$\nf(0) = 15\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following equation, which we solve for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0) &= 15 \\\\\n6 \\cdot 3^{0} + n &= 15 \\\\\n6 \\cdot 1 + n &= 15 \\\\\n6 + n &= 15 \\\\\nn &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n =9$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225032 1069 "e-21249" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225032 1071 "The function\n$k(t) = 650 \\cdot (1.04)^{(2/5)t}$\nmodels the number of messages sent $t$ seconds after a chat stream begins. According to the model, what percentage of messages are predicted to increase by every $1$ minute? Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225032 1072 #uuid "9baac0f3-8e76-5290-b03c-a3b7c90f6ec0" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225032 1074 "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nk(t) = 650 \\cdot (1.04)^{(2/5)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents messages $t$ seconds after the stream begins. We want to rewrite this model in terms of minutes instead of seconds.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=60m$ into the original equation.\n- We *multiply* by $60$ (not divide) because we want $m=1$ minute to correspond to $t=60$ seconds.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $K(m)$ for messages as a function of time in minutes $m$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK(m) = k(60m) &= 650 \\cdot (1.04)^{(2/5) \\cdot (60m)} \\\\\n&= 650 \\cdot (1.04)^{24m}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the percentage increase over $1$ minute. Inspecting the model in terms of minutes $K(m)$, we see that the growth factor over $m$ minutes is\n$(1.04)^{24m}$.\n\nSo, the growth factor over $1$ minute is\n\n$$\n(1.04)^{24} \\approx 2.563\\,304\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an increase of approximately\n\n$$\n2.563\\,304 - 1 = 1.563\\,304 \\approx 156.33\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, messages are predicted to increase by $156.33\\%$ every $1$ minute." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225033 1069 "e-21250" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225033 1071 "Which of the following functions' equations displays, as a constant term or as a coefficient, the **minimum** value of the function, each defined on $x\\ge 1$?\n\n1. $f(x)=8\\cdot(0.7)^x+2$\n2. $g(x)=12\\cdot(1.4)^x-3$\n3. $h(x)=15\\cdot(1.5)^x-7.5$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225033 1072 #uuid "c603d989-2925-5c84-b21d-504a9f29de92" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225033 1074 "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, and has **no** minimum, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, with its **minimum** at $x=1$.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=8\\cdot(0.7)^x+2$,\nthe base is $0.7\\in(0,1)$. So, the function is decreasing on $x\\ge 1$ and has **no** minimum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display a minimum value.\n- For the function\n$g(x)=12\\cdot(1.4)^x-3$,\nthe base is $1.4>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$g(1)|= 12 \\cdot (1.4)^{1} - 3; = 12 \\cdot 1.4 - 3; = 16.8 - 3; = 13.8$.\nThe minimum value is $13.8$, which is **not** equal to the coefficient ($12$) or the constant term $(-3)$. Hence, the equation of $g$ does *not* display the minimum.\n- For the function\n$h(x)=15\\cdot(1.5)^x-7.5$,\nthe base is $1.5>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$h(1)|= 15 \\cdot (1.5)^{1} - 7.5; = 15 \\cdot 1.5 - 7.5; = 22.5 - 7.5; = 15$.\nThus, the minimum value is $15$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $h$ *does* display the minimum as a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225034 1069 "e-21251" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225034 1071 "An exponential function is defined by $f(x) = 15 \\cdot c^{x}$, where $c$ is a positive constant. What is the value of $c$ if $f(2) = 135$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225034 1072 #uuid "8a225593-034f-5fb6-a03b-21bf3a3f5af6" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225034 1074 "We are told that\n\n$$\nf(x) = 15 \\cdot c^{x}\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nf(2) = 135\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 2$ into the function $f$ and solve for $c$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2) &= 15 \\cdot c^{2} \\\\\n135 &= 15c^{2} \\\\\n15c^{2} &= 135 \\\\\n\\frac{15c^{2}}{15} &= \\frac{135}{15} \\\\\nc^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nc &= ± 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, taking into account that $c$ is positive, we get $c = 3$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225035 1069 "e-21252" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225035 1071 "The function\n$P(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}$\nmodels the population of bacteria in a sealed container $t$ weeks after a chemical is introduced. According to the model, what percentage is the population predicted to decrease by every $2$ days? Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225035 1072 #uuid "e47b9965-8c51-5789-8209-cbb12c0e542b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225035 1074 "Explanation:\n\nWe are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nP(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the population $t$ weeks after the chemical is introduced. We want to rewrite this model in terms of days instead of weeks.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $7$ days in a week, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{d}{7}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $7$ (not multiply) because we want $d=7$ days to correspond to $t=1$ week.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $p(d)$ for the population as a function of time in days $d$ after the chemical is introduced:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(d) = P(\\frac{d}{7}) &= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4) \\cdot (d/7)} \\\\\n&= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{3d/28}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the percentage decrease over $2$ days. Inspecting the model in terms of days $p(d)$, we see that the decay factor over $d$ days is\n$(0.95)^{3d/28}$.\n\nSo, the decay factor over $2$ days is\n\n$$\n(0.95)^{3\\cdot 2/28} = (0.95)^{3/14} \\approx 0.989\\,069\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to a decrease of approximately\n\n$$\n1 - 0.989\\,069 = 0.010\\,931 \\approx 1.09\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the population is predicted to decrease by $1.09\\%$ every $2$ days." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225036 1069 "e-21253" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225036 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = -4\\cdot 4^x - 2$ on the domain $x\\geq 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The maximum value of $f$ is $-6$.\n2. $f$ is increasing on its domain.\n3. The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty,-6]$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225036 1072 #uuid "c697d3d7-70b9-5712-b98f-aeb5d40e057e" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225036 1074 "On the domain $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$, an exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x)=a\\cdot b^x+c\n$$\n\napproaches, but never reaches, a horizontal asymptote on one side, and increases (or decreases) without bound on the other.\n\nLet's start by sketching the graph of $y=f(x)$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = -4\\cdot 4^x - 2\n$$\n\nwith the domain restriction\n\n$$\nx\\geq 0\n$$\n\n![](images/37/37109f91408cdf491b4d9b260ad13e9738225bd224fa9589eaff472ff5ba7558.png)\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function attains a maximum at the left endpoint $x=0$, with $f(0)=-4\\cdot 1-2=-6$.\n- Statement II is false. We can see from the graph that $f$ is decreasing on its domain.\n- Statement III is true. Because $f(0)=-6$ is the largest value and $f(x)\\to -\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$, the range is $(-\\infty,-6]$ on it domain.\n\nTherefore, the correct statements are “I and III only”." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225037 1069 "e-21254" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225037 1071 "The function is defined by $f(x) = -a^{x} + d$, where $a$ and $d$ are positive constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, and the product of $a$ and $d$ is $\\dfrac{21}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225037 1072 #uuid "068a7ba4-70c4-5bbb-bd7a-64fe3f036a99" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225037 1074 "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$f(0)|= \\frac{5}{2}; - a^{0} + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d + 1|= \\frac{5}{2} + 1; d|= \\frac{7}{2}$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$ad|= \\frac{21}{2}; a(\\frac{7}{2})|= \\frac{21}{2}; \\frac{7a}{2}|= \\frac{21}{2}; 2 \\cdot \\frac{7a}{2}|= 2 \\cdot \\frac{21}{2}; 7a|= 21; \\frac{7a}{7}|= \\frac{21}{7}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225038 1069 "e-21255" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225038 1071 "The function\n$C(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}$\nmodels the number of data packets processed, in thousands, by a server $t$ minutes after a throughput upgrade. According to the model, in how many seconds is the number of packets expected to increase by $5\\%$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225038 1072 #uuid "2cbdbc3c-9016-53b4-8414-42e451321415" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225038 1074 "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nC(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the number of data packets processed, in thousands, $t$ minutes after the upgrade. We want to rewrite this model in terms of seconds rather than minutes.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{s}{60}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $60$ (not multiply) because we want $s=60$ seconds to correspond to $t=1$ minute.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $c(s)$ for the number of packets as a function of time in seconds $s$ after the upgrade:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc(s) = C(\\frac{s}{60}) &= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2) \\cdot (s/60)} \\\\\n&= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{s/24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe base $1.05$ means that the number of packets grows by $5\\%$ each time the exponent increases by $1$, i.e., once every growth period.\n\nThe exponent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{s}{24}\n$$\n\nshows that for every increase in $s=24$ seconds, the exponent increases by $1$.\n\nTherefore, the number of packets is expected to increase by $5\\%$ every ${24}$ seconds." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225039 1069 "e-21256" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225039 1071 "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = a^{x} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants and $a > 0$. In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $y = g(x)$ has the $y$-intercept at $(0,-1)$ and passes through the point $(3,25)$. What is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225039 1072 #uuid "cb984573-04f9-5a0a-be6c-f842a9a6e70c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225039 1074 "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(0,-1)$, we have $g(0) = -1$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $b{:}$\n$g(0)|=-1; a^{0} + b|=-1; 1 + b|=-1; b|=-2$\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(3,25)$, we have $g(3) = 25$. Then, using the fact that $b=-2$, we can determine the value of $c$, as follows:\n$g(3)|= 25; a^{3} + (-2)|= 25; a^{3} - 2|= 25; a^{3}|= 27; a|= \\sqrt[3]{27}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 3 + (-2) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225040 1069 "e-21257" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225040 1071 "What is the minimum value of $f(x)=5\\cdot 4^x+1$ for $x \\geq 0$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225040 1072 #uuid "e37918f8-d125-5228-9e99-090e002dd7b6" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225040 1074 "Let's start by sketching the graph of $y=f(x)$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 5\\cdot 4^x+1\n$$\n\nwith the domain restriction\n\n$$\nx\\geq 0\n$$\n\n![](images/80/806a654419b43bc70c873107a5baef4b15790a5af97f0e2c66fabb5c3fc98ec7.png)\n\nEvaluating $f$ at $x=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0) &= 5 \\cdot 4^{0} + 1 \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ on the domain\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nis $6$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225041 1069 "e-21258" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225041 1071 "What is the maximum value of $f(x)=-2\\cdot 3^x-5$ for $x \\geq 2$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225041 1072 #uuid "2a7b4d79-8cf8-50df-b499-00c9f2351def" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225041 1074 "Let's start by sketching the graph of $y=f(x)$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2\\cdot 3^x-5\n$$\n\nwith the domain restriction\n\n$$\nx\\geq 2\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c721cfe78559b47c55e42301c1c4cc1ead91be2f46c2ecc1b43c22876754c2b2.png)\n\nOn the domain\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2\n$$\n\n$f$ attains its *maximum* at $x=2$.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at $x=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2) &= -2 \\cdot 3^{2} - 5 \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot 9 - 5 \\\\\n&=-18 - 5 \\\\\n&=-23\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ on the domain\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2\n$$\n\nis $-23$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225042 1069 "e-21259" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225042 1071 "The function $k$ is defined by $k(x) = -2x^{2} + 24x - 60$. What is the maximum value of the given function?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225042 1072 #uuid "2989d643-b8c6-56f1-8dba-334265ce4a77" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225042 1074 "The given equation is a quadratic function of the form:\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwhere $a = -2$, $b = 24$, and $c = -60$.\n\nBecause $a < 0$, the parabola opens downward, so it has a maximum at its vertex, whose $x$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\\n&= - (24)/(2(-2)) \\\\\n&= - (24)/((-4)) \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting this value back, we find the maximum value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk(6) &= -2(6)^{2} + 24(6) - 60 \\\\\n&=-72 + 144 - 60 \\\\\n&= (-72 + 144) - 60 \\\\\n&= 72 - 60 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225043 1069 "e-21260" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225043 1071 "A curve is defined by $y = f(x)$, where $f(x) = -x^2 + x + 2$. If $y = g(x)$ is a horizontal translation of $y = f(x)$ by $2$ units left, what is the function $g(x)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225043 1072 #uuid "3980452c-84b6-50f4-806b-2a188542873f" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225043 1074 "We're given the curve\n\n$$\ny = -x^2 + x + 2\n$$\n\nand we wish to translate this curve by $2$ units left.\n\nFor each point on the curve $y = f(x)$, we must subtract $2$ from each $x$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on the new curve $y = g(x)$. So, the $x$-coordinates of the points on the new curve are given by\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{new}} = x - 2\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\nx = x_{\\textrm{new}} + 2\n$$\n\nTo get the equation of the new curve, we substitute this into the equation of the first curve. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x^{2} + x + 2 \\\\\ny &= - (x_{new} + 2)^{2} + (x_{new} + 2) + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\ny = -(x + 2)^2 + (x + 2) + 2\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= - (x + 2)^{2} + (x + 2) + 2 \\\\\ny &= -x^{2} - 4x - 4 + x + 2 + 2 \\\\\ny &= -x^{2} - 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2 - 3x\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225044 1069 "e-21261" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225044 1071 "A parabola is given by $y=f(x)$, where\n$f(x) = x^2 + 8x + 5$.\nThe function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(x - 5)$. What is the vertex of $y = g(x)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225044 1072 #uuid "1332b2d0-ebb2-5ccb-928f-54db34111d05" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225044 1074 "We start by noting the following:\n\n- Since $g(x) = f(x - 5)$, the graph of $y = g(x)$ is obtained by shifting the graph of $y = f(x)$ by $5$ units to the *right.*\n- Therefore, to obtain the corresponding points on $y = g(x)$, we *add* $5$ to each $x$-coordinate of $y = f(x)$ while keeping the $y$-coordinate unchanged.\n\nNow, recall that the $x$-coordinate of the vertex of a quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nHere, $a = 1$ and $b = 8$, so the $x$-coordinate of the vertex of $y = f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= - (8)/(2(1)) \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex of $y = f(x)$, we substitute $x = -4$ into $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{2} + 8x + 5 \\\\\nf(-4) &= (-4)^{2} + 8(-4) + 5 \\\\\n&= 16 - 32 + 5 \\\\\n&=-11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vertex of $y = f(x)$ is $(-4,-11)$.\n\nTherefore, *adding* $5$ to the $x$-coordinate, the vertex of $y = g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(-4+5, -11) = \\left(1, -11\\right)\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225045 1069 "e-21262" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225045 1071 "A parabola is given by $y = g(x)$, where\n$g(x) = (x-7)(x+5)$.\nSuppose the function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(x-6)$. What is the vertex of $y = f(x)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225045 1072 #uuid "7fcf01a7-ea61-552e-9d9e-bdc2d9121cd5" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225045 1074 "We start by noting the following:\n\n- Since $g(x) = f(x - 6)$, the graph of $y = g(x)$ is obtained by shifting the graph of $y = f(x)$ by $6$ units to the right.\n- Therefore, the graph of $y = f(x)$ is obtained by shifting the graph of $y = g(x)$ by $6$ units to the *left.*\n- So, to obtain the corresponding points on $y = f(x)$, we *subtract* $6$ from each $x$-coordinate of $y = g(x)$ while keeping the $y$-coordinate unchanged.\n\nFrom the equation\n\n$$\ng(x) = (x-7)(x+5)\n$$\n\nwe find that the roots of the parabola are $x_1=7$ and $x_2=-5$.\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex of $y = g(x)$, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= (7 + (-5))/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex of $y = g(x)$, we substitute $x = 1$ into $g(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= (x - 7)(x + 5) \\\\\ng(1) &= (1 - 7)(1 + 5) \\\\\n&= (-6)(6) \\\\\n&=-36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vertex of $y = g(x)$ is $(1,-36)$.\n\nTherefore, *subtracting* $6$ from the $x$-coordinate, the vertex of $y = f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(1-6, -36) = \\left(-5, -36\\right)\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225046 1069 "e-21263" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225046 1071 "A function $f$ is defined by\n$f(x)=(x+5)(x+1)(x-6)$.\nDefine $k$ by $k(x)=f(x+2)$. The graph $y=k(x)$ has $x$-intercepts at $(s,0)$, $(t,0)$, and $(u,0)$, where $s> t > u$ are distinct constants. What is the value of $s+t+u$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225046 1072 #uuid "3aa94167-9c11-597f-83cf-1c376f623dc6" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225046 1074 "We start by noting the following:\n\n- Since $k(x) = f(x + 2)$, the graph of $k(x)$ is obtained by shifting the graph of $f(x)$ by $2$ units to the *left.*\n- Therefore, to obtain the corresponding points on $k(x)$, we *subtract* $2$ from each $x$-coordinate of $f(x)$ while keeping the $y$-coordinate unchanged.\n\nThe $x$-intercepts of $y=k(x)$ occur where $k(x)=0$, i.e., where\n\n$$\nf(x+2)=0\n$$\n\nBy the zero product property, the zeros of $f$ are\n\n$$\nx = -5,\\qquad x = -1, \\qquad x = 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, *subtracting* $2$ from the $x$-coordinates, we get the roots of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= 6 - 2 = 4 \\\\\nt &= -1 - 2 =-3 \\\\\nu &= -5 - 2 =-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the $x$-intercepts are $(4,0)$, $(-3,0)$, and $(-7,0)$, and their $x$-coordinates sum to\n\n$$\ns+t+u=4+(-3)+(-7)=-6\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ns+t+u=-6\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225047 1069 "e-21265" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225047 1071 "Consider quadratic function $g(x) = ax^{2} - 12x + c$, where $a$ and $c$ are constants. The graph of $y = g(x)$ in the $xy$-plane is a parabola that opens upward and has a vertex at the point $(h, k)$, where $h$ and $k$ are constants. If $k < 0$ and $g(3) = g(9)$, which of the following **MUST** be true?\n\n1. $c < 0$\n2. $a \\gt 0$\n3. $c > 0$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225047 1072 #uuid "536ba4b4-0479-5c38-9b54-6618b7660962" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225047 1074 "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36a - 72 + c & < 0 \\\\ 36a + c & < 72\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(1) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(3) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < - 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225048 1069 "e-21266" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225048 1071 "$y = ax^2 + bx + c$,\nIn the $xy$-plane, a parabola has the vertex $(0, 3)$. If the equation of the parabola can be written in the standard form above, where $a, b$, and $c$ are constants, find the sum $a + b + c$ in terms of the parameter $a$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225048 1072 #uuid "93fa1e21-06dc-5270-9af0-fc30f7040766" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225048 1074 "We know the vertex is at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (0, 3)\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - h)^{2} + k \\\\\ny &= a(x - 0)^{2} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - 0)^{2} + 3 \\\\\n&= a(x^{2}) + 3 \\\\\n&= ax^{2} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing it with the given standard quadratic form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= a \\\\\nb &= 0 \\\\\nc &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, adding them up, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b + c &= a + 0 + 3 \\\\\n&= a + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225049 1069 "e-21267" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225049 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, a parabola has vertex $(4, -8)$ and intersects the $x$-axis at two points. If the equation of the parabola is written in the form\n$y = ax^2 + bx + c$,\nwhere $a, b$, and $c$ are constants, which of the following could be the value of $a + b + c$?\n\n1. $-8$\n2. $-1$\n3. $10$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225049 1072 #uuid "ee125552-ecf9-505a-ab14-86004e09d51f" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225049 1074 "We know the vertex is at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4, -8)\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - h)^{2} + k \\\\\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= a(x^{2} - 8x + 16) - 8 \\\\\n&= ax^{2} - 8ax + 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing it with the given standard quadratic form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= a \\\\\nb &= -8a \\\\\nc &= 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, adding them up, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b + c &= a + (-8a) + (16a - 8) \\\\\n&= 9a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at two points and has its vertex at $(4, -8)$ below the $x$-axis, it must open upward, meaning $a > 0$.\n\nNow, let's go through all the options and check which ones give $a > 0{:}$\n\n- Option I, $-8{:}$\n$9a - 8|=-8; 9a|=-8 + 8; 9a|= 0; a|= 0≯0\\cdot$\n- Option II, $-1{:}$\n$9a - 8|=-1; 9a|=-1 + 8; 9a|= 7; a|= \\frac{7}{9} > 0✓$\n- Option III, $10{:}$\n$9a - 8|= 10; 9a|= 10 + 8; 9a|= 18; a|= 2 > 0✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225050 1069 "e-21268" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225050 1071 "The expression $x + 2k$ is a factor of $2x^{2} + 15x + 6k$. Which of the following could be the value of $k$?\n\n1. $k=3$ or $k=5$\n2. $k=0$ only\n3. $k=0$ or $k=3$\n4. $k=5$ only" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225050 1072 #uuid "98d9973e-10ea-5ef0-91b8-83d655133533" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225050 1074 "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then $x=r$ is a root of the polynomial.\n\nNow, since $x + 2k$ is a factor of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)=2x^{2} + 15x + 6k\n$$\n\nwe must have\n$p(-2k) = 0$.\n\nSo, substituting $x=-2k$ into the expression for $p(x)$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(-2k) &= 2(-2k)^{2} + 15(-2k) + 6k \\\\\n&= 8k^{2} - 30k + 6k \\\\\n&= 8k^{2} - 24k \\\\\n&= 8k(k - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the zero product rule, we conclude that $8k(k-3)=0$ if\n\n$$\nk=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k-3=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225051 1069 "e-21269" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225051 1071 "The expression $4x - g$ is a factor of $16x^{2} + 12x - 7g$. Which of the following could be the value of $g$?\n\n1. $g=0$ or $g=4$\n2. $g=4$ only\n3. $g=-1$ or $g=0$\n4. $g=0$ only" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225051 1072 #uuid "bf86e910-6c38-5a48-81a1-34af8ab1d59c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225051 1074 "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\n\nNow, since $4x - g$ is a factor of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)=16x^{2} + 12x - 7g\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{g}{4}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{g}{4}\n$$\n\ninto the expression for $p(x)$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\frac{g}{4}) &= 16(\\frac{g}{4})^{2} + 12(\\frac{g}{4}) - 7g \\\\\n&= g^{2} + 3g - 7g \\\\\n&= g^{2} - 4g \\\\\n&= g(g - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the zero product rule, we conclude that $g(g - 4)=0$ if\n\n$$\ng=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad g-4=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $g=0$ or $g=4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225052 1069 "e-21270" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225052 1071 "Which of the following expressions has a factor of $2x - 5m$, where $m$ is a **negative integer** constant?\n\n1. $4x^{2} + 2x + 18m$\n2. $4x^{2} + 2x + 10m$\n3. $4x^{2} + 2x + 20m$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225052 1072 #uuid "82246586-84f6-5a40-a17e-9144d2ef21f2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225052 1074 "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$p(\\frac{3c}{2})|= 4(\\frac{5m}{2})^{2} + 2(\\frac{5m}{2}) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23)$.\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\frac{3c}{2}) &= 4(\\frac{5m}{2})^{2} + 2(\\frac{5m}{2}) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\frac{3c}{2}) &= 4(\\frac{5m}{2})^{2} + 2(\\frac{5m}{2}) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225053 1069 "e-21278" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225053 1071 "The product of two positive integers is $585$. If the first integer is $6$ greater than three times the second integer, what is the smaller of the two integers?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225053 1072 #uuid "642b74d4-e73b-52c6-970b-57b9ad9beae8" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225053 1074 "Let the smaller integer be $x$. Then the first integer is $3x + 6$.\n\nSince their product is $585$, we set up the equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(3x + 6) &= 585 \\\\\n3x^{2} + 6x &= 585 \\\\\n3x^{2} + 6x - 585 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since this equation is a quadratic of the form\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwe apply the quadratic formula to solve it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (-6 ± \\sqrt{6^{2} - 4(3)(-585)})/(2(3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-6 ± \\sqrt{36 + 7020}}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-6 ± \\sqrt{7056}}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-6 ± 84}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $x$ must be a positive integer, we take the positive root and find that the value of the smaller integer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-6 + 84}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{78}{6} \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225054 1069 "e-21279" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225054 1071 "The product of two positive integers is $351$. If the first integer is $12$ less than three times the second integer, what is the smaller of the two integers?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225054 1072 #uuid "585223f6-447c-502c-8af1-ee5e96c59a9b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225054 1074 "We are told that the product of two positive integers is $351$, and one is $12$ less than three times the other.\n\nLet the smaller integer be $x$. Then the larger integer is $3x - 12$.\n\nSince their product is $351$, we set up the equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(3x - 12) &= 351 \\\\\n3x^{2} - 12x &= 351 \\\\\n3x^{2} - 12x - 351 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x - 117 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since this equation is a quadratic of the form\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwe apply the quadratic formula to solve it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-4) ± \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} - 4(1)(-117)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{16 + 468}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{484}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± 22}{2} \\\\\n&=-9, 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the two solutions are $x = -9$ and $x = 13$.\n\nSince $x$ represents a positive integer, we discard the negative root and conclude that the smaller integer is $13$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225055 1069 "e-21280" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225055 1071 "The current density of a wire is defined as the amount of electric current, in microamperes, that passes perpendicularly through the wire’s cross-sectional surface, divided by the area of that surface, measured in square meters. What is the current density, in microamperes per square meter, of a wire with a cross-sectional area of $0.004$ square meters carrying a current of $12$ microamperes?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225055 1072 #uuid "5b7159ee-8a09-525c-a94b-d26bfce91c73" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225055 1074 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The current density of a wire is defined as the **amount of electric current,** in microamperes, that passes perpendicularly through the wire’s cross-sectional surface, **divided by** the **area of that surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $J$ is the current density, in microamperes per square meter.\n- $I$ is the current, in microamperes\n- $A$ is the cross-sectional area, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that the current density is the current divided by the area:\n\n$$\nJ = \\frac{I}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{microamperes per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} J} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{microamperes}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} I/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $I = 12$ microamperes\n- $A = 0.004$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= \\frac{12microamperes}{0.004square meters} \\\\\n&= 3000microamperes per square meter\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the current density is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3000}$ microamperes per square meter." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225056 1069 "e-21281" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225056 1071 "The magnetic flux density on a surface is defined as the magnetic flux, in webers, passing perpendicularly through the surface, divided by the area of the surface, measured in square meters. If the magnetic flux density on a surface is $10$ webers per square meter and the area of the surface is $7$ square meters, what is the magnetic flux through the surface, in webers?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225056 1072 #uuid "4a8cc7e9-df75-5330-90f6-d697f7ba088c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225056 1074 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux density on a surface is defined as the **magnetic flux**, in webers, passing perpendicularly through the surface, **divided by** the **area of the surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $B$ is the magnetic flux density, in webers per square meter.\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\nB = \\frac{\\Phi}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} B} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{webers}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $B = 10$ webers per square meter\n- $A = 7$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= \\frac{Φ}{A} \\\\\n10 &= \\frac{Φ}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we find\n\n$$\n\\Phi = 10 \\cdot 7 = 70\\,\\,\\textrm{webers}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $70$ webers." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225057 1069 "e-21282" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225057 1071 "The force exerted on a stretched spring is defined as the product of the spring constant, in newtons per meter, and the extension of the spring, in meters. A spring has a natural length of $0.46$ meters. If the spring constant is $120$ newtons per meter and the spring experiences a force of $30$ newtons, what is the stretched length of the spring, in meters?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225057 1072 #uuid "d3d7b7a7-9efd-5ba7-8cc1-b4620dc7c14c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225057 1074 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The force exerted on a stretched spring is defined as the **product** of the **spring constant**, in newtons per meter, and the **extension of the spring**, in meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $F$ is the force exerted on the spring, in newtons.\n- $k$ is the spring constant, in newtons per meter.\n- $\\Delta x$ is the extension of the spring, in meters.\n- $L_0$ is the natural length of the spring, in meters.\n- $L$ is the stretched length of the spring, in meters.\n\nThe extension of the spring equals the change in length of the spring:\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = L - L_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force is related to the extension by\n\n$$\nF = k \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{newtons}}_{\\textrm{units of} F} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{newtons per meter})(\\textrm{meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} k \\cdot \\Delta x}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following information:\n\n- $F = 30$ newtons\n- $k = 120$ newtons per meter\n- $L_0 = 0.46$ meters\n\nRearranging the formula for the extension, we get\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\frac{F}{k}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔx &= \\frac{30}{120} = 0.25m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the stretched length of the spring is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= L_{0} + Δx \\\\\n&= 0.46 + 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0.71m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stretched length of the spring is $0.71$ meters." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225058 1069 "e-21283" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225058 1071 "The magnetic flux of a magnetic field passing perpendicularly through a surface is defined as the product of the magnetic field strength, in webers per square meter, and the area of the surface, measured in square meters. What is the magnetic flux, in webers, through a rectangular surface whose width is four times its length, if the length is $1.5$ meters and the magnetic field strength is $4$ weber per square meter?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225058 1072 #uuid "e78f8ea3-35f8-5eaa-88d2-52847b895201" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225058 1074 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux of a magnetic field passing perpendicularly through a surface is defined as the **product** of the **magnetic field strength**, in webers per square meter, and the **area of the surface,** measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $B$ is the magnetic field strength, in webers per square meter.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n- $\\ell$ is the length of the rectangle, in meters.\n- $w$ is the width of the rectangle, in meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\n\\Phi = B \\cdot A\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{webers per square meter})(\\textrm{square meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} B \\cdot A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nSince the width is four times the length, we have $w = 4\\ell$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\ell \\cdot w = \\ell \\cdot (4\\ell) = 4\\ell^2\n$$\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $\\ell = 1.5$ meters\n- $B = 4$ weber per square meter\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = 4 \\cdot (1.5)^2 = 9\\ \\text{square meters}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula for $\\Phi$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ &= 4 \\cdot 9 = 36webers\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $36$ webers." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225059 1069 "e-21285" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225059 1071 "The volume flux of a fluid through a surface perpendicular to its motion is defined as the product of the cross-sectional area of the surface, in square meters, and the velocity of the fluid, in meters per second. If the volume flux of water through a circular cross-section of a pipe is $180$ cubic meters per second and the water moves at a velocity of $15$ meters per second, what is the radius of the cross-section, in meters? Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225059 1072 #uuid "85b83791-d46e-5a6c-a661-fef807b8c9b0" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225059 1074 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The volume flux of a fluid through a surface perpendicular to its motion is defined as the **product** of the **cross-sectional area** of the surface, in square meters, and the **velocity of the fluid,** in meters per second.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $F$ is the volume flux, in cubic meters per second.\n- $A$ is the cross-sectional area, in square meters.\n- $v$ is the velocity of the fluid, in meters per second.\n- $r$ is the radius of the circular cross-section, in meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\nF = A \\cdot v\n$$\n\nand since the cross-sectional area is a circle, we also have\n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $F = 180$ cubic meters per second\n- $v = 15$ meters per second\n\nFirst, solve for the area by rearranging\n\n$$\nF = A \\cdot v\n$$\n\n:\n\n$$\nA = \\frac{F}{v} = \\frac{180}{15} = 12\n$$\n\nNext, substitute this into the circle area formula:\n\n$$\n\\pi r^2 = 12\n$$\n\nSolving for $r$, we get\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt{\\frac{12}{\\pi}} \\approx 2.0\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of the cross-section is approximately $2.0$ meters, rounded to one decimal place." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225060 1069 "e-21286" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225060 1071 "A rectangular mirror has an area of $168$ square feet. The length of the mirror is $2$ feet more than $5$ times the width of the mirror. What is the width of the mirror, in feet?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225060 1072 #uuid "08882f99-b1eb-57fc-8426-57dd38fedb05" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225060 1074 "We are told that the area of a rectangular mirror is $168$ square feet, and that the length is $2$ feet more than $5$ times the width. Let the width be $w$ feet. Then the length is $5w + 2$.\n\nWe start by writing the equation for the area and then expanding:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\narea &= 168 \\\\\nw(5w + 2) &= 168 \\\\\n5w^{2} + 2w &= 168 \\\\\n5w^{2} + 2w - 168 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since this equation is a quadratic of the form\n\n$$\naw^2 + bw + c = 0\n$$\n\nwe apply the quadratic formula to solve it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (-2 ± \\sqrt{(2)^{2} - 4(5)(-168)})/(2(5)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 ± \\sqrt{4 + 3360}}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 ± \\sqrt{3364}}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 ± 58}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the width must be positive, we take the positive root and find that the width of the mirror is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw &= \\frac{-2 + 58}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{56}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{28}{5} \\\\\n&= 28 5feet\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225061 1069 "e-21288" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225061 1071 "A rectangular sandbox has an area of $405$ square meters. If the length of the sandbox is $5$ times its width, what is the width of the sandbox, in meters?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225061 1072 #uuid "803b194c-e0e4-55e1-98d5-8928b0765b63" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225061 1074 "We are told the area of a rectangular sandbox and the relationship between its length and width. We'll use this to find the width.\n\nLet the width be $w$ meters. Then the length is $5w$ meters.\n\nSo we set up the area expression and then solve for $w{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\narea &= 405 \\\\\nw \\cdot 5w &= 405 \\\\\n5w^{2} &= 405 \\\\\nw^{2} &= 81 \\\\\nw &= ± \\sqrt{81} \\\\\nw &= ± 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since the width must be positive, we take the positive square root.\n\nTherefore, the width of the sandbox is $9$ meters." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225062 1069 "e-21289" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225062 1071 "The area of a rectangular floor is $736$ square meters. The width of the floor is $9$ meters less than its length. What is the equation that represents this situation?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225062 1072 #uuid "e2be2889-94ca-5ec4-8473-c34e08e2b2b9" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225062 1074 "Let the length be $x$ meters. Then the width is $x-9$ meters.\n\nSince their product is $702$, we set up the equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx \\cdot (x - 9) &= 736 \\\\\nx^{2} - 9x &= 736 \\\\\nx^{2} - 9x - 736 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that represents this situation is\n\n$$\n0 = x^2 - 9x - 736\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225063 1069 "e-21290" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225063 1071 "A builder is constructing a rectangular patio. The width of the patio is to be $6$ meters less than its length. If the area of the patio will be $160$ square meters, what will be the width, in meters, of the patio?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225063 1072 #uuid "7e37467d-308e-57c8-9df8-e2001632c05c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225063 1074 "We are told that the area of a rectangular patio is $160$ square meters, and that the width is $6$ meters less than the length.\n\nLet the length be $l$ meters. Then the width is $l - 6$ meters.\n\nSince area is the product of length and width, we set up the equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\narea &= 160 \\\\\nl \\cdot (l - 6) &= 160 \\\\\nl^{2} - 6l &= 160 \\\\\nl^{2} - 6l - 160 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n(l + 10) (l - 16) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $l = -10$ and $l = 16$. Since the length $l$ must be positive, we discard the negative solution.\n\nTherefore, the width is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwidth &= l - 6 \\\\\n&= 16 - 6 \\\\\n&= 10meters\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225064 1069 "e-21291" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225064 1071 "${y = 4x^{2} - 8x + 7; y = m$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $m$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $m$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225064 1072 #uuid "a505e255-2944-5cb6-b1a6-dd7a5d85bf83" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225064 1074 "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-48 + 16m & > 0 \\\\ 16m & > 48 \\\\ m & > 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225065 1069 "e-21292" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225065 1071 "If the graph of the equation $y=3x^2 - 12x + 8$ intersects the line $y=k-3x$, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane, what is the least possible value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225065 1072 #uuid "fdec9d31-2b4f-54f5-a7ac-ea9b233f5c9c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225065 1074 "The line $y=k-3x$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=3x^2 - 12x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n3x^2 - 12x + 8 = k - 3x\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 &= k - 3x \\\\\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 + 3x - k &= 0 \\\\\n3x^{2} - 9x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b = -9, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-9)^{2} - 4(3)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 81 - 96 + 12k \\\\\n&=-15 + 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-15 + 12k & > 0 \\\\ 12k & > 15 \\\\ k & > \\frac{15}{12} \\\\ k & > 1.25\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $2$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225066 1069 "e-21293" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225066 1071 "${y =-3x^{2} - 6x + 9; y = k$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the smallest possible value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225066 1072 #uuid "178d10c8-b941-5883-a392-36b7d27ddad2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225066 1074 "The line $y=k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = -3x^2 - 6x + 9\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-3x^2 - 6x + 9 = k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x^{2} - 6x + 9 &= k \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + 9 - k &= 0 \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + (9 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -3, \\qquad b = -6, \\qquad c = 9-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-6)^{2} - 4(-3)(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 12(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 108 - 12k \\\\\n&= 144 - 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}144 - 12k & < 0 \\\\ -12k & < - 144 \\\\ 12k & > 144 \\\\ k & > \\frac{144}{12} \\\\ k & > 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the smallest possible value is $13$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225067 1069 "e-21294" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225067 1071 "If the graph of the equation $y=2x^2-4x+8$ doesn't intersect the line $y=x+k$ in the $xy$-plane, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, what is the greatest possible value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225067 1072 #uuid "08523c47-390f-58ff-a954-b3ef5b607a88" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225067 1074 "The line\n\n$$\ny=x+k\n$$\n\nintersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 2x^2 - 4x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n2x^2 - 4x + 8 = x+k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 &= x + k \\\\\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 - x - k &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 5x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 2, \\qquad b = -5, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-5)^{2} - 4(2)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 8(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 64 + 8k \\\\\n&= 8k - 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8k - 39 & < 0 \\\\ 8k & < 39 \\\\ k & < \\frac{39}{8} \\\\ k & < 4.875\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $4$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225068 1069 "e-21295" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225068 1071 "What is the positive solution to the equation $-12p^2 =-17p - 5$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225068 1072 #uuid "d2b01ad8-a6c0-5c46-95b3-d7b8d55f21cc" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225068 1074 "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12p^{2} &= -17p - 5 \\\\\n- 12p^{2} + 17p + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n12 \\cdot (-5) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2}- 20p + 3p_(⏟)_(-17p) - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n4p(3p - 5) + 1(3p - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(4p + 1)(3p - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4p + 1 = 0 & ⇒p =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 3p - 5 = 0 & ⇒p = \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225069 1069 "e-21296" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225069 1071 "${5x - \\frac{7}{11} = \\sqrt{3}; y =-2(\\frac{7}{11} - 5x)^{2}$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225069 1072 #uuid "87769321-5c2b-5036-9d13-5f6ae47acc1c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225069 1074 "From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n5x-\\dfrac{7}{11}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply this equation by $-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{11} - 5x = -\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the expression on the left-hand side features in the second equation.\n\nSo next, we substitute $-\\sqrt{3}$ for\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{11}-5x\\right)\n$$\n\nin the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2(\\frac{7}{11} - 5x)^{2} \\\\\n&=-2(-\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $-6$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225070 1069 "e-21297" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225070 1071 "${2x - 2y =-6; (y - x)(x + y) = 15$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x+y$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225070 1072 #uuid "c6ded42c-7bcf-5444-9f7d-9bdfff048d28" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225070 1074 "First, we write both equations of the given system in factored form:\n\n$$\n{-2(y - x) =-6; (y - x)(x + y) = 15\n$$\n\nNotice that $(y-x)$ is a common factor in both equations.\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $(y-x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(y - x) &= -6 \\\\\ny - x &= \\frac{-6}{-2} \\\\\ny - x &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the result in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - x)(x + y) &= 15 \\\\\n(3) \\cdot (x + y) &= 15 \\\\\nx + y &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\\nx + y &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x+y$ is $5$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225071 1069 "e-21298" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225071 1071 "${4x + 8y = 32; 4y^{2} - x^{2} =-32$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225071 1072 #uuid "392a35c1-6c25-5d26-bbcd-9f401c1040e5" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225071 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n4y^2-x^2 = (2y)^2-x^2\n$$\n\nis a difference of squares, so we apply the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(2y)^2-x^2=(2y+x)(2y-x)\n$$\n\nThus, we write both equations of the given system in factored form:\n\n$$\n{4(2y + x) = 32; (2y + x)(2y - x) =-32\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2y+x)$ is a common factor in both equations.\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $(2y+x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(2y + x) &= 32 \\\\\n2y + x &= \\frac{32}{4} \\\\\n2y + x &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the result in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2y + x)(2y - x) &= -32 \\\\\n8 \\cdot (2y - x) &= -32 \\\\\n2y - x &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we get the following system with linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2y + x = 8; 2y - x =-4\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation,\n\n$$\nx = 2y+4\n$$\n\nwhich we substitute into the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y + (2y + 4) &= 8 \\\\\n4y + 4 &= 8 \\\\\n4y &= 4 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $1$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225072 1069 "e-21301" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225072 1071 "During a charity livestream, the event had already received $\\[math]60$ in donations when it went live. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after the livestream began, the total amount of money donated increased by $140\\%$ of the amount from the previous hour.\n\nWrite equation that best represents this model, where $D(t)$ is the estimated total amount of money, in dollars, donated $t$ hours after the livestream began and $t \\leq 5$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225072 1072 #uuid "247df12b-2d55-50c7-b118-cee3c99ec4a7" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225072 1074 "We are told the initial amount of donations was $\\[math]60$. The total amount donated increases by $140\\%$ of the previous hour’s total each hour.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nD(t) = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=60$ is the initial amount of money received when the livestream began.\n- $r = 1.4$ is the growth rate ($140\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t$ is the time, in hours, after the livestream began.\n\nTherefore, the equation that best represents this model is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(t) &= 60 \\cdot (1 + 1.4)^{t} \\\\\n&= 60 \\cdot (2.4)^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225073 1069 "e-21302" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225073 1071 "A bank account is opened with a balance of $\\[math]5,000$. The balance doubles every $6$ years. How much money will be in the account after $18$ years?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225073 1072 #uuid "78776c99-0b3c-5e5e-b59a-b500204f4ab2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225073 1074 "We're told that a bank account starts with $\\[math]5,000$, and the balance doubles every $6$ years. We want to find out how much money will be in the account after $18$ years.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth, in terms of a doubling period $d$, is\n\n$$\nN(t) = a \\cdot 2^{t/d}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5,000$ is the initial balance.\n- The balance doubles every $6$ years. Therefore, the doubling period is $d=6$.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after the account is opened.\n\nThus, the correct expression for the balance $N(t)$ is\n\n$$\nN(t) = 5,000 \\cdot 2^{t/6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to find the balance after $t=18$ years. Substituting $t=18$ into the equation for $N(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(18) &= 5, 000 \\cdot 2^{18/6} \\\\\n&= 5, 000 \\cdot 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 5, 000 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 40, 000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $\\[/math]40,000$ in the account after $18$ years." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225074 1069 "e-21303" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225074 1071 "A rocket is launched upward and after each stage separation it reaches $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ the height it reached in the previous stage. The rocket initially reaches a height of $64.0 \\, \\text{m}$.\n\nWhat is the maximum height the rocket reaches above the ground between the first and second stage separations?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225074 1072 #uuid "e9064009-9634-5b8d-a398-58a6db00179a" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225074 1074 "We're told that a rocket is launched upward to an initial height of $64.0\\,\\text{m}$. After each stage separation, it reaches\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nthe height it reached on the previous stage.\n\nThis scenario represents exponential decay, where the height is multiplied by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nafter each stage. The general form for this type of relationship can be expressed as\n\n$$\nH(n) = a \\cdot b^n\n$$\n\nwhere $H(n)$ is the height after $n$ stages, $a$ is the initial height, and $b$ is the decay factor.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=64.0$ is the initial height of the rocket.\n- $b = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is the decay factor.\n- $n$ represents the number of stage separations.\n\nSo, the expression for $H(n)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH(n) &= 64.0 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are looking for the height between the first and second stage separations. This corresponds to the height after the first separation. Hence, we need to calculate $H(1)$.\n\nSubstitute $n=1$ into the formula for $H(n)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH(1) &= 64.0 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{1} \\\\\n&= 64.0 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{64.0}{2} \\\\\n&= 32.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum height the rocket reaches above the ground between the first and second stage separations is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{32.0}\\,\\text{m}$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225075 1069 "e-21304" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225075 1071 "In a science experiment, Priya heated a metal rod and then left it to cool in a room that maintained a constant temperature of $65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$. At $3{:}00$ p.m., the rod’s temperature was $200^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$. After $5$ minutes, its temperature had dropped to $170^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$, and after $10$ minutes, it was $146.5^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$. The rod continued to cool over time.\n\nWrite the function that best models the temperature $T(t)$ in degrees Fahrenheit of the rod $t$ minutes after it was removed from the heat." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225075 1072 #uuid "11ec5b75-416b-52b7-a731-8338a3cafaad" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225075 1074 "The temperature $T(t)$ at time $t$ is modeled by the equation\n\n$$\nT(t) = a \\cdot b^t + T_r\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $T_r = 65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$ is the room (ambient) temperature,\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants to be determined, and\n- $t$ is the time, in minutes.\n\nAt $t=0$, the temperature is $T(0) = 200$. Therefore, we can determine the value of $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot b^{0} + 65 &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot 1 &= 135 \\\\\na &= 135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our model becomes\n\n$$\nT(t) = 135 \\cdot b^t + 65\n$$\n\nAt $t=5$, the temperature is $T(5) = 170$. Hence, we can determine the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5) &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} + 65 &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} &= 105 \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{105}{135} \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{7}{9} \\\\\nb &= (\\frac{7}{9})^{1/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function best models the temperature $T(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= 135 \\cdot ((\\frac{7}{9})^{1/5})^{t} + 65 \\\\\n&= 135 \\cdot (\\frac{7}{9})^{t/5} + 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225076 1069 "e-21306" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225076 1071 "The function $f$ is defined by\n$f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 4)(x + 1)$.\nIn the $xy$-plane, the graph of $y = g(x)$ is the result of translating the graph of $y = f(x)$ up $6$ units. What is the value of $g(-1)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225076 1072 #uuid "bb5b7b33-eaa6-5182-86bc-16f7d765f302" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225076 1074 "We are told that the graph of $g(x)$ is the result of translating the graph of $y = f(x)$ up $6$ units. This means\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= f(x) + 6 \\\\\n&= (x - 3)(x + 4)(x + 1) + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $g(-1)$, we substitute $x=-1$ into the expression for $g(x)$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(-1) &= (-1 - 3)(-1 + 4)(-1 + 1) + 6 \\\\\n&= (-4)(3)(0) + 6 \\\\\n&= 0 + 6 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225077 1069 "e-21308" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225077 1071 "![](images/18/1866f13825b56aaa2f4890b28d24f99bab425f5333c3408e581cf9f845a163ac.png)\n\nWrite the equation that could represent the graph shown in the $xy$-plane?\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225077 1072 #uuid "d7013224-2af1-572a-9d5e-5e74255a01fd" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225077 1074 "First, we notice that the function has three distinct roots, $x = -4$, $x = 0$, and $x = 3$.\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = -4$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n- The graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x = 0$. Therefore, this root has even multiplicity.\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = 3$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the equation we need to find has the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a(x + 4)x^n(x - 3)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an even natural number.\n\nOf the given options, the following are similar to the form described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3), \\\\\nf(x) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we can notice that the value of the function at $x=1$ is negative, that is, $f(1) \\lt 0$.\n\n- For $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$f(1)|= \\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); =-\\frac{10}{3}; < 0$.\n- For $f(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$f(1)|=-\\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); = \\frac{10}{3}; ≮0$.\n\nTherefore, we discard\n\n$$\nf(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$\n\nConsequently, the equation that could represent the graph shown is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225078 1069 "e-21310" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225078 1071 "$y=(x-3)(x^2+x+2)$\nIn the $xy$-plane, the graph of the equation above contains the point $(a,b)$. If $1 \\leq a \\leq 3$, which of the following is **NOT** a possible value of $b$?\n\n1. $-1$\n2. $0$\n3. $2$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225078 1072 #uuid "79ca6843-1a80-5e85-9546-bb9f4c88c47c" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225078 1074 "First, notice that the discriminant of the factor $x^2+x+2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = 1^2-4(1)(2) = -7 < 0\n$$\n\nso our cubic cannot be factored any further over the real numbers.\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has an $x$-intercept at $x=3$.\n- $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$y|= (0 - 3)(0^{2} + 0 + 2); = (-3) \\cdot (2); =-6$.\n\nThe corresponding partial graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6efc85f87a2ca97602de742653ccfe08b08cd409a35bca97b5dd3470984f71f0.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n1 \\leq a \\leq 3\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph.\nNotice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225079 1069 "e-21315" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225079 1071 "The functions $c$ and $d$ are defined by the given equations.\n$c(m) = 8 + \\left|2m + m^2\\right|, \\qquad d(z) = \\left|\\dfrac{z - 5}{2z}\\right| + z + 2$\nIf $c(-2) = c$, where $c$ is a constant, what is the value of $d(c)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225079 1072 #uuid "d836e339-78bd-5be0-a007-980cf453f15a" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225079 1074 "We begin by evaluating $c(-2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc(-2) &= 8 + \\mid 2(-2) + (-2)^{2} \\mid \\\\\n&= 8 + \\mid - 4 + 4 \\mid \\\\\n&= 8 + \\mid 0 \\mid \\\\\n&= 8 + 0 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $c = 8$. Now, we evaluate\n\n$$\nd(c) = d(8){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(8) &= \\begin{vmatrix}(8 - 5)/(2(8)) & + 8 + 2 \\\\ = & \\frac{3}{16}\\end{vmatrix}+ 10 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{16} + 10 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 160}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{163}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225080 1069 "e-21316" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225080 1071 "Let $z(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid$. For which values of $k$ does $z(k) = 5k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225080 1072 #uuid "8deefa88-ce6b-5a15-98be-fdac3bb3788b" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225080 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nz(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid\n$$\n\nand asked to find the value of $k$ for which $z(k) = 5k$. Therefore, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 5k\n$$\n\nWe now split into two cases based on the definition of absolute value.\n\n- First, we consider the case where $25 - 5k \\ge 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 25 - 5k$.\nTherefore, our absolute value equation becomes\n$25 - 5k = 5k$.\nSolving the above equation for $k$, we obtain\n$25 - 5k|= 5k; 25|= 10k; k|= \\frac{5}{2}$.\nNow, we check whether this value of $k$ satisfies the condition for the first case:\n$25 - 5(\\frac{5}{2})|= 25 - \\frac{25}{2}; = \\frac{25}{2}; \\ge 0✓$\nSo, $k = \\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n- Now, we consider the case where $25 - 5k < 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = -(25 - 5k)$.\nThis equation becomes:\n$-25 + 5k = 5k \\Rightarrow -25 = 0$,\nwhich is not true. Therefore, no solution arises from this case.\n\nThus, the only value that satisfies the equation $z(k) = 5k$ is\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225081 1069 "e-21320" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225081 1071 "A mining drone is used to monitor tunnel conditions. The function\n$m(t) = -\\dfrac{1}{55}(t - 19)(t + 11)$\ngives the height above the mine floor, in meters, of the drone $t$ minutes after it starts a scan, where $t > 0$. How many minutes after starting the scan did it take for the drone to return to the mine floor?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225081 1072 #uuid "4f5a1387-ed94-56da-a9a6-b68b65bcfb00" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225081 1074 "The drone reaches the mine floor when its height is $m(t) = 0$. So we have\n\n$$\nm(t) = -\\dfrac{1}{55}(t - 19)(t + 11) = 0\n$$\n\nThis product equals $0$ when either factor is $0$. That gives\n\n$$\n(t - 19) = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad t = 19\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\n(t + 11) = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad t = -11\n$$\n\nWe discard $t = -11$, because $t > 0$.\n\nTherefore, the drone reaches the mine floor after\n\n$$\nt = 19 \\text{minutes}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225082 1069 "e-21321" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225082 1071 "The function $g(x) = x^2 - 8x - 20$ is graphed in the $xy$-plane. What are the $x$-intercepts of $g(x)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225082 1072 #uuid "784e71b3-4ece-51ee-84af-a8d6c884939f" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225082 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2 - 8x - 20\n$$\n\n, we set $g(x) = 0$ and factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 8x - 20 = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n(x - 10)(x + 2) = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation is satisfied when either factor is zero:\n\n$$\nx - 10 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = 10\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{or}\n$$\n\n$$\nx + 2 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = -2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are the points $(10, 0)$ and $(-2, 0)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225083 1069 "e-21322" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225083 1071 "$f(x) = (x + 9)(x + 1)$\n\nIf the given function $f$ is graphed in the $xy$-plane, where $y = f(x)$, what are the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercepts of the graph, in **ascending order**?\n\n$XX$ and $\\quad$\n$XX$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225083 1072 #uuid "9017d84f-2481-5b49-8067-c675e93572b9" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225083 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the graph, we first set\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 9)(x + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is satisfied when either factor is zero:\n\n$$\nx + 9 = 0\\begin{vmatrix}⇒x =-9 \\\\ or \\\\ x + 1 = 0\\end{vmatrix}⇒x =-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the $x$-intercepts of the graph of $y = f(x)$ are:\n\n$$\nx = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-9} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-1}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225084 1069 "e-21323" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225084 1071 "![](images/6c/6c88957e8d275b0fa317625557871dc03bf262ccf700c84a546ecc6c51a64f43.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = 2x^{2} + bx + c$ is shown, where $b$ and $c$ are constants. What is the value of $bc$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225084 1072 #uuid "5d6586f7-f549-5d94-a973-d79118813efb" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225084 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercept, which is the value of $y$ at $x = 0$. From the graph, this is $y = -4$. Substituting into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2x^{2} + bx + c \\\\\n- 4 &= 2(0)^{2} + b(0) + c \\\\\nc &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we know $c = -4$, we substitute one of the $x$-intercept points, say $(1, 0)$, and solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2x^{2} + bx - 4 \\\\\n0 &= 2(1)^{2} + b(1) - 4 \\\\\n0 &= 2 + b - 4 \\\\\n0 &= b - 2 \\\\\nb &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nbc &= (2)(-4) \\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225085 1069 "e-21324" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225085 1071 "![](images/7a/7a9094182720f7eab5dac20e4a84b0641a195d3b124b4889238bc8e6d57666a8.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above shows the function $y = 6\\left(3^{x}\\right)$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225085 1072 #uuid "c158f693-ac1b-5179-b737-626f09ac58d5" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225085 1074 "We are given the exponential function\n\n$$\ny = 6 \\left(3^{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's analyze the properties of this function:\n\n- Since the base of the exponential is $3 > 1$, the function is increasing and $y$ grows rapidly as $x \\to \\infty$.\n- The coefficient $6$ vertically stretches the graph and makes it pass through the point $(0,6)$.\n- As $x \\to -\\infty$, $y \\to 0$, so the graph approaches the horizontal asymptote $y = 0$.\n\nAdditionally, note that from the given equation, we have:\n\n$$\ny(1) = 6 \\cdot 3^1 = 18 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad y(2) = 6 \\cdot 3^2 = 54\n$$\n\nSo, the graph contains the points $(1, 18)$ and $(2, 54)$.\n\nTherefore, the graph that satisfies all of these properties is the following (top-left in the original picture).\n\n![](images/22/222bca310c10c5c3dec5c03186013a0a0cc20b620fefa55f04e38895c8a42baf.png)\n\nPlease note that the following (bottom-right in the original picture) **cannot** be the correct answer, since it clearly does not pass through the point $(2,54)$.\n\n![](images/3f/3fc3359efa5f4e3ad66a66f3777d2a1375ab25f6787e5bda126f6082a5b339ef.png)" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225086 1069 "e-21325" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225086 1071 "$u(x) = (x-5)^{2}$\nThe function $u$ is defined as shown. Which of the following graphs in the $xy$-plane could be the graph of $y = u(x)$?\n\n![](images/ae/aef0396e8be08883090e0c0b4d58d5f776cb0b49f5ce266ea14d4c094dda9ca2.png)" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225086 1072 #uuid "2c7d2df3-f342-57f8-848e-326a70e97dd4" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225086 1074 "Explanation:\n\nThe graph of $y = (x-5)^2$ can be obtained by shifting the graph of the parent function $y = x^2$ to the right by $5$ units.\n\nThe graph of $y = x^2$ can be represented as:\n\n![](images/cb/cbd2da3e8d0b56fe076a94f817a40cf0ad5a4b3f8ae80b293401907bb50cfd79.png)\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y = (x-5)^2$ is\n\n![](images/d1/d1e142759c8b301ddbf7563e495be87308b2a8e5141bedae58d5197d43d7e388.png)" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225087 1069 "e-21327" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225087 1071 "![](images/fd/fd1ebef3a87b9acc74b3c95d28fa797ab33bd861601d57ce3cee1762038260fe.png)\n\nThe graph shown will be translated left $5$ units. Find the resulting graph." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225087 1072 #uuid "ef10ec43-eb0a-5622-ae70-52084d5affdf" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225087 1074 "We are given a graph of a parabola whose vertex is at $(-1, 2)$, and we are told it will be translated left $5$ units.\n\nA horizontal translation to the left subtracts the translation amount from the $x$-coordinates of all the points on the graph.\n\nSo, to translate the graph left $5$ units, we subtract $5$ from the $x$-coordinates of all the points on the graph, including the vertex. This results in the same shape of the parabola but with a new vertex at\n\n$$\n(-1 - 5, 2) = (-6, 2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting graph can be represented as follows:\n\n![](images/69/69cd658d14ee40c8f4ab258302b523bed08ceb7f71320048da76ddeb604c39e3.png)" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225088 1069 "e-21328" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225088 1071 "| $x$ | $t(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-3$ | $5$ |\n| $-1$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $7$ |\n\nThe table above shows selected values of a polynomial function $t(x)$. Based on the values in the table, which of the following must be a factor of $t$?\n\n1. $(x + 1)(x - 2)$\n2. $(x - 1)$\n3. $(x + 2)$\n4. $(x + 3)(x - 2)$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225088 1072 #uuid "990deb04-14ff-569a-8367-175dc58f5369" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225088 1074 "We are told that $t(x)$ is a polynomial function. By the *factor theorem*, if $t(a) = 0$, then $(x - a)$ is a factor of $t(x)$.\n\nFrom the table, we see:\n\n- $t(-1) = 0$, so $(x + 1)$ is a factor\n- $t(2) = 0$, so $(x - 2)$ is a factor\n\nBoth of these factors appear in the product $(x + 1)(x - 2)$, so this product must be a factor of $t(x)$.\n\nNone of the other answer choices are consistent with both of these zeros.\n\nSo, the correct answer is $(x + 1)(x - 2)$." 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225089 1069 "e-21330" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225089 1071 "$s(x) = (x - 8)(x + 2)(x + 5)$\n\nThe function $s$ is given. Which of the following tables of values represents $y = s(x) + 1$?\n\n**I.**\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-5$ | $1$ |\n| $-2$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ |\n\n**II.**\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-5$ | $0$ |\n| $-2$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ |\n\n**III.**\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-5$ | $-1$ |\n| $-2$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ |\n\n**IV.**\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-5$ | $0$ |\n| $-2$ | $0$ |\n| $8$ | $0$ |" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225089 1072 #uuid "0f4f3b1d-805d-5d81-9e10-ab43f6978f38" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225089 1074 "We are given the function\n\n$$\ns(x) = (x - 8)(x + 2)(x + 5)\n$$\n\nand asked to evaluate the transformed expression\n\n$$\ny = s(x) + 1\n$$\n\nfor different values of $x$.\n\nFirst, we note that $s(x)$ has roots at $x=-5, x=-2$, and $x=8$. Therefore, a table of values for $s(x)$ at these points is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $s(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-5$ | $0$ |\n| $-2$ | $0$ |\n| $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\ny = s(x) + 1\n$$\n\nthe correct table for $y$ must be the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-5$ | $1$ |\n| $-2$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ |" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225090 1069 "e-21331" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225090 1071 "Which table gives three values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $p(x)$ at $x=-1,0,1$ for the function $p$ shown below?\n$p(x) = 24(3^x)$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225090 1072 #uuid "545dfbb2-c9a5-50e0-8c79-22f5c16608ae" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225090 1074 "We are given the exponential function\n\n$$\np(x) = 24 \\cdot 3^x\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $p(x)$ at three different $x$-values.\n\n- When $x = -1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(-1) &= 24 \\cdot 3^{-1} \\\\\n&= 24 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When $x = 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(0) &= 24 \\cdot 3^{0} \\\\\n&= 24 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When $x = 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(1) &= 24 \\cdot 3^{1} \\\\\n&= 24 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the table that matches the function is the following:\n\n| $x$ | $p(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $8$ |\n| $0$ | $24$ |\n| $1$ | $72$ |" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225091 1069 "e-21334" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225091 1071 "| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1.80$ |\n| $2$ | $1.91$ |\n| $4$ | $2.03$ |\n\nThe table shows the exponential relationship between the number of days, $x$, since a freediver began breath-hold training, and the average breath-hold time $f(x)$, in minutes.\n\nWhat function represents this relationship, where $x \\leq 4$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225091 1072 #uuid "a24b9ff1-94cd-5225-be3a-7d28a723cc22" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225091 1074 "We are told that $f$ grows exponentially over time, so the relationship between $x$ and $f$ must have the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial amount and $b$ is the growth factor.\n\nFrom the table, we see that the value at time $x = 0$ is $1.80$, so\n\n$$\nf(0)=a = 1.80\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1.80 \\cdot b^{x}\n$$\n\nTo find the growth factor $b$, we use the value at $x = 2{:}$\n\n$$\nf(2)= 1.80\\cdot b^{2} = 1.91\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb^{2} &= \\frac{1.91}{1.80} \\\\\nb^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 1.06111 \\\\ b &= \\sqrt{1.06111} \\\\ b\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 1.03\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the exponential equation is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1.80 \\cdot (1.03)^x\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that this equation works for $x = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(4) &= 1.80 \\cdot (1.03)^{4} \\\\\n&\\approx 2.03\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the value in the table.\n\nSo, the equation is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1.80 \\cdot (1.03)^x\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225092 1069 "e-21380" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225092 1071 "The expression $5a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{9}b^2$ is equivalent to $5(a - cb)(a + cb)$, where $c$ is a positive constant. What is the value of $c$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225092 1072 #uuid "7b2a2b9c-8780-5800-a673-551f45918bc2" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225092 1074 "Since our equivalent expression has a constant factor $5$, we start by factoring $5$ out of our original expression:\n\n$$\n5a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{9}b^2 = 5\\left(a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{45}b^2\\right)\n$$\n\nTo write our bracketed expression as a difference of squares, we must write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{45}\n$$\n\nas a square. Applying the exponent rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{45} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{45}}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nHence, we apply our formula for factoring a difference of squares:\n\n$$\na^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)\n$$\n\nWith $a=a$ and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}\n$$\n\nthis yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(a^{2} - \\frac{4}{45}b^{2}) &= 5(a^{2} - (\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}})^{2}b^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5(a^{2} - (\\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}})^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5(a - \\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}})(a + \\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\\n&= 5(a - \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}b)(a + \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225093 1069 "e-21381" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225093 1071 "The expression\n$\\dfrac{3}{4} z^{2} - 12$\ncan be rewritten in the form\n$\\dfrac{3}{4} (z - k)(z + k)$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225093 1072 #uuid "ab7a5840-b43f-557c-9f03-1c1ec9d3a921" 13194139533380 17592186045585 true] [17592186225093 1074 "We are asked to rewrite\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} z^{2} - 12\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} (z - k)(z + k)\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor out\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nfrom the entire expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} z^{2} - 12 = \\dfrac{3}{4} \\left(z^{2} - 16\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we can factor $z^2 - 16$ as follows:\n\n$$\nz^{2} - 16 = z^{2} - 4^{2} = (z - 4)(z + 4)\n$$\n\nThen, the entire expression can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} (z - 4)(z + 4)\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare the above expression with the given expression of $k$. Therefore, the value of $k$ is $4$." 13194139